WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome to
the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.680
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.430
for everyday life.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.355
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.355 --> 00:00:11.110
I'm Andrew Huberman,
and I'm a professor

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.390
of neurobiology
and ophthalmology

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.070
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.650
Today my guest is
Dr. Sam Harris.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.020
Dr. Sam Harris did his
undergraduate training

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.690
in philosophy at
Stanford University

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:25.030
and then went on to do his
doctorate in neuroscience

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:27.770
at the University of
California at Los Angeles.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:29.470
He is well known
as an author who

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.960
has written about
everything from meditation

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.910
to consciousness, free will.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.070
And he holds many
strong political views

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.560
that he's voiced on social
media and in the content

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.280
of various books as they relate
to philosophy and neuroscience.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.530
During today's
episode, I mainly talk

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.440
to Dr. Harris about his
views and practices related

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.770
to meditation,
consciousness, and free will.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:52.960
In fact, he made
several important points

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.930
about what a proper meditation
practice can accomplish.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:58.720
Prior to this episode, I
thought that meditation

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.810
was about deliberately changing
one's conscious experience

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:04.209
in order to achieve
things such as deeper

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:07.150
relaxation, a heightened
sense of focus or ability

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:10.750
to focus generally,
elevated memory, and so on.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.270
What Sam taught me and what
you'll soon learn as well

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.280
is that while
meditation does indeed

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.530
hold all of those
valuable benefits,

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.810
the main value of a
meditation practice,

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.990
or perhaps the greater value
of a meditation practice,

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.720
is that it doesn't
just allow one

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.700
to change their
conscious experience,

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.190
but it actually can
allow a human being

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.080
to view consciousness itself.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.890
That is to understand what the
process of consciousness is.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.740
And in doing so, to
profoundly shift the way

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.200
that one engages with the
world and with oneself

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.110
in all practices, all
environments, and at all times,

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:47.410
both in sleep and
in waking states.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.300
And in that way
making meditation

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.930
perhaps the most potent
and important portal

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:56.890
by which one can access novel
ways of thinking and being

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:58.790
and viewing one's
life experience.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:02.110
We also discussed the so-called
mind-body problem and issues

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:04.030
of duality and free will.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:06.880
Concepts from philosophy and
neuroscience that, fortunately,

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.110
thanks to valuable experiments
and deep thinking on the part

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.840
of people like Dr.
Sam Harris and others,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.110
is now leading people to
understand really what free

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:18.700
will is and isn't.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.730
Where the locus of free will
likely sits in the brain,

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:24.320
if it indeed resides
in the brain at all.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.160
And what it means to
be a conscious being

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:29.710
and how we can modify our
conscious states in ways that

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.340
allow us to be more functional.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.240
We also discuss perception,
both visual perception auditory

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.030
perception, and especially
interesting to me,

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.840
and I think as well, hopefully
to you, time perception,

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.080
which we know is very
elastic in the brain.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.330
The literal frame
rate by which we

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:49.840
process our conscious experience
can expand and contract

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.750
dramatically depending
on our state of mind

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:55.420
and how conscious we are
about our state of mind.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.950
So we went deep into
that topic as well.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.970
Today's discussion was indeed
an intellectual deep dive

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.740
into all the topics that I
mentioned a few moments ago,

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.070
but it also included
many practical tools.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:08.710
In fact, I pushed
Sam to share with us

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:12.010
what his specific practices
are and how we can all

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.280
arrive at a clearer and better
understanding of a meditation

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.500
practice that we can
each and all apply.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:20.530
So that we can derive these
incredible benefits, not just

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:23.440
the ones related to stress
and focus and enhanced memory,

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.320
but the ones that relate
to our consciousness.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.230
That is to our deeper sense
of self and to others.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:30.850
Several times during
today's episode,

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:32.410
I mentioned the Waing Up app.

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:34.720
The Waking Up app was
developed by Sam Harris,

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.140
but I want to emphasize that my
mention of the app is in no way

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:39.220
a paid promotional.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.050
Rather the Waking Up
app is one that I've

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:44.525
used for some period of time
now and find very, very useful.

00:03:44.525 --> 00:03:46.150
I have family members
that also use it.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.430
Other staff members here
at the Huberman Lab podcast

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.520
use it because we find it
to be such a powerful tool.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.400
Sam has generously offered
Huberman Lab podcast listeners

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.542
a 30-day completely free
trial of the Waking Up app.

00:03:57.542 --> 00:03:59.500
If any of you want to
try it, you can simply go

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.252
to wakingup.com/huberman to
get that 30-day free trial.

00:04:03.252 --> 00:04:04.960
During today's
discussion, we didn't just

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.810
talk about meditation
consciousness and free will.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:09.340
We also talked
about psychedelics.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.650
Both their therapeutic
applications for the treatment

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:14.060
