00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:38.920
As far as I know, we have
not localized the region

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:41.410
in the brain that
can entirely account

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:43.180
for our perception of self.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:46.210
There are areas, of course,
that regulate proprioception,

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:48.800
our awareness of where
our limbs are in space.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:51.730
Maybe even our awareness of
where we are in physical space.

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:53.620
There are such circuits,
as we both know.

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:54.328
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

00:10:54.328 --> 00:10:57.930
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But when
we talk about sense of self,

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:01.818
I have to remember this kind
of neuroscience 101 thing

00:11:01.818 --> 00:11:02.610
that we always say.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:05.130
When you teach memory, you
say you wake up every morning,

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:06.480
and you remember who you are.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:07.620
You know who you are.

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:08.850
Most people do.

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:11.580
Even if they lack memory systems
in the brain for whatever

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:15.520
reason, pretty much everyone
seems to know who they are.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:18.900
What are your thoughts on what
that whole thing is about?

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:21.360
And do we come into the
world feeling that way?

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:25.922
I would appreciate answers from
the perspective of any field--

00:11:25.922 --> 00:11:26.630
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:27.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --including
neuroscience, of course.

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:28.050
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:28.980
Well, big question.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:31.980
I mean, the problem is we
use the term self in so

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:33.360
many different ways, right.

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:38.400
And there's one sense
of that term, which

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:40.590
is the target of
meditation, and it's

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:43.650
the target of deconstruction
by the practice and by,

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:45.420
as you said, any
surrounding philosophy.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:48.030
So you'll hear, and
you'll hear it from me,

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:51.480
that this self is
an illusion, right.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:54.840
And that there's a
psychological freedom that

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.000
can be experienced on the
other side of discovering

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:59.683
it to be an illusion.

00:11:59.683 --> 00:12:01.350
And some people don't
like that framing.

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:04.470
Some people would insist that
it's not so much an illusion,

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:09.990
but it's a construct, and
it's not what it seems, right.

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:14.430
But it's not that every use of
the term self is illegitimate.

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:17.580
And there are certain types of
selves that are not illusory.

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:20.730
I mean, I'm not saying
that people are illusions.

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:26.400
I'm not saying that you
can't talk about yourself

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:28.800
as distinct-- yourself
as the whole person

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:31.530
and with as
psychological continuity

00:12:31.530 --> 00:12:35.010
with your past experience
as being distinct

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:37.740
from the person and
psychological continuity

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:38.907
of some other person, right.

00:12:38.907 --> 00:12:40.365
I mean-- because
obviously, we have

00:12:40.365 --> 00:12:42.090
to be able to
conserve those data.

00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:44.040
It's not fundamentally
mysterious

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:45.630
that you're going
to wake up tomorrow

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:48.090
morning still being
psychologically continuous

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:50.220
with your past and
not my past, right.

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:56.920
And if we swapped lives, that
would demand some explanation.

00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:59.460
So the illusorines
of the self doesn't

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:02.070
cut against any of
those obvious facts.

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:06.210
So the sense of self
that is illusory.

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:08.850
And again, we might want to
talk about self in other modes

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:11.850
because there's just
a lot of interest

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:17.550
there psychologically and
ultimately scientifically.

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:19.770
The thing that doesn't exist--

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:21.510
it certainly doesn't
exist as it seems

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:23.610
and I would want to argue
that it actually is just

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:28.750
a proper illusion,
is this the sense

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:32.950
that there is a subject
interior to experience

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:34.310
in addition to experience.

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:36.580
So most people feel
like they're having

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:37.847
an experience of the world.

00:13:37.847 --> 00:13:40.180
And they're having their--
an experience of their bodies

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:41.390
in the world.

00:13:41.390 --> 00:13:43.750
And in addition
to that, they feel

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:48.370
that they are a subject internal
to the body, very likely

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:49.030
in the head.

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:52.420
Most people feel like they're
behind their face as a kind

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:57.400
of locus of awareness and
thought and intention and that

00:13:57.400 --> 00:14:00.010
every-- it's almost like
they're-- you're a passenger

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:01.240
inside your body.

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:04.030
You don't-- most people don't
feel identical to their bodies.

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:06.880
And they can
imagine this is sort

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:09.070
of the origin, the
psychological origin,

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:12.820
the folk psychological origin,
of a sense of that there

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:15.280
might be a soul that could
survive the death of the body.

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:17.800
I mean, most people are
what my friend Paul Blum

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:19.720
calls common sense do lists.

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:23.800
You-- you're just the
default expectation

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:27.430
seems to be that whatever the
relationship between the mind

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:30.580
and the body, there
is this-- there's

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:33.290
some promise of
separability there, right.

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:38.063
That the-- and whenever you
really push hard on the science

00:14:38.063 --> 00:14:39.980
side and say, well, no,
no, the mind is really

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:42.050
just what the brain
is doing, that

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:44.840
begins to feel more and more
counterintuitive to people,

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:47.930
and there still seems
some residual mystery

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:52.060
that at death maybe something
is going to lift off the brain

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:53.060
and go elsewhere, right.

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:56.330
So there's this sense of
dualism that many people have.

00:14:56.330 --> 00:15:01.610
And obviously, that's supported
by many religious beliefs.

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:07.310
But this feeling it is a
very peculiar starting point.

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:10.550
People feel that in a--

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:12.948
they don't feel identical
to their experience.

00:15:12.948 --> 00:15:14.990
As a matter of experience,
they feel like they're

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:17.600
on the edge of experience.

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:20.670
Somehow appropriating
it from the side.

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:22.470
You kind of on the
edge of the world.

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:24.050
And the world is out there.

00:15:24.050 --> 00:15:27.170
Your body is, in
some sense, an object

00:15:27.170 --> 00:15:30.590
in the world which is
different from the world.

00:15:30.590 --> 00:15:33.380
The boundary of your
skin is still meaningful.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:36.650
You can sort of loosely
control your body.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:39.080
I mean, you can't control it--
you can control your gross

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:41.990
and subtle voluntary
motor movements,

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:45.428
but you can't-- you're
not controlling everything

00:15:45.428 --> 00:15:46.220
your body is doing.

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:47.762
You're not controlling
your heartbeat

00:15:47.762 --> 00:15:51.925
and your hormonal
secretions and all of that.

00:15:51.925 --> 00:15:53.300
And so there's a
lot that's going

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:55.610
on that is in the dark for you.

00:15:55.610 --> 00:16:01.530
And then you give someone an
instruction to meditate, say.

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:03.530
And you say, OK, well,
let's examine all of this

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:04.655
from the first person side.

00:16:04.655 --> 00:16:07.640
Let's look for this
thing you're calling I.

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:10.850
And again, I is not
identical to the body.

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:12.642
People feel like their
hands are out there.

00:16:12.642 --> 00:16:14.308
And when-- if they're
going to meditate,

00:16:14.308 --> 00:16:16.225
they're going to close
their eyes very likely.

00:16:16.225 --> 00:16:18.391
And now they're going to
pay attention to something.

00:16:18.391 --> 00:16:20.870
They're going to pay attention
to the breath or the sounds.

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:25.010
And it's from the point of view
of being a locus of attention

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:28.170
that is now aiming attention
strategically at an object,

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:32.150
like the breath, that there's
this dualism that is set up.

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:36.420
And ultimately, the ultimate
promise of meditation.

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:38.640
I mean, there are
really two levels

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:42.210
at which you could be
interested in meditation.

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:46.890
One is very straightforward and
remedial and non paradoxical

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:48.420
and very well subscribed.

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:52.262
And it's the usual set of claims
about all the benefits you're

00:16:52.262 --> 00:16:53.470
going to get from meditation.

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:54.660
So you're going to
lower your stress,

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:56.460
and you're going to
increase your focus,

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:58.830
and you're going to stave
off cortical thinning,

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:00.330
and there's all
kinds of good things

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:05.770
that science is saying
meditation will give you.

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:08.770
And none of that entails
really drilling down

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:11.170
on this paradoxical claim
that the self is an illusion

00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:14.000
or anything else of that sort.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.200
But from my point of
view, the real purpose

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:18.819
of meditation and
its real promise

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:22.450
is not in this long
list of benefits.

00:17:22.450 --> 00:17:24.670
And I'm not discounting
any of those

00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:29.290
though the science for many
of them is quite provisional.

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:33.040
It's in this deeper claim that
if you look for this thing

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:35.110
you're calling I. If
you look for the sense

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:39.220
that there's a thinker
in addition to the mirror

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:43.340
rising of the next thought,
say, you won't find that thing.

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:47.810
And you can-- what's more,
you cannot find it in a way

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:50.990
that's conclusive and
that matters, right.

00:17:50.990 --> 00:17:53.810
And it has-- there's
a host of benefits

00:17:53.810 --> 00:17:59.980
that follow from that
discovery which are quite

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:02.620
a bit deeper and more
interesting than engaging

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:04.270
meditation on the
side of its benefits.

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:06.490
You know, de-stressing,
increasing focus,

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:08.080
and all the rest.

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:09.190
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I have a
number of questions related--

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:09.400
SAM HARRIS: Sure.

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:11.108
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --to
what you just said.

00:18:11.108 --> 00:18:15.190
And first of all, I agree that
the evidence that meditation

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:17.080
can improve focus,
reduce stress, et cetera.

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:17.710
It's there.

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:21.220
It's not an enormous pile of
evidence, but it's growing.

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:21.720
And--

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:21.790
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

00:18:21.790 --> 00:18:23.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --I
think that especially

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:25.360
for some of the shorter
meditations, which

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:29.500
I these days view more
as perceptual exercises.

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:32.140
I've talked about this
in the podcast before.

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:34.880
But for those who haven't heard
it before about perception.

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:37.510
You can have extra perception
extending to things

00:18:37.510 --> 00:18:38.920
beyond the confines
of your skin.

00:18:38.920 --> 00:18:41.230
Interception, which
is, I think, also

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:44.260
includes the surface of the
skin but everything inward.

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:48.820
And meditation through eyes
closed typically involving

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:51.130
some sort of attentional
spotlighting, something

00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:53.080
we'll get into more.

00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:55.370
Interior receptive versus
external receptive events,

00:18:55.370 --> 00:18:57.160
et cetera, including thoughts.

00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:59.470
And-- so I think of--

00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:02.080
at a basic level
meditation as somewhat

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:04.690
of a perceptual exercise.

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:06.400
You can tell me where
you disagree there,

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:08.755
and I would expect and
hope that you would.

00:19:08.755 --> 00:19:09.756
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

00:19:09.756 --> 00:19:12.256
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But I would
like to just touch on this idea

00:19:12.256 --> 00:19:14.631
that you brought up because
it's such an interesting one.

00:19:14.631 --> 00:19:17.170
Of this idea that our bodies
are containers and that

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:20.020
we somehow view
ourselves as passengers

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:22.000
within those containers.

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:24.370
That's certainly
been my experience.

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:27.680
And the image that
I have is of--

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:30.340
as you say, that is
of myself or of people

00:19:30.340 --> 00:19:34.390
out there that sit a few
centimeters below the surface

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:36.460
or that sit entirely
in their head.

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:38.590
And, of course,
the brain and body

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:40.360
are connected through
the nervous system.

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:42.778
I think sometimes
a brain is used

00:19:42.778 --> 00:19:44.320
to replace a nervous
system, and that

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:46.330
can get us into trouble
in terms of coming up

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:48.490
with real directions
and definitions.

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:52.193
But the point is that
there is something special

00:19:52.193 --> 00:19:53.610
about the real
estate in the head.

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:55.680
I think for as much
as my laboratory,

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:58.080
and many other scientists
are really interested

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:00.540
in brain-body connections
through the nervous system

00:20:00.540 --> 00:20:04.290
and other organ systems that
the nervous system binds

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:07.200
that if you cut
off all my limbs,

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:10.140
I'm going to be different, but
I'm fundamentally still Andrew.

00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:10.890
SAM HARRIS: Right.

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:12.348
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Whereas if we were

00:20:12.348 --> 00:20:14.340
to lesion a couple of
square millimeters out

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:17.400
of my parietal cortex,
it's an open question

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:20.563
as to whether or not I would
still seem as much like Andrew

00:20:20.563 --> 00:20:21.855
to other people and to myself--

00:20:21.855 --> 00:20:22.170
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:22.740
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --even.

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:24.782
And so there is something
fundamentally different

00:20:24.782 --> 00:20:27.090
about the real estate
in the cranial vault.

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:27.210
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

00:20:27.210 --> 00:20:27.450
I mean, we are--

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:29.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You can
even remove both of my eyes,

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:29.993
I'd still be Andrew.

00:20:29.993 --> 00:20:32.490
And those are two pieces of
my central nervous system

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:37.290
that are fundamental to my
daily life, but I'd still be me.

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:39.780
Whereas-- and this
doesn't, I think,

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:40.988
just apply to memory systems.

00:20:40.988 --> 00:20:42.363
I mean, I think
there are regions

00:20:42.363 --> 00:20:44.160
of the frontal cortex
that, when destroyed,

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:47.880
have been shown to modify
personality and self-perception

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:49.150
in dramatic ways.

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:51.630
So it's a sort of obvious
point once it's made,

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:54.465
but I do think it's worth
highlighting because there

00:20:54.465 --> 00:20:56.340
does seem to be something
special about being

00:20:56.340 --> 00:20:56.880
in the head.

00:20:56.880 --> 00:21:02.200
The other thing is that
sitting a few centimeters

00:21:02.200 --> 00:21:05.140
below the surface or
riding in this container

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:06.400
makes sense to me.

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:10.180
Except I wonder if
you've ever experienced

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:13.840
a shift as I have when
something very extreme happens.

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:17.200
Let's use the negative
example of all of a sudden

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:18.400
you're in a fear state.

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:22.030
All of a sudden, it feels as
if your entire body is you

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:23.530
or is me.

00:21:23.530 --> 00:21:26.680
And now I need to get this
thing-- the whole container

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:31.490
and me to some place of
safety in whatever form.

00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:33.730
This is also true, I
think, in ecstatic states--

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:34.000
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:34.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --where
you can feel really--

00:21:34.840 --> 00:21:37.090
when people say embodied,
I wonder whether or not

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:39.445
we normally oscillate below
the surface of our body.

00:21:39.445 --> 00:21:41.320
When I say oscillate,
I mean in neural terms.

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:43.180
I mean, maybe our
sensory experience

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:45.520
is not truly at
the bodily surface

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:47.847
but sits below the
bodily surface more

00:21:47.847 --> 00:21:49.930
at the level of organ
systems and within our head.

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:52.300
And then certain
things that jolt us--

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:55.240
our autonomic nervous system
into heightened states

00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:57.616
bring us into states of--

00:21:57.616 --> 00:22:00.400
bring us closer to the
surface and therefore include

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:01.390
all of us.

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:04.480
Again, I don't want to take us
down a mechanistic description

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:06.190
of something that doesn't exist.

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:08.800
But does any of that
resonate in terms

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:11.080
of how you are thinking
about or describing the self?

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:12.130
SAM HARRIS: Yeah, yeah.

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:13.690
There's a lot there.

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:16.990
First, on the point of
the brain being the locus

00:22:16.990 --> 00:22:20.193
of what we are as minds.

00:22:20.193 --> 00:22:21.610
Yeah, I mean, there
are people who

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:25.210
will insist that sort of
the whole nervous system

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:26.977
has to be thought of as a--

00:22:26.977 --> 00:22:28.810
when you're talking
about our emotional life

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:35.380
and the insulas
connection to the gut.

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:40.390
And just the sense of self
extends beyond the brain.

00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:44.230
But I totally take your
point that a brain transplant

00:22:44.230 --> 00:22:46.330
is a coherent
idea, and you would

00:22:46.330 --> 00:22:49.120
expect to go with the brain
rather than with the viscera.

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:52.720
And so, in that
sense, we really are

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:55.090
the old philosophical
thought experiment

00:22:55.090 --> 00:22:56.440
of being a brain in a VAT.

00:22:56.440 --> 00:22:57.940
I mean, we essentially
are already--

00:22:57.940 --> 00:23:03.003
the VAT is our skull, and we're
virtually in that situation.

00:23:03.003 --> 00:23:04.420
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
A horrible movie.

00:23:04.420 --> 00:23:05.485
I'm sorry I can't
help but interrupt.

00:23:05.485 --> 00:23:05.650
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:07.420
ANDREW HUBERMAN: When
I was a teenager,

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:08.560
my sister and I used
to go to the movies

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:10.090
every once in a while,
and we'd trade off

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:11.260
who could pick the movie.

00:23:11.260 --> 00:23:14.537
And she took me to see once
the movie Boxing Helena.

00:23:14.537 --> 00:23:15.370
SAM HARRIS: Oh yeah.

00:23:15.370 --> 00:23:16.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The
David Lynch film--

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:17.380
SAM HARRIS: Which I
never saw that again.

00:23:17.380 --> 00:23:18.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--where he amputates

00:23:18.310 --> 00:23:20.850
the limbs of a woman who he's
obsessed by and keeps her.

00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:21.850
It's a really horrible--

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:22.330
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

00:23:22.330 --> 00:23:23.330
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --film.

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:25.840
And about 20 minutes into it,
my sister just turned to me

00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:27.402
and said, I'm so sorry.

00:23:27.402 --> 00:23:29.110
And the question there
was whether or not

00:23:29.110 --> 00:23:31.590
two siblings should actually
persist in a movie like that.

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:31.960
SAM HARRIS: Right.

00:23:31.960 --> 00:23:33.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And we
decided to persist in the movie

00:23:33.460 --> 00:23:35.440
so that we could
laugh about it later.

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:36.793
But it was rather disturbing.

00:23:36.793 --> 00:23:37.960
I don't recommend the movie.

00:23:37.960 --> 00:23:39.793
Nor do I recommend
seeing it with a sibling.

00:23:39.793 --> 00:23:44.140
But in that movie, the woman,
he takes her as a container

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:45.610
and restricts her movement.

00:23:45.610 --> 00:23:49.600
Quite sadistic and
horrible thing really.

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:52.960
David Lynch, interesting
mind perhaps.

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:57.460
But the idea was to question
how much of the person

00:23:57.460 --> 00:24:00.130
persists in the absence of their
ability to move, et cetera.

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:01.030
Could there be love?

00:24:01.030 --> 00:24:03.550
Could there be these
other affections?

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:05.260
Anyway, a rather
extreme example.

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:07.218
But one that still haunts me.

00:24:07.218 --> 00:24:09.010
And I suppose I'm
thinking about still now.

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:09.700
SAM HARRIS: Yeah, yeah.

00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:10.617
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:24:10.617 --> 00:24:14.500
SAM HARRIS: Well, so just
to follow that point,

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:20.050
there's a lot about us that we
don't have access to unless we

00:24:20.050 --> 00:24:21.220
enact it physically.

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:26.650
Like if I ask you, do you
still know how to ride a bike?

00:24:26.650 --> 00:24:28.780
There's no place in
your memory where

00:24:28.780 --> 00:24:32.320
you can inspect but just
sitting in your chair

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:34.630
that you've retained
the knowledge of how

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:35.662
to ride a bike.

00:24:35.662 --> 00:24:37.120
Procedural memory
is different from

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:38.350
semantic or episodic memory.

00:24:38.350 --> 00:24:42.400
If I asked you, do
you know your address?

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:45.730
Yes, you can recall your
address just sitting there.

00:24:45.730 --> 00:24:48.790
But if you had had a micro
stroke that neatly dissected

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:51.490
out your ability to
ride a bike and left

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:53.773
everything else
intact, you might

00:24:53.773 --> 00:24:54.940
think you could ride a bike.

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:56.963
But suddenly, you
stand up next to one.

00:24:56.963 --> 00:24:58.630
And you have no idea
what to do with it.

00:24:58.630 --> 00:25:00.047
And that would be
a discovery that

00:25:00.047 --> 00:25:02.440
would only happen if
you were motorically

00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:06.010
engaged with that object.

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:07.990
And I'm sure we could
probably come up

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:11.530
with 100 things about us
that really seem core to us

00:25:11.530 --> 00:25:18.390
and not separable from
our personhood which

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:21.780
seemed to only get
invoked when we're

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:25.230
out they're moving in the world,
and we have limbs, et cetera.

00:25:29.530 --> 00:25:36.948
No, it's the seat
of consciousness,

00:25:36.948 --> 00:25:38.490
the right framework
to talk about all

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:39.420
of this, from my
point of view, is

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:41.018
consciousness and its contents.

00:25:41.018 --> 00:25:42.060
So we have consciousness.

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:44.940
The fact that there's something
that is like to be us,

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:49.350
the fact that the world
and our internal experience

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:50.790
is illuminated.

00:25:50.790 --> 00:25:54.277
It has a qualitative character.

00:25:54.277 --> 00:25:55.860
And then, there's
the question of what

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:58.050
is that qualitative character?

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:00.330
What kinds of information
do we have access to?

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:01.950
What does it feel like to be us?

00:26:01.950 --> 00:26:05.880
How do different states
of arousal change that?

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:06.960
So you talked about fear.

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.820
Yeah, fear can change
a lot of things.

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:14.640
And various neurological
deficits, or you

00:26:14.640 --> 00:26:15.850
can add drugs to the mix.

00:26:15.850 --> 00:26:18.690
You add psychedelics
that radically transform

00:26:18.690 --> 00:26:20.310
the contents of consciousness.

00:26:20.310 --> 00:26:21.810
From my point of
view, consciousness

00:26:21.810 --> 00:26:26.790
itself is simply the
cognizance, the awareness,

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:31.140
that is the flood lights
by which any of that stuff

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:31.980
appears.

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:36.330
So consciousness doesn't change.

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:38.010
But its contents change.

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:42.450
And to come back to meditation
for a second, many people

00:26:42.450 --> 00:26:44.610
think meditation
is about changing

00:26:44.610 --> 00:26:46.120
the contents of consciousness.

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:49.290
There are some contents you
want to get rid of like anxiety.

00:26:49.290 --> 00:26:51.570
Other contents you
want to encourage,

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:55.800
like calm, and unconditional
love, or some other classically

00:26:55.800 --> 00:26:58.740
pleasant, prosocial emotion.

00:26:58.740 --> 00:26:59.880
And that's all fine.

00:26:59.880 --> 00:27:01.230
That's all possible.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:07.350
But the real wisdom of
the 2000-year-old wisdom

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:11.580
of meditation that really is the
chewy center of the Tootsie Pop

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:16.080
is a recognition of what
consciousness itself is always

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:18.540
already like regardless
of the contents in it

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:19.980
and the changes in contents.

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:23.010
And this is why we
might talk about this.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:26.520
But this is why they're
mutually compatible.

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:29.310
Psychedelics and meditation,
for me, are somewhat orthogonal.

00:27:29.310 --> 00:27:32.940
Because psychedelics is all
about making wholesale changes

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:34.320
to the contents
of consciousness.

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:38.070
And there's some wonderful
consequences of doing that.

00:27:38.070 --> 00:27:40.677
There can be some harrowing
and terrifying consequences

00:27:40.677 --> 00:27:41.260
of doing that.

00:27:41.260 --> 00:27:45.137
But generally speaking,
I think used wisely,

00:27:45.137 --> 00:27:46.470
they can be incredibly valuable.

00:27:46.470 --> 00:27:50.160
And the therapeutic
potential there is enormous.

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:55.560
But the crucial disjunction
here is that there really

00:27:55.560 --> 00:27:58.630
is something to recognize about
ordinary waking consciousness,

00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:01.080
the consciousness that's
compatible with my driving

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:03.150
a car to get here on time.

00:28:03.150 --> 00:28:08.580
You don't have to have the
pyrotechnics of being on LSD

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:16.240
to see to transcend the
central illusion that I'm

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:18.190
saying is the thing
to be transcended,

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:21.520
which is the sense that there
is a duality between subject

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:24.940
and object in every
moment of experience.

00:28:24.940 --> 00:28:27.580
And to take it back to something
you said about just all

00:28:27.580 --> 00:28:30.130
of our different modes
in ordinary life,

00:28:30.130 --> 00:28:32.500
the interesting thing is I
think people are constantly

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:34.600
losing their sense of self.

00:28:34.600 --> 00:28:35.920
And they're not aware of it.

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:41.590
And there's probably an
analogy to the visual system

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:46.600
here, which is to visual
saccade, which perhaps you've

00:28:46.600 --> 00:28:48.550
spoken about at some
point on your podcast.

00:28:48.550 --> 00:28:49.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Not enough.

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:49.980
So please.

00:28:49.980 --> 00:28:50.710
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

00:28:50.710 --> 00:28:53.350
So what happens every
time we move our eyes,

00:28:53.350 --> 00:28:54.730
this is called a saccade.

00:28:54.730 --> 00:28:59.890
And we do that about three times
a second or so just normally.

00:28:59.890 --> 00:29:03.370
There is the region
of motor cortex

00:29:03.370 --> 00:29:06.550
that affects that
movement sends what's

00:29:06.550 --> 00:29:12.430
called an efferent copy of
that motor movement, which

00:29:12.430 --> 00:29:15.040
is used as information
that propagates back

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:19.390
to visual cortex, that
suppresses the data of vision

00:29:19.390 --> 00:29:20.920
while the eyes are moving.

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:23.223
Because otherwise if
you weren't doing that,

00:29:23.223 --> 00:29:24.640
every time you
moved your eyes, it

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:27.580
would seem like the visual scene
itself was lurching around.

00:29:27.580 --> 00:29:29.830
And people can experience
this for themselves

00:29:29.830 --> 00:29:33.220
if they just touch one of
their eyeballs on the side,

00:29:33.220 --> 00:29:35.505
not all that hard,
and kind of jiggle it.

00:29:35.505 --> 00:29:36.880
And then, you can
roll it around.

00:29:36.880 --> 00:29:38.380
You can jiggle it
from side to side.

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:43.390
You can see a movement
of the eyeball that's

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:46.060
not governed by your
oculomotor system

00:29:46.060 --> 00:29:47.980
delivers a jiggling
of the world.

00:29:47.980 --> 00:29:50.163
Because your brain is
not anticipating it

00:29:50.163 --> 00:29:50.830
in the same way.

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:56.500
And you're not producing
that same predictive copy

00:29:56.500 --> 00:29:57.860
of the movement.

00:29:57.860 --> 00:30:00.447
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
a little bit like--

00:30:00.447 --> 00:30:02.530
we have some action sports
filmmakers on our staff

00:30:02.530 --> 00:30:05.660
here that the gimbal,
that holds an iPhone,

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:07.183
like you see the
kids on surfboards

00:30:07.183 --> 00:30:08.350
or skateboards or something.

00:30:08.350 --> 00:30:10.392
They're going to hold a
phone while moving around

00:30:10.392 --> 00:30:12.490
or the people, the
vloggers-- does anyone

00:30:12.490 --> 00:30:13.850
even still use that for phrase?

00:30:13.850 --> 00:30:14.892
SAM HARRIS: I don't know.

00:30:14.892 --> 00:30:16.000
I guess.

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:18.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Moving around
and it's image stabilization,

00:30:18.730 --> 00:30:21.970
essentially, that keeps
the camera steady.

00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:24.647
And these are more than
cameras, of course,

00:30:24.647 --> 00:30:26.230
for those listening,
point at my eyes.

00:30:26.230 --> 00:30:28.480
But they do far more than
just what a camera would do.

00:30:28.480 --> 00:30:32.500
But this internal system
of image stabilization, I

00:30:32.500 --> 00:30:35.290
can see perhaps where
you're going with this,

00:30:35.290 --> 00:30:42.120
that it allows us to remain in
a self-referencing scheme, as

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:45.840
opposed to paying attention
to just how confusing it

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:47.703
is to track the visual
world at some level.

00:30:47.703 --> 00:30:49.620
SAM HARRIS: Well, actually
where I'm going is,

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:54.750
that people are having this
suppression of vision three

00:30:54.750 --> 00:30:56.580
times a second on average.

00:30:56.580 --> 00:30:58.830
And they're not experiencing it.

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:02.670
You're effectively going blind.

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:05.897
And you're not noticing it.

00:31:05.897 --> 00:31:07.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's very fast.

00:31:07.230 --> 00:31:08.563
SAM HARRIS: Yes, it's very fast.

00:31:08.563 --> 00:31:15.450
Now there's an analogous
suppression, I would say,

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:20.960
of the sense of self that
occurs every time attention gets

00:31:20.960 --> 00:31:24.530
absorbed significantly
in its object.

00:31:24.530 --> 00:31:29.810
We even have this concept of
losing yourself in your work.

00:31:29.810 --> 00:31:33.860
Classic flow experiences
have this quality.

00:31:33.860 --> 00:31:38.090
And this tends to be why
they're so rewarding.

00:31:38.090 --> 00:31:45.290
If you're in some athletic
activity or an aesthetic one,

00:31:45.290 --> 00:31:47.150
or you could be having
sex, or whatever

00:31:47.150 --> 00:31:51.500
it is, some peak experience,
its peakness usually

00:31:51.500 --> 00:31:55.010
entails there being
some brief period

00:31:55.010 --> 00:31:59.462
where there was no distance
between you and the experience.

00:31:59.462 --> 00:32:00.920
For that moment,
you were no longer

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:05.090
looking over your own shoulder
or anticipating the next moment

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:07.790
or trying to get somewhere,
where you weren't,

00:32:07.790 --> 00:32:12.440
or micromanaging errors.

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:18.290
There's just the flow of unity
with whatever the experience

00:32:18.290 --> 00:32:21.890
is, a surfer on the wave.

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:24.780
And we love those experiences.

00:32:24.780 --> 00:32:30.770
And then we are continually
abstracted away from them

00:32:30.770 --> 00:32:32.477
by our thinking about them.

00:32:32.477 --> 00:32:34.310
We're thinking, oh my
god, that was so good,

00:32:34.310 --> 00:32:35.518
or how do I get back to that?

00:32:35.518 --> 00:32:37.220
Or you're looking at a sunset.

00:32:37.220 --> 00:32:39.650
It's the most beautiful
sunset you've ever seen.

00:32:39.650 --> 00:32:43.640
And then you're continually
interrupting the experience

00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:47.210
of merely seeing it with
a commentary about how

00:32:47.210 --> 00:32:48.050
amazing this is.

00:32:48.050 --> 00:32:50.540
And I wonder, what real
estate prices are here?

00:32:50.540 --> 00:32:52.520
I mean, is it possible
that I could move here.

00:32:52.520 --> 00:32:56.960
And your mind is just
continually narrating

00:32:56.960 --> 00:32:59.720
a conversation you're having
with yourself, however

00:32:59.720 --> 00:33:00.410
paradoxically.

00:33:00.410 --> 00:33:02.930
I mean, you're telling
yourself things

00:33:02.930 --> 00:33:06.500
that you already know as
though there are two of you

00:33:06.500 --> 00:33:08.960
rather often.

00:33:08.960 --> 00:33:11.867
I'm looking for--
which is the water.

00:33:11.867 --> 00:33:12.950
And it's, oh, there it is.

00:33:12.950 --> 00:33:15.500
But I'm the one seeing it.

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:18.260
Who am I saying,
oh, there it is to.

00:33:18.260 --> 00:33:20.990
Is there someone else
who needs to be informed

00:33:20.990 --> 00:33:23.660
about the thing I already saw.

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:26.390
So there's something about
our internal dialogue

00:33:26.390 --> 00:33:29.780
that is paradoxical.

00:33:29.780 --> 00:33:34.136
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is there
any neurologic condition

00:33:34.136 --> 00:33:37.850
call it soulectomy or anything
like that where somehow people

00:33:37.850 --> 00:33:41.900
feel more unified with the
self on a continual basis,

00:33:41.900 --> 00:33:46.220
the observer and
the actor within?

00:33:46.220 --> 00:33:49.250
State more as a
complete sentence.

00:33:49.250 --> 00:33:52.697
Is there any known
neurological syndrome--

00:33:52.697 --> 00:33:55.280
makes it sound like a bad thing,
but it could be a good thing.

00:33:55.280 --> 00:33:58.250
--whereby people
feel that the actor

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:01.460
and the observer within them
are unified continually?

00:34:01.460 --> 00:34:03.380
SAM HARRIS: There's
not a pathological one.

00:34:03.380 --> 00:34:05.900
Some the work on the
default-mode network

00:34:05.900 --> 00:34:09.030
suggests that that's at
least part of the story.

00:34:09.030 --> 00:34:12.980
So the default-mode
network, which

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:15.710
has been talked about a
lot of late because it

00:34:15.710 --> 00:34:18.800
has come up both in the
meditation literature

00:34:18.800 --> 00:34:21.080
and in the psychedelic
literature.

00:34:21.080 --> 00:34:25.400
But its original discovery
was that-- and the reason

00:34:25.400 --> 00:34:27.199
why I was called the
default mode was is

00:34:27.199 --> 00:34:30.050
that in virtually every
neuroimaging experiment ever

00:34:30.050 --> 00:34:34.370
run, they found that between
tasks, when the brain was just

00:34:34.370 --> 00:34:38.810
in its default state, these
midline structures would

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:40.760
increase their activity.

00:34:40.760 --> 00:34:43.550
And then they would
reliably diminish

00:34:43.550 --> 00:34:47.960
whenever the person in
the scanner was on task.

00:34:47.960 --> 00:34:52.310
And usually, that meant
some outward looking

00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:53.810
visual discrimination task.

00:34:53.810 --> 00:34:55.730
But it could be visual.

00:34:55.730 --> 00:34:56.840
It could be semantic.

00:34:56.840 --> 00:34:58.732
But it tends to be
their eyes are open.

00:34:58.732 --> 00:35:00.440
And they're paying
attention to something

00:35:00.440 --> 00:35:04.588
that's being broadcast to
them through monitor goggles,

00:35:04.588 --> 00:35:07.130
or they're looking at a mirror
that's showing them a computer

00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:08.970
monitor.

00:35:08.970 --> 00:35:12.170
But so the general
insight was, there

00:35:12.170 --> 00:35:14.120
are these midline
structures in the brain that

00:35:14.120 --> 00:35:18.380
seem to be increasing
their activity when

00:35:18.380 --> 00:35:21.110
the brain is idling
between tasks, waiting

00:35:21.110 --> 00:35:22.800
for something to happen.

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:25.760
And then further
experiments found

00:35:25.760 --> 00:35:31.730
tasks that actually upregulated
activity there beyond baseline.

00:35:31.730 --> 00:35:35.870
And those tasks seem
to be self referential.

00:35:35.870 --> 00:35:37.380
So that when you ask people--

00:35:37.380 --> 00:35:38.630
you give them a list of words.

00:35:38.630 --> 00:35:43.250
And you say, well, do any
of these apply to you?

00:35:43.250 --> 00:35:44.855
Or you ask people
to think about--

00:35:48.020 --> 00:35:49.430
Actually, one experiment I did.

00:35:49.430 --> 00:35:53.090
When you're challenging
people's beliefs, when you're

00:35:53.090 --> 00:35:55.370
challenging beliefs
that have more

00:35:55.370 --> 00:35:58.640
of a personal significance, like
political or religious beliefs,

00:35:58.640 --> 00:36:00.650
you get an upregulation
in these regions

00:36:00.650 --> 00:36:03.920
as opposed to just generic
beliefs about you're

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:04.920
in Los Angeles.

00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:05.690
This is a table.

00:36:05.690 --> 00:36:09.920
That is something to which
people are not holding fast

00:36:09.920 --> 00:36:11.030
as a matter of identity.

00:36:13.650 --> 00:36:17.900
So anyway, both meditation
and psychedelics

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:22.040
seem to suppress activity
in these regions, which

00:36:22.040 --> 00:36:28.010
we know are associated with
both self-talk, mind-wandering,

00:36:28.010 --> 00:36:29.985
and explicit acts of
self representation.

00:36:29.985 --> 00:36:31.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Could we say that they

00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:33.650
are somewhat
autobiographical because they

00:36:33.650 --> 00:36:35.180
access memory systems?

00:36:35.180 --> 00:36:37.803
And in the way you're
describing them,

00:36:37.803 --> 00:36:39.470
and in the way that
a colleague of mine,

00:36:39.470 --> 00:36:40.800
who's been a guest
on this podcast--

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:42.140
I don't know if you've
interacted with him before.

00:36:42.140 --> 00:36:45.110
But I think you'd very much
enjoy whatever interaction you

00:36:45.110 --> 00:36:46.850
would have. --is David Spiegel.

00:36:46.850 --> 00:36:48.800
He's our associate
chair of psychiatry.

00:36:48.800 --> 00:36:50.660
He and his father
actually-- his father

00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:55.190
then he founded hypnosis as
a valid clinical practice

00:36:55.190 --> 00:36:55.850
in psychiatry.

00:36:55.850 --> 00:36:58.970
And hypnosis, which is obviously
a heightened sense of attention

00:36:58.970 --> 00:37:02.300
with deep relaxation, is
known to dramatically suppress

00:37:02.300 --> 00:37:04.337
the default-mode network.

00:37:04.337 --> 00:37:05.420
He talks about this a lot.

00:37:05.420 --> 00:37:10.550
And I always wonder as
we take down activity

00:37:10.550 --> 00:37:16.460
within the default-mode
network, what what surfaces

00:37:16.460 --> 00:37:20.450
in its place, does that
somehow reflect that the two

00:37:20.450 --> 00:37:22.100
are normally in a push-pull?

00:37:22.100 --> 00:37:24.140
Because that's not
necessarily the case.

00:37:24.140 --> 00:37:25.910
When I fall asleep,
I can hallucinate.

00:37:25.910 --> 00:37:27.830
But that doesn't mean
that during the day,

00:37:27.830 --> 00:37:29.630
the fact that I'm
looking at objects

00:37:29.630 --> 00:37:31.790
is what's preventing
me from hallucinating.

00:37:31.790 --> 00:37:33.650
If I close my eyes,
I can get imagery.

00:37:33.650 --> 00:37:36.620
But there's this
different illusion,

00:37:36.620 --> 00:37:39.722
the illusion of antagonistic
circuitry sometimes.

00:37:39.722 --> 00:37:41.180
I don't want to
take us off course,

00:37:41.180 --> 00:37:45.715
but the default-mode network
seems to "want to be there,"

00:37:45.715 --> 00:37:46.340
quote, unquote.

00:37:46.340 --> 00:37:50.030
It seems to be fighting
for our attention,

00:37:50.030 --> 00:37:51.770
unless we give ourselves
a visual target,

00:37:51.770 --> 00:37:54.350
or an auditory target, or
some salient experience

00:37:54.350 --> 00:37:55.820
of some kind, it sounds like.

00:37:55.820 --> 00:38:00.410
And then, I'm surprised to
hear that meditation reduces

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:02.780
activity in the default-mode
network at some level

00:38:02.780 --> 00:38:06.050
because meditation
to me oftentimes

00:38:06.050 --> 00:38:10.550
involves paying attention
to some perceptual target.

00:38:10.550 --> 00:38:12.980
Maybe you could
eventually explain

00:38:12.980 --> 00:38:15.757
as to how it might do
that or why it might.

00:38:15.757 --> 00:38:17.840
SAM HARRIS: And I don't
think it's the whole story

00:38:17.840 --> 00:38:21.660
because, obviously,
outward-going attention is

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:22.160
not--

00:38:25.080 --> 00:38:27.650
even if you're having
the kind of egoic

00:38:27.650 --> 00:38:30.780
saccade that I'm talking about,
where you're actually not

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:32.580
clearly aware of yourself.

00:38:32.580 --> 00:38:34.440
You're not clearly
defining yourself

00:38:34.440 --> 00:38:39.210
as separate from experience for
the moment of paying attention,

00:38:39.210 --> 00:38:41.250
so you are sort of losing
yourself in your work.

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:42.930
That's not the same
thing as having

00:38:42.930 --> 00:38:46.500
the clear meditative insight of
selflessness that I'm claiming

00:38:46.500 --> 00:38:49.410
is the goal of meditation.

00:38:49.410 --> 00:38:53.280
But to wind back to the
original point I was making,

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:57.300
and the reason why I drew the
analogy to visual saccades,

00:38:57.300 --> 00:39:01.230
I do think there's a continuous
interruption in our sense

00:39:01.230 --> 00:39:03.390
of self that goes unrecognized.

00:39:07.190 --> 00:39:13.350
But the conscious acquisition
of the understanding

00:39:13.350 --> 00:39:16.940
that the self is an illusion
is a different experience

00:39:16.940 --> 00:39:21.200
because you're then
focusing on this absence.

00:39:21.200 --> 00:39:23.210
Actually, there's
another analogy

00:39:23.210 --> 00:39:26.610
to the visual system
that applies here,

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:31.070
which is to the optic
blind spot, which

00:39:31.070 --> 00:39:33.080
is a good analogy for
me because it cuts

00:39:33.080 --> 00:39:34.970
through a bunch of
false assumptions

00:39:34.970 --> 00:39:37.700
as to where you
would look for this

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:41.000
or how this relates to
ordinary experience.

00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:44.960
So as many people know
that in both eyes we have

00:39:44.960 --> 00:39:46.940
what's called the
blind spot, which

00:39:46.940 --> 00:39:50.210
is a consequence of the
optic nerve transiting

00:39:50.210 --> 00:39:53.930
through the retina,
unlike cephalopods.

00:39:53.930 --> 00:39:57.950
I think cephalopods
have their optic nerve,

00:39:57.950 --> 00:40:02.420
as an omniscient being
would have engineered it,

00:40:02.420 --> 00:40:04.520
connecting the
retina from the back.

00:40:04.520 --> 00:40:08.083
And therefore, there
is no area of blindness

00:40:08.083 --> 00:40:10.250
associated with its transit
back through the retina.

00:40:10.250 --> 00:40:11.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But our
receptors are one the outside.

00:40:11.990 --> 00:40:12.770
SAM HARRIS: Exactly.

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:14.312
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Humans, for whatever

00:40:14.312 --> 00:40:16.190
reason, put photoreceptors--
well, I always

00:40:16.190 --> 00:40:18.080
say I wasn't consulted
on the design phase.

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:21.920
Something put photoreceptors,
combination of things,

00:40:21.920 --> 00:40:23.270
put photoreceptors in the back.

00:40:23.270 --> 00:40:26.300
And so you actually have to
send the highway of information

00:40:26.300 --> 00:40:30.450
through the pixel
center of the eye.

00:40:30.450 --> 00:40:35.060
Cephalopods and
drosophila, basically

00:40:35.060 --> 00:40:40.400
invertebrates, the design
is more at its face logical.

00:40:40.400 --> 00:40:43.970
Mammals, very illogical
design, at least as far

00:40:43.970 --> 00:40:45.440
as our judgments go.

00:40:45.440 --> 00:40:47.250
SAM HARRIS: But it
gives me a good analogy.

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:48.462
So I'll take it.

