00:41:57.362 --> 00:41:58.820
SAM HARRIS: So in
any case, we have

00:41:58.820 --> 00:42:03.410
this blind spot, which I
think most people learn

00:42:03.410 --> 00:42:04.580
this in school.

00:42:04.580 --> 00:42:06.997
Although, my daughters had not
been taught this in school.

00:42:06.997 --> 00:42:09.660
I just showed them this for
the first time a month ago.

00:42:09.660 --> 00:42:11.480
And they were
briefly fascinated.

00:42:11.480 --> 00:42:14.000
And then they went to
return to their screen time.

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:16.580
But anyway, you can
take a piece of paper.

00:42:16.580 --> 00:42:18.170
And you make two marks on it.

00:42:18.170 --> 00:42:19.700
And then you cover one eye.

00:42:19.700 --> 00:42:23.162
And you fixate on one mark.

00:42:23.162 --> 00:42:24.620
I mean, you can
look this up online

00:42:24.620 --> 00:42:26.930
if you need details
about how to do this.

00:42:26.930 --> 00:42:30.380
And while staring at
one fixation point,

00:42:30.380 --> 00:42:32.312
you move the paper
back and forth.

00:42:32.312 --> 00:42:33.770
And you can get it
to a place where

00:42:33.770 --> 00:42:36.110
the other mark disappears.

00:42:36.110 --> 00:42:38.510
And you can run this
experiment long enough

00:42:38.510 --> 00:42:40.400
to satisfy yourself
that there is

00:42:40.400 --> 00:42:42.680
in fact a blind spot in
your visual field, which

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:46.700
with one eye closed you
don't normally notice.

00:42:46.700 --> 00:42:48.560
The reason why you
have to cover one eye

00:42:48.560 --> 00:42:51.050
is because each eye
compensates for the blind spot

00:42:51.050 --> 00:42:52.970
of the other.

00:42:52.970 --> 00:42:54.950
Which is to say that
if you close one eye

00:42:54.950 --> 00:42:58.455
and survey the visual scene,
something really is missing,

00:42:58.455 --> 00:42:59.580
whatever you're looking at.

00:42:59.580 --> 00:43:01.205
If you're looking at
a crowd of people,

00:43:01.205 --> 00:43:03.680
somebody is missing a head
and you're not noticing it.

00:43:03.680 --> 00:43:09.680
And it's not easy to notice
because the brain doesn't

00:43:09.680 --> 00:43:14.760
tend to vividly represent
the absence of information.

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:17.930
I mean, it's just part of the
game that's not being rendered.

00:43:17.930 --> 00:43:22.320
It's not showing up as a
break in the visual field.

00:43:22.320 --> 00:43:23.990
It's just not there.

00:43:23.990 --> 00:43:27.020
And I mean, people have
argued that there's

00:43:27.020 --> 00:43:29.570
a filling-in phenomenon
that happens,

00:43:29.570 --> 00:43:32.947
but I think that can be
misunderstood or exaggerated.

00:43:32.947 --> 00:43:35.030
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But the
eye movements themselves,

00:43:35.030 --> 00:43:36.770
that you described before--

00:43:36.770 --> 00:43:40.370
I guess, I should say
that the saccade analogy

00:43:40.370 --> 00:43:44.780
about transiently and
repetitively erasing the self

00:43:44.780 --> 00:43:47.570
works perfectly here because,
indeed, microsaccades,

00:43:47.570 --> 00:43:49.940
little smaller saccades, occur
all the time also prevent

00:43:49.940 --> 00:43:52.017
our eyes from fixating
in one location

00:43:52.017 --> 00:43:53.600
long enough to observe
our blind spot,

00:43:53.600 --> 00:43:55.550
even if one eye is closed.

00:43:55.550 --> 00:43:59.360
So if the experiment's done with
paralytics to essentially lock

00:43:59.360 --> 00:44:02.300
eyes at one location, basically,
things start disappearing.

00:44:02.300 --> 00:44:02.810
SAM HARRIS: It just fades away.

00:44:02.810 --> 00:44:03.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: We
all love to think

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:06.260
that we start hallucinating, but
actually, we start going blind.

00:44:06.260 --> 00:44:08.600
And those experiments
have been done on humans.

00:44:08.600 --> 00:44:10.832
I hear they're quite terrifying.

00:44:10.832 --> 00:44:13.040
SAM HARRIS: I mean, you can
do that for yourself too.

00:44:13.040 --> 00:44:19.070
It begins to just all
melt away in a warm glow.

00:44:19.070 --> 00:44:21.050
No psychedelics required.

00:44:21.050 --> 00:44:24.560
But the interesting
point there is

00:44:24.560 --> 00:44:27.620
that when you ask
yourself, OK, so

00:44:27.620 --> 00:44:30.260
because as a consequence
of the eyes' anatomy,

00:44:30.260 --> 00:44:34.280
there's this thing
you can see that is

00:44:34.280 --> 00:44:36.920
absent from your experience.

00:44:36.920 --> 00:44:39.830
But the question
is, where is that

00:44:39.830 --> 00:44:43.290
in relationship to the
rest of you, to your mind?

00:44:43.290 --> 00:44:44.480
Is that deep within?

00:44:44.480 --> 00:44:47.760
Or is that in some sense right
on the surface of experience?

00:44:47.760 --> 00:44:51.310
And there's expectation
that people have.

00:44:51.310 --> 00:44:54.010
Again, I think
conflating meditation

00:44:54.010 --> 00:44:57.490
with a search for changes in
the context of consciousness.

00:44:57.490 --> 00:45:01.840
They're looking for
much more subtle things

00:45:01.840 --> 00:45:07.030
to notice about the mind or
much vaster things to notice.

00:45:07.030 --> 00:45:09.880
Psychedelics sets
up this expectation

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:14.110
that you do a massive
dose of mushrooms or LSD,

00:45:14.110 --> 00:45:16.150
and everything changes.

00:45:16.150 --> 00:45:20.170
I mean, you get this
full beatific vision.

00:45:20.170 --> 00:45:25.120
And you get not only visual
changes, but emotional changes.

00:45:25.120 --> 00:45:29.260
And you get synesthesia,
where you have

00:45:29.260 --> 00:45:33.800
much more mind in so many ways.

00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:36.970
So they begin having
these experiences,

00:45:36.970 --> 00:45:39.350
or reading the mystical
literature, you begin to think,

00:45:39.350 --> 00:45:45.100
OK, well, then freedom is really
elsewhere or it's deep within.

00:45:45.100 --> 00:45:50.320
It's not coincident with
the ordinary awareness

00:45:50.320 --> 00:45:53.920
that can see this
coffee cup clearly

00:45:53.920 --> 00:45:55.690
and that can just
transition attention

00:45:55.690 --> 00:46:00.550
to reading an email
with the full sobriety

00:46:00.550 --> 00:46:04.630
of ordinary waking
consciousness.

00:46:04.630 --> 00:46:07.390
But the truth is, this
insight into selflessness,

00:46:07.390 --> 00:46:10.930
this insight into the nonduality
of subject and object,

00:46:10.930 --> 00:46:16.510
is as close to ordinary
consciousness as this insight

00:46:16.510 --> 00:46:18.130
into the optic blind spot.

00:46:18.130 --> 00:46:20.267
Where do you have to
go to have this insight

00:46:20.267 --> 00:46:21.100
into the blind spot?

00:46:21.100 --> 00:46:23.300
you don't have to go anywhere.

00:46:23.300 --> 00:46:26.290
You just have to set up the
experiment correctly such

00:46:26.290 --> 00:46:31.130
that you can see the data.

00:46:31.130 --> 00:46:32.810
But the data is
right on the surface.

00:46:32.810 --> 00:46:35.390
It's almost too close
to you to notice.

00:46:35.390 --> 00:46:37.790
If it's at all hard
to notice, it's

00:46:37.790 --> 00:46:39.980
because it's so close,
rather than it's

00:46:39.980 --> 00:46:42.180
deep within or far away.

00:46:42.180 --> 00:46:44.973
And there are other
analogies like--

00:46:44.973 --> 00:46:45.890
I don't even remember.

00:46:45.890 --> 00:46:47.810
There's Mind's Eye,
pieces of artwork,

00:46:47.810 --> 00:46:50.430
that were the random
dot stereo grams, where

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:52.280
we have an image that pops out.

00:46:52.280 --> 00:46:54.530
I always find it very difficult
to see those because I

00:46:54.530 --> 00:46:56.390
have a very dominant eye.

00:46:56.390 --> 00:46:57.440
But some people can see.

00:46:57.440 --> 00:46:59.107
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
People can't see those.

00:46:59.107 --> 00:47:01.468
These images that used to
be at the touristy shops.

00:47:01.468 --> 00:47:03.260
But people say, oh,
there it is, the whale.

00:47:03.260 --> 00:47:06.110
And I'm thinking,
I don't see it.

00:47:06.110 --> 00:47:08.750
Kids that swim a lot
when they're younger,

00:47:08.750 --> 00:47:11.202
and they tend to breathe
just to one side--

00:47:11.202 --> 00:47:12.410
I don't know if this was you.

00:47:12.410 --> 00:47:13.368
This was definitely me.

00:47:13.368 --> 00:47:16.220
They tend to keep
one eye closed.

00:47:16.220 --> 00:47:18.740
You set up a pretty
strong ocular dominance.

00:47:18.740 --> 00:47:21.422
Biasing your vision to one or
the other eye early in life,

00:47:21.422 --> 00:47:23.630
whether or not you're learning
how to be a bow hunter

00:47:23.630 --> 00:47:26.120
or you're learning how
to throw darts or shoot

00:47:26.120 --> 00:47:30.253
billiards or anything that,
involves selectively viewing

00:47:30.253 --> 00:47:32.420
the world through one eye
for even a couple of hours

00:47:32.420 --> 00:47:39.185
can set up a permanent
asymmetry in the weighting

00:47:39.185 --> 00:47:41.810
flow of visual information
from the eye to the brain.

00:47:41.810 --> 00:47:44.480
It's reversible, but only
through the reverse gymnastics

00:47:44.480 --> 00:47:46.430
of covering up the
other eye intentionally.

00:47:46.430 --> 00:47:48.710
So actually I had to be
reversed-patched for a while

00:47:48.710 --> 00:47:49.850
because I was seeing
double, because I

00:47:49.850 --> 00:47:50.900
lost binocular vision.

00:47:50.900 --> 00:47:56.210
I don't stand a chance in hell
of seeing an image pop out

00:47:56.210 --> 00:47:57.980
of a random dot
stereogram, which

00:47:57.980 --> 00:48:01.190
is kind of ironic because I did
my PhD on binocular circuitry.

00:48:01.190 --> 00:48:06.820
But nonetheless, if
people can see these,

00:48:06.820 --> 00:48:09.293
or if they can't, I
think they provide

00:48:09.293 --> 00:48:10.960
a really terrific
example of what you're

00:48:10.960 --> 00:48:14.237
talking about as a larger theme,
which is that perceptually you

00:48:14.237 --> 00:48:15.070
see a bunch of dots.

00:48:15.070 --> 00:48:18.800
And then all of a sudden,
what you thought wasn't there

00:48:18.800 --> 00:48:21.460
is suddenly there, but
can just disappear again.

00:48:21.460 --> 00:48:23.050
There are certain
visual illusions,

00:48:23.050 --> 00:48:25.510
if we were to include others,
that once you see them,

00:48:25.510 --> 00:48:27.010
you cannot unsee them.

00:48:27.010 --> 00:48:31.960
So there's the faces-vases,
figure-ground type stuff.

00:48:31.960 --> 00:48:33.400
SAM HARRIS: Bi-stable percepts.

00:48:33.400 --> 00:48:34.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Bi-stable percepts.

00:48:34.900 --> 00:48:37.420
And then there's sort
of ocular competition.

00:48:37.420 --> 00:48:40.480
You show two different images to
the eyes, each of the two eyes.

00:48:40.480 --> 00:48:43.180
And it is near
impossible for people

00:48:43.180 --> 00:48:45.662
to perceive them
both simultaneously.

00:48:45.662 --> 00:48:47.620
So it's a little bit of
what you're describing.

00:48:47.620 --> 00:48:49.180
I mean, these seem to
be fundamental features

00:48:49.180 --> 00:48:51.370
about the way the neural
circuits are organized,

00:48:51.370 --> 00:48:55.600
that they don't want to stay
fixated on any one thing

00:48:55.600 --> 00:48:56.260
for very long.

00:48:56.260 --> 00:48:59.800
To do so either takes training,
intense interest, intense fear,

00:48:59.800 --> 00:49:01.120
intense excitement.

00:49:01.120 --> 00:49:03.370
When I say intense, I guess,
I come back to this idea

00:49:03.370 --> 00:49:05.203
that the autonomic
nervous system is somehow

00:49:05.203 --> 00:49:09.190
governing our ability to
spotlight at any one location

00:49:09.190 --> 00:49:10.450
for very long.

00:49:10.450 --> 00:49:11.930
Is that a useful framework?

00:49:11.930 --> 00:49:13.610
Or is that going to take
us down a different path?

00:49:13.610 --> 00:49:15.985
SAM HARRIS: Well, it's sort
of a different path for this.

00:49:15.985 --> 00:49:17.980
I mean, the only
point I was making

00:49:17.980 --> 00:49:21.010
is that the
seemingly paradoxical

00:49:21.010 --> 00:49:24.250
claim that something can
be right on the surface

00:49:24.250 --> 00:49:25.390
and yet hard to see.

00:49:29.800 --> 00:49:34.780
And again, this seems to
justify the expectation held

00:49:34.780 --> 00:49:36.880
by, I would think,
the vast majority

00:49:36.880 --> 00:49:40.750
of people who get interested
in these spiritual things,

00:49:40.750 --> 00:49:45.340
for lack of a better word,
that the truth must somehow

00:49:45.340 --> 00:49:48.130
be deep within.

00:49:48.130 --> 00:49:52.930
There's some distance between
the one who is looking

00:49:52.930 --> 00:49:55.060
and the thing that
has to be found right

00:49:55.060 --> 00:49:58.357
and that you have to go through
this long evolution of changes.

00:49:58.357 --> 00:50:00.440
I mean, there are many
metaphors that set this up.

00:50:00.440 --> 00:50:02.232
It's like you're at
the base of a mountain,

00:50:02.232 --> 00:50:04.100
and you have to
climb to the top.

00:50:04.100 --> 00:50:06.200
And so you have
to find the path,

00:50:06.200 --> 00:50:10.240
however secure it
is to get you there.

00:50:10.240 --> 00:50:14.110
But there really is a distance
between your starting point

00:50:14.110 --> 00:50:15.050
and the goal.

00:50:15.050 --> 00:50:17.290
And what I'm arguing--

00:50:17.290 --> 00:50:23.830
and this is a kind of a nondual,
to use a term of jargon--

00:50:23.830 --> 00:50:27.040
this is a nondual
approach to meditation,

00:50:27.040 --> 00:50:29.440
as opposed to a dualistic one.

00:50:29.440 --> 00:50:34.420
The path and the
goal are coincident,

00:50:34.420 --> 00:50:39.070
that you have to unravel the
logic by which you would seek

00:50:39.070 --> 00:50:42.970
something that's outside
of the present moment's

00:50:42.970 --> 00:50:47.590
experience, i.e. not available,
really not available to you

00:50:47.590 --> 00:50:49.450
now.

00:50:49.450 --> 00:50:53.960
Because so many things
worth having, so many skills

00:50:53.960 --> 00:50:57.230
worth acquiring, really are
not available to you now.

00:50:57.230 --> 00:50:59.460
It's like if you
want to be a pianist,

00:50:59.460 --> 00:51:02.420
or if you want to
speak Chinese, there's

00:51:02.420 --> 00:51:03.807
something you don't know.

00:51:03.807 --> 00:51:05.390
And then you want
to learn that thing.

00:51:05.390 --> 00:51:07.070
And there's a whole process.

00:51:07.070 --> 00:51:10.280
And you might not be
capable of doing it.

00:51:10.280 --> 00:51:13.070
And real mastery is far away.

00:51:13.070 --> 00:51:15.410
If you've never hit
a golf ball, and you

00:51:15.410 --> 00:51:18.747
want to hit a golf ball
300 yards straight,

00:51:18.747 --> 00:51:20.330
I can pretty much
guarantee you're not

00:51:20.330 --> 00:51:21.710
going to do that initially.

00:51:21.710 --> 00:51:24.020
And you're not going
to do it on day two.

00:51:24.020 --> 00:51:27.230
And you're not going to do it
reliably for the longest time.

00:51:27.230 --> 00:51:30.260
And there's real
training in front of you

00:51:30.260 --> 00:51:34.260
to be able to do that reliably.

00:51:34.260 --> 00:51:38.430
An insight into, and
really the core insight,

00:51:38.430 --> 00:51:41.880
I mean, the insight that is the
core of the Buddha's teaching,

00:51:41.880 --> 00:51:47.440
to take one historical
example of this,

00:51:47.440 --> 00:51:50.155
really is available now.

00:51:53.080 --> 00:51:55.880
I mean, granted, it can be
very hard, one, for people.

00:51:55.880 --> 00:51:58.390
I mean, I had
probably spent a year

00:51:58.390 --> 00:52:02.560
on silent retreat in one week
to three month increments

00:52:02.560 --> 00:52:08.690
before I got the
point I'm making now.

00:52:08.690 --> 00:52:11.390
I mean, literally,
these are retreats

00:52:11.390 --> 00:52:15.590
where you spend 12 to 18
hours a day just meditating,

00:52:15.590 --> 00:52:23.300
trying to unpack the kinds
of claims I'm making now.

00:52:23.300 --> 00:52:27.780
So it's possible to
rigorously overlook this.

00:52:27.780 --> 00:52:30.800
It's possible to stand in
front of the mind's eye image

00:52:30.800 --> 00:52:33.860
and stare in a way
that is guaranteed

00:52:33.860 --> 00:52:36.530
not to give you
pop out and to be

00:52:36.530 --> 00:52:39.840
adept at staring in that way.

00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:41.420
So it's possible to be misled.

00:52:41.420 --> 00:52:43.100
And so what I'm
trying to argue here

00:52:43.100 --> 00:52:46.360
is that there's a fair
amount of leverage

00:52:46.360 --> 00:52:49.030
you can get with better
information, which

00:52:49.030 --> 00:52:52.000
can cut the time course of
your searching for this thing

00:52:52.000 --> 00:52:54.760
and cancel your false
expectations about just

00:52:54.760 --> 00:52:57.640
where this is in relation
to your ordinary waking

00:52:57.640 --> 00:52:59.650
consciousness.

00:52:59.650 --> 00:53:02.500
And it's possible to
get bad information

00:53:02.500 --> 00:53:04.370
and to have a bunch
of experiences.

00:53:04.370 --> 00:53:07.300
You go you go and do
an ayahuasca trip.

00:53:07.300 --> 00:53:09.310
And it's incredibly valuable.

00:53:09.310 --> 00:53:10.930
And it's valuable
for all the ways

00:53:10.930 --> 00:53:13.240
in which it changed the
contents of your consciousness

00:53:13.240 --> 00:53:14.980
in startling ways.

00:53:14.980 --> 00:53:16.720
And you had insights
into your past

00:53:16.720 --> 00:53:18.880
and into your relationships
and into why you're

00:53:18.880 --> 00:53:20.380
not as loving as you might be.

00:53:20.380 --> 00:53:21.850
And there's lots to think about.

00:53:21.850 --> 00:53:23.440
And you're like, OK,
that's all great.

00:53:23.440 --> 00:53:26.260
That's all something
that we can talk about.

00:53:26.260 --> 00:53:30.130
But it truly is orthogonal.

00:53:30.130 --> 00:53:31.630
I mean, it makes a
point of contact,

00:53:31.630 --> 00:53:34.360
or what I'm talking about,
it's really just at one point.

00:53:34.360 --> 00:53:38.370
And it's at the point
where this sense

00:53:38.370 --> 00:53:41.370
of subject-object
division in consciousness

00:53:41.370 --> 00:53:44.920
is illusory and vulnerable
to investigation.

00:53:44.920 --> 00:53:49.260
And if you investigate it as
the right plane of focus--

00:53:49.260 --> 00:53:53.430
you pick the analogy you
want, whether it's setting up

00:53:53.430 --> 00:53:58.080
the optic blind spot experiment
in just the right way,

00:53:58.080 --> 00:54:04.020
so that you can see that
the data is not there.

00:54:04.020 --> 00:54:08.760
I mean, the bi-stable percept is
great because when you see one

00:54:08.760 --> 00:54:12.780
of these images, like
the vase-face diagram,

00:54:12.780 --> 00:54:16.200
or the Dalmatian that it
looks like a mess of dots,

00:54:16.200 --> 00:54:20.040
and then you see the image
of a Dalmatian dog pop out--

00:54:20.040 --> 00:54:23.440
once you see it, you
really can't unsee it.

00:54:23.440 --> 00:54:27.940
I mean, once you have the
requisite conceptual anchor

00:54:27.940 --> 00:54:31.510
to it, then every time you look,
you're going to find it again.

00:54:31.510 --> 00:54:33.640
And eventually, it
becomes effortless.

00:54:33.640 --> 00:54:36.160
And that's what,
ultimately, meditation is,

00:54:36.160 --> 00:54:37.720
I mean, this kind of meditation.

00:54:37.720 --> 00:54:41.770
You ultimately
learn to recognize

00:54:41.770 --> 00:54:44.590
that there's no
separation between you

00:54:44.590 --> 00:54:45.910
and your experience.

00:54:45.910 --> 00:54:48.160
There's not the
experience on the one hand

00:54:48.160 --> 00:54:50.500
and the self on the other.

00:54:50.500 --> 00:54:52.540
There's just experience.

00:54:52.540 --> 00:54:55.000
There's just seeing, hearing,
smelling, tasting, touching,

00:54:55.000 --> 00:54:57.130
thinking, feeling,
proprioception,

00:54:57.130 --> 00:55:01.480
add whatever channels of
information you want to that.

00:55:01.480 --> 00:55:08.070
But there's just the totality
of the energy of consciousness

00:55:08.070 --> 00:55:08.910
and its contents.

00:55:08.910 --> 00:55:13.830
And it's not that
you're on the riverbank.

00:55:13.830 --> 00:55:16.020
And this is how it can
seem in the beginning,

00:55:16.020 --> 00:55:19.080
even when you're practicing
meditation fairly diligently.

00:55:19.080 --> 00:55:21.000
You can seem like
you're on the riverbank,

00:55:21.000 --> 00:55:23.610
watching the contents of
consciousness flow by.

00:55:23.610 --> 00:55:28.590
And meditation is the act
of doing that more and more

00:55:28.590 --> 00:55:31.648
dispassionately, so you're no
longer grabbing at the pleasant

00:55:31.648 --> 00:55:32.940
or pushing the unpleasant away.

00:55:32.940 --> 00:55:37.650
You're just kind of relaxing in
the most non-judgmental frame

00:55:37.650 --> 00:55:41.730
of mind, just
witnessing the flow.

00:55:41.730 --> 00:55:44.820
But if you're doing
that dualisticly,

00:55:44.820 --> 00:55:46.320
you feel like the meditator.

00:55:46.320 --> 00:55:48.580
You feel like the
subject aiming attention.

00:55:48.580 --> 00:55:50.070
And so now you're
on the riverbank

00:55:50.070 --> 00:55:51.960
watching everything go past.

00:55:51.960 --> 00:55:56.220
But the truth is,
you are the river.

00:55:56.220 --> 00:55:59.520
Experience itself is that there
is just experience itself.

00:55:59.520 --> 00:56:01.950
You're not on the
edge of experience.

00:56:01.950 --> 00:56:07.020
And everything you can
notice is part of the flow.

00:56:07.020 --> 00:56:11.040
And there's no point from
which to abstract yourself away

00:56:11.040 --> 00:56:13.950
from the flow to
stand outside it

00:56:13.950 --> 00:56:16.450
and to say, OK, this is my life.

00:56:16.450 --> 00:56:17.560
This is my experience.

00:56:17.560 --> 00:56:18.540
This is my body.

00:56:18.540 --> 00:56:19.530
Yes, you can do that.

00:56:19.530 --> 00:56:21.030
I mean, those are
all just thoughts.

00:56:21.030 --> 00:56:22.650
But that's more of the flow.

00:56:22.650 --> 00:56:26.790
And so there's a process by
which you would eventually

00:56:26.790 --> 00:56:30.660
recognize that there's
no distance between you

00:56:30.660 --> 00:56:31.660
and your experience.

00:56:31.660 --> 00:56:34.200
And again, you can wait
for those moments in life

00:56:34.200 --> 00:56:38.280
where experience gets so
good, or so terrifying,

00:56:38.280 --> 00:56:40.560
it's just so salient.

00:56:40.560 --> 00:56:42.660
Your amygdala is
driving so hard.

00:56:42.660 --> 00:56:44.670
I mean, so you're
in a war, and you

00:56:44.670 --> 00:56:47.130
can't think about anything
because the enemy is

00:56:47.130 --> 00:56:47.850
shooting at you.

00:56:47.850 --> 00:56:49.558
And this is the most
thrilling video game

00:56:49.558 --> 00:56:50.970
you've ever played in your life.

00:56:50.970 --> 00:56:52.410
And your life is on the line.

00:56:52.410 --> 00:56:57.270
Or you're at the peak
of some athletic event,

00:56:57.270 --> 00:57:01.320
where you don't know how you're
doing the things you're doing,

00:57:01.320 --> 00:57:04.770
but it's all happening
automatically.

00:57:04.770 --> 00:57:11.310
But those are 1/100
of 1% of one's life.

00:57:11.310 --> 00:57:14.240
And you know what I'm
calling meditation

00:57:14.240 --> 00:57:19.380
is a way of simply understanding
the mechanics of a tension

00:57:19.380 --> 00:57:23.550
whereby you are denying yourself
that unity of experience

00:57:23.550 --> 00:57:27.360
so much of the time and
recognizing that it's

00:57:27.360 --> 00:57:31.290
based on a misperception
of the way consciousness

00:57:31.290 --> 00:57:32.105
always already is.

00:57:32.105 --> 00:57:34.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, if
there wasn't an incentive

00:57:34.230 --> 00:57:40.630
to learn how to meditate
properly, that was one.

00:57:40.630 --> 00:57:43.240
And I've been meditating
for a fair amount

00:57:43.240 --> 00:57:46.450
since I was in my teens, but
more along the lines of paying

00:57:46.450 --> 00:57:53.260
attention to breath and
open-observer type meditation,

00:57:53.260 --> 00:57:54.285
or focused-attention.

00:57:54.285 --> 00:57:56.410
I would suppose more of
the focused-attention type.

00:57:56.410 --> 00:57:57.610
We'll get into these
a little bit later.

00:57:57.610 --> 00:58:00.235
But I have a number of questions
related to what you just said.

00:58:00.235 --> 00:58:02.350
SAM HARRIS: Sure.

00:58:02.350 --> 00:58:07.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love the idea
that this thing that we would

00:58:07.690 --> 00:58:12.430
all do well to understand to
observe consciousness of self

00:58:12.430 --> 00:58:17.500
as opposed to trying to alter
the contents of consciousness

00:58:17.500 --> 00:58:23.350
may sit much closer to
us than one might think.

00:58:23.350 --> 00:58:26.220
And because it sits
so close to us,

00:58:26.220 --> 00:58:28.500
that might be one of the
reasons why we miss it.

00:58:28.500 --> 00:58:30.810
I go right to a
visual system example.

00:58:30.810 --> 00:58:33.580
I mean, if you're
wearing corrective lenses

00:58:33.580 --> 00:58:35.130
and there's a
speck on your lens,

00:58:35.130 --> 00:58:37.047
typically you're looking
out through the lens,

00:58:37.047 --> 00:58:38.910
and so you wouldn't
observe that speck.

00:58:38.910 --> 00:58:41.220
Any number of different
analogies could work here.

00:58:41.220 --> 00:58:45.210
The fact that there
are states, however

00:58:45.210 --> 00:58:50.910
few, positive and negative,
extreme ecstasy and extreme

00:58:50.910 --> 00:58:53.850
fear being the two, I
think, most obvious ones

00:58:53.850 --> 00:58:55.290
that seems like
we agree on, that

00:58:55.290 --> 00:58:59.280
allow us to capture the
sense of completeness of self

00:58:59.280 --> 00:59:02.625
or the unity of the
observer and the actor.

00:59:05.330 --> 00:59:08.810
The fact that those are
seldom for the nontrained,

00:59:08.810 --> 00:59:12.440
for the nonmeditator,
suggests to me two things.

00:59:12.440 --> 00:59:15.560
I think one perhaps worth
exploring more than the other.

00:59:15.560 --> 00:59:20.330
But one is that what's really
being revealed in the states

00:59:20.330 --> 00:59:22.280
where we can feel the
unity of the observer

00:59:22.280 --> 00:59:25.670
and the actor is understanding
something fundamental

00:59:25.670 --> 00:59:28.535
about the algorithm, not
the online algorithm,

00:59:28.535 --> 00:59:30.410
but the algorithm that
is our nervous system.

00:59:30.410 --> 00:59:32.240
Just as you mentioned
cephalopods.

00:59:32.240 --> 00:59:35.750
I mean, mantis shrimp see an
enormous array of color hues

00:59:35.750 --> 00:59:38.150
that we don't.

00:59:38.150 --> 00:59:39.950
Their maps and
representations of the world

00:59:39.950 --> 00:59:41.117
are fundamentally different.

00:59:41.117 --> 00:59:43.380
Pit vipers see in the infrared.

00:59:43.380 --> 00:59:45.650
We're restricted to
somewhat of a limited range

00:59:45.650 --> 00:59:47.600
within the color
spectrum, but still more

00:59:47.600 --> 00:59:50.330
vast than that of dogs or cats.

00:59:50.330 --> 00:59:54.040
So understanding
that for seeing what

00:59:54.040 --> 00:59:58.700
a pit viper can see for
moments would be informative.

00:59:58.700 --> 01:00:04.097
Perhaps sensing-heat emissions
as a human might be invasive,

01:00:04.097 --> 01:00:05.430
maybe that's why we don't do it.

01:00:05.430 --> 01:00:11.100
So the question is, to just
make it straightforward,

01:00:11.100 --> 01:00:14.070
why would the system
be designed this way?

01:00:14.070 --> 01:00:16.472
Again, neither of us were
consulted in the design phase.

01:00:16.472 --> 01:00:18.930
But that brings me to, perhaps,
the more tractable question

01:00:18.930 --> 01:00:22.290
was, which is about development.

01:00:22.290 --> 01:00:27.510
I'm a great believer that the
neural circuits that encouraged

01:00:27.510 --> 01:00:30.450
healthy parent-child relations,
or unhealthy parent-child

01:00:30.450 --> 01:00:32.820
relations as the case
may be, in childhood stem

01:00:32.820 --> 01:00:35.282
from the initial
demands of internal

01:00:35.282 --> 01:00:37.740
versus external states, which
is exactly what we're talking

01:00:37.740 --> 01:00:40.590
about, which is that a young
child feels anxious because it

01:00:40.590 --> 01:00:41.590
needs his diaper change.

01:00:41.590 --> 01:00:44.550
It doesn't really know it needs
its diaper changed or it's cold

01:00:44.550 --> 01:00:47.760
or it's uncomfortable or it's
hungry or it's overly full.

01:00:47.760 --> 01:00:49.052
And so it vocalizes.

01:00:49.052 --> 01:00:50.760
And then some external
source comes to us

01:00:50.760 --> 01:00:52.552
and relieves that hopefully.

01:00:52.552 --> 01:00:54.510
And so the fundamental
rule that we first learn

01:00:54.510 --> 01:00:58.980
is not that we have a self or
that things fall down, not up.

01:00:58.980 --> 01:01:03.150
But it's that when
uncomfortable,

01:01:03.150 --> 01:01:04.980
externalize that discomfort.

01:01:04.980 --> 01:01:06.890
And it will be relieved
by an outside player.

01:01:06.890 --> 01:01:09.390
And then, of course, there's a
repurposing of that circuitry

01:01:09.390 --> 01:01:10.800
for adult romantic attachments.

01:01:10.800 --> 01:01:12.630
I don't think
anyone doubts that.

01:01:12.630 --> 01:01:15.160
And that can explain a lot
indeed about attachment

01:01:15.160 --> 01:01:17.230
and so forth.

01:01:17.230 --> 01:01:20.400
So something about our
developmental wiring

01:01:20.400 --> 01:01:23.970
and the algorithms that
these neural circuits run

01:01:23.970 --> 01:01:28.140
tend to bias most people,
the nonpractice meditators,

01:01:28.140 --> 01:01:34.920
to live a somewhat
functional life at least

01:01:34.920 --> 01:01:38.640
without this awareness
of actor and observer.

01:01:38.640 --> 01:01:40.650
And so what you're
really talking about

01:01:40.650 --> 01:01:46.590
is a deliberate intervention
to understand and resolve

01:01:46.590 --> 01:01:48.775
that gap in the algorithm.

01:01:48.775 --> 01:01:49.650
Do I have that right?

01:01:49.650 --> 01:01:49.990
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

01:01:49.990 --> 01:01:50.580
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I'm more or less

01:01:50.580 --> 01:01:52.800
restating what you said
in a way that, I'm hoping,

01:01:52.800 --> 01:01:54.840
will serve as a
jumping off point.

01:01:54.840 --> 01:01:58.200
Why questions are always very
dangerous in biology, or any.

01:02:00.568 --> 01:02:01.860
SAM HARRIS: Or in relationship.

01:02:01.860 --> 01:02:02.370
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What's that?

01:02:02.370 --> 01:02:03.180
SAM HARRIS: Or in relationship.

01:02:03.180 --> 01:02:03.930
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Or in relationship.

01:02:03.930 --> 01:02:04.430
Right.

01:02:04.430 --> 01:02:05.038
Exactly.

01:02:05.038 --> 01:02:07.080
Although, I think it all
does really hearken back

01:02:07.080 --> 01:02:08.610
to this early
developmental wiring which,

01:02:08.610 --> 01:02:09.652
of course, is modifiable.

01:02:09.652 --> 01:02:12.420
That's the beauty of
the nervous system is,

01:02:12.420 --> 01:02:14.910
it's the one organ that seems
to be able to change itself

01:02:14.910 --> 01:02:16.170
at least to some degree.

01:02:18.710 --> 01:02:21.080
So what are your thoughts
about the organization

01:02:21.080 --> 01:02:24.890
of the circuitry to essentially
under normal conditions to not

01:02:24.890 --> 01:02:28.760
reveal what seems to be one of
its more important and profound

01:02:28.760 --> 01:02:33.920
and, for dare I say,
enlightening features.

01:02:33.920 --> 01:02:37.160
It's almost as if we are
potentially like mantis shrimp.

01:02:37.160 --> 01:02:41.180
We can see so many more
colors than we actually see.

01:02:41.180 --> 01:02:44.540
And yet, we don't.

01:02:44.540 --> 01:02:46.680
Most people opt not to.

01:02:46.680 --> 01:02:50.570
And I would argue that
one of the great strengths

01:02:50.570 --> 01:02:52.490
of the Waking Up
app, for instance,

01:02:52.490 --> 01:02:55.670
is that it essentially walks
you through the process of being

01:02:55.670 --> 01:02:58.160
able to arrive at these
things without having

01:02:58.160 --> 01:03:02.750
to go to one-year or three-year
long silent meditation

01:03:02.750 --> 01:03:03.350
retreats.

01:03:03.350 --> 01:03:06.080
So if you could just elaborate
for a moment before we move on.

01:03:06.080 --> 01:03:08.180
What are your thoughts
about how the circuitry is

01:03:08.180 --> 01:03:10.640
arranged by default versus--

01:03:10.640 --> 01:03:15.500
and what that means for there
to be an intervention, that we

01:03:15.500 --> 01:03:18.710
have to intervene in the self
in order to reveal the self.

01:03:18.710 --> 01:03:21.603
SAM HARRIS: Well, so there
are two big questions there,

01:03:21.603 --> 01:03:23.520
one about evolution and
one about development.

01:03:23.520 --> 01:03:27.620
So with respect
to evolution, it's

01:03:27.620 --> 01:03:31.280
important to recognize
that evolution doesn't

01:03:31.280 --> 01:03:35.060
see our deepest concerns
about human flourishing

01:03:35.060 --> 01:03:36.080
and human well-being.

01:03:36.080 --> 01:03:38.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
all about the offspring.

01:03:38.480 --> 01:03:42.500
SAM HARRIS: We are set up
to spawn and to survive

01:03:42.500 --> 01:03:47.210
long enough to help our progeny
spawn if we can do that.

01:03:47.210 --> 01:03:48.920
And that's it.

01:03:48.920 --> 01:03:53.180
And so anything that was
good for that, including

01:03:53.180 --> 01:04:01.100
tribalism and xenophobia and all
kinds of hardware and software

01:04:01.100 --> 01:04:04.602
flaws that revealed
themselves to be flaws

01:04:04.602 --> 01:04:06.560
in the present time, when
we're trying to build

01:04:06.560 --> 01:04:08.780
a viable global civilization.

01:04:08.780 --> 01:04:12.470
But they redounded to the
advantage of our ancestors

01:04:12.470 --> 01:04:16.760
somehow, or there are things
about us that were simply not

01:04:16.760 --> 01:04:18.320
selected for, they
just came along

01:04:18.320 --> 01:04:23.630
for the ride, what Stephen
Jay Gould called a spandrel.

01:04:23.630 --> 01:04:28.310
So we are not set
up by evolution

01:04:28.310 --> 01:04:32.990
to be as happy as
we possibly can be

01:04:32.990 --> 01:04:37.010
and to do almost anything
that interests us well.

01:04:37.010 --> 01:04:38.750
I mean, we're not
set up by evolution

01:04:38.750 --> 01:04:41.660
to be mathematicians
or musicians

01:04:41.660 --> 01:04:45.080
or to create democracies
that are healthy.

01:04:45.080 --> 01:04:47.780
I mean, evolution
can see none of this.

01:04:47.780 --> 01:04:51.170
And we are doing
these things based

01:04:51.170 --> 01:04:55.460
on cognitive and
emotional hardware

01:04:55.460 --> 01:04:58.490
that we are leveraging
in new directions.

01:04:58.490 --> 01:05:00.320
I mean, we are primates.

01:05:00.320 --> 01:05:03.807
And we're communicating
with small mouth noises.

01:05:03.807 --> 01:05:05.390
I mean, we're
language-using primates.

01:05:05.390 --> 01:05:06.848
And all of that is
clearly evolved.

01:05:09.360 --> 01:05:12.440
And we're doing these amazing
things, including science.

01:05:12.440 --> 01:05:14.960
However improbably,
where we're actually

01:05:14.960 --> 01:05:20.750
able to, almost
entirely with language,

01:05:20.750 --> 01:05:24.200
understand reality
that at a scale

01:05:24.200 --> 01:05:26.120
that exceeds us in
both directions,

01:05:26.120 --> 01:05:29.420
I mean, the very vast and
the very small, and also

01:05:29.420 --> 01:05:31.430
temporally, the very old.

01:05:31.430 --> 01:05:33.560
We have visions
of the far future.

01:05:33.560 --> 01:05:36.140
We can figure out
where an asteroid

01:05:36.140 --> 01:05:40.010
is going to cross Earth's
orbit 1,000 years from now

01:05:40.010 --> 01:05:41.090
if we just do the math.

01:05:41.090 --> 01:05:43.257
And it's amazing that we
can do all of those things,

01:05:43.257 --> 01:05:45.980
but evolution is
blind to all of that.

01:05:45.980 --> 01:05:51.580
And so in terms of what we care
about and certainly in terms

01:05:51.580 --> 01:05:55.120
of what's going to ensure
our survival as a species,

01:05:55.120 --> 01:05:58.570
we have flown the perch that
was created for us by evolution.

01:05:58.570 --> 01:06:01.700
I mean, it's not just
the primate things.

01:06:01.700 --> 01:06:06.220
And so it is with learning
how to regulate our emotions

01:06:06.220 --> 01:06:10.090
and punch through
to a self concept

01:06:10.090 --> 01:06:12.520
or beyond a self
concept that is more

01:06:12.520 --> 01:06:18.100
normative, psychologically, that
allows us to not be terrorized

01:06:18.100 --> 01:06:22.780
by our ape-ish genes as
fully as we seem to be,

01:06:22.780 --> 01:06:26.350
even in the presence of more
and more destructive technology.

01:06:26.350 --> 01:06:30.850
I mean, we're still
practically chimpanzees armed

01:06:30.850 --> 01:06:31.990
with nuclear weapons.

01:06:31.990 --> 01:06:34.600
And that is increasingly
dysfunctional.

01:06:34.600 --> 01:06:38.890
And very soon we're going to
be in the presence of minds,

01:06:38.890 --> 01:06:41.020
or apparent minds,
that we have built

01:06:41.020 --> 01:06:43.750
that are as
intelligent as we are

01:06:43.750 --> 01:06:46.990
and very quickly, probably 15
minutes after that, far more

01:06:46.990 --> 01:06:48.140
intelligent than we are.

01:06:48.140 --> 01:06:51.640
And so what we do with all
of that is, again, something

01:06:51.640 --> 01:06:57.190
that we have to figure out
based on the minds we have,

01:06:57.190 --> 01:07:01.000
the minds we can build,
the minds we can change.

01:07:01.000 --> 01:07:02.950
We can meddle with
our own genomes now.

01:07:02.950 --> 01:07:07.150
And that will produce
its own consequences

01:07:07.150 --> 01:07:10.660
in ourselves and in
future generations

01:07:10.660 --> 01:07:12.280
if we meddle with the germline.

01:07:12.280 --> 01:07:16.450
And again, all of that
is just evolution.

01:07:16.450 --> 01:07:18.310
It's just sort of the
womb we came out of,

01:07:18.310 --> 01:07:22.930
but it didn't
anticipate any of that.

01:07:22.930 --> 01:07:26.500
Mother nature simply not had
our best interests at heart.

01:07:26.500 --> 01:07:29.980
And we might die off, and
from the point of view

01:07:29.980 --> 01:07:34.180
of mother nature that's fine
because 99% of every species

01:07:34.180 --> 01:07:34.750
dies off.

01:07:38.000 --> 01:07:39.750
So there's that.

01:07:39.750 --> 01:07:43.340
But when you're talking
about the individual,

01:07:43.340 --> 01:07:45.710
developmentally-- we all
come into this world,

01:07:45.710 --> 01:07:50.300
again, as a fairly
hairless primate

01:07:50.300 --> 01:07:52.820
that needs a tremendous
amount of care by others.

01:07:52.820 --> 01:08:00.320
And the logic of that is
that the reason why we're not

01:08:00.320 --> 01:08:03.830
a gazelle that can run 45
minutes later and then,

01:08:03.830 --> 01:08:07.910
basically, do all the
gazelle things perfectly soon

01:08:07.910 --> 01:08:10.220
thereafter.

01:08:10.220 --> 01:08:17.300
The reason why we have
this time of immaturity,

01:08:17.300 --> 01:08:19.340
and has become
functional for us,

01:08:19.340 --> 01:08:23.060
is that we're far
more flexible, and we

01:08:23.060 --> 01:08:28.279
can learn based on the needs
of an environment to do so much

01:08:28.279 --> 01:08:29.810
more than a gazelle can.

01:08:29.810 --> 01:08:32.359
And language is part of that.

01:08:32.359 --> 01:08:38.020
And in the last
10,000 years or so,

01:08:38.020 --> 01:08:40.810
culture increasingly has been
more and more a part of that.

01:08:40.810 --> 01:08:43.479
And there's probably a layer
at which we can plausibly

01:08:43.479 --> 01:08:46.450
talk about cultural evolution
and cultural evolution

01:08:46.450 --> 01:08:50.245
interacting with biological
evolution to change us.

01:08:50.245 --> 01:08:52.120
But when you're talking
about the development

01:08:52.120 --> 01:08:58.390
of an individual, each of us
comes into this world, I think,

01:08:58.390 --> 01:09:02.140
not recognizing
ourselves in any sense

01:09:02.140 --> 01:09:06.937
that would make sense to reify.

01:09:06.937 --> 01:09:08.770
I mean, it's not that
there's nothing there.

01:09:08.770 --> 01:09:11.680
I mean, there could be some kind
of proto-self differentiation.

01:09:11.680 --> 01:09:15.399
But I think it
takes a long while.

01:09:15.399 --> 01:09:19.000
And there is very
likely a coincidence

01:09:19.000 --> 01:09:23.260
between really
recognizing others.

01:09:23.260 --> 01:09:25.240
We recognize others first.

01:09:25.240 --> 01:09:26.950
certainly in
relationship immediately.

01:09:26.950 --> 01:09:28.600
And we orient to human faces.

01:09:28.600 --> 01:09:34.743
And we even detect other humans
as good and bad moral actors

01:09:34.743 --> 01:09:36.160
very early, I mean,
certainly long

01:09:36.160 --> 01:09:40.569
before we recognize
ourselves in a mirror.

01:09:40.569 --> 01:09:44.710
The experiments run, again, this
is Paul Blume and colleagues,

01:09:44.710 --> 01:09:49.120
experiments run on the
moral hardware and software

01:09:49.120 --> 01:09:51.723
of developing toddlers.

01:09:51.723 --> 01:09:53.140
But I think at
this point, they've

01:09:53.140 --> 01:09:55.930
pushed it down all the
way to six months of age,

01:09:55.930 --> 01:09:59.380
where you'll get these infants
staring at a puppet show.

01:09:59.380 --> 01:10:04.490
And they'll show
a greater interest

01:10:04.490 --> 01:10:08.680
in classically good actors
versus bad actors, cooperators

01:10:08.680 --> 01:10:12.550
versus defectors, in
various puppet show games.

01:10:15.080 --> 01:10:21.080
So it's not that we have no
mind and no proto-awareness

01:10:21.080 --> 01:10:25.580
of others and of self, but
what eventually happens,

01:10:25.580 --> 01:10:30.980
certainly as we become at
all facile with language use,

01:10:30.980 --> 01:10:35.330
is that we become aware
that not only are we

01:10:35.330 --> 01:10:38.240
in relationship to others,
but we are an object

01:10:38.240 --> 01:10:40.250
in the world for them.

01:10:40.250 --> 01:10:44.870
So we have enough people
pointing at us in our cribs

01:10:44.870 --> 01:10:47.990
and impinging upon
our experience.

01:10:47.990 --> 01:10:51.530
You're being physically moved,
and prodded, and touched,

01:10:51.530 --> 01:10:54.080
and consoled, or not consoled.

01:10:54.080 --> 01:10:56.540
And just imagine
what all of these--

01:10:56.540 --> 01:11:00.770
you're on the receiving end
of 10,000 interventions.

01:11:00.770 --> 01:11:04.190
And you're completely
helpless for the longest time.

01:11:04.190 --> 01:11:06.380
And all of that
attention, you have

01:11:06.380 --> 01:11:08.870
all of these people
coming up to the crib

01:11:08.870 --> 01:11:10.183
and making faces at you--

01:11:10.183 --> 01:11:11.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Cheering for you.

01:11:11.600 --> 01:11:14.150
SAM HARRIS: And it's
all pointed at you.

01:11:14.150 --> 01:11:18.380
There's a classic
magical narcissism

01:11:18.380 --> 01:11:21.650
that gets constructed
there if you

01:11:21.650 --> 01:11:24.680
take the psychological
literature, at least

01:11:24.680 --> 01:11:27.260
a certain strand
of it, seriously.

01:11:27.260 --> 01:11:33.440
And I think it's largely apt
to think of a child at that age

01:11:33.440 --> 01:11:35.120
as a kind of--

01:11:38.460 --> 01:11:40.980
there is a kind of
narcissistic structure

01:11:40.980 --> 01:11:44.190
there, where it's all
kind of going inward.

01:11:44.190 --> 01:11:48.900
And at a certain point
you realize, OK, I'm

01:11:48.900 --> 01:11:51.030
the center of all of this.

01:11:51.030 --> 01:11:58.140
It's not just a movie where
you're completely absorbed in,

01:11:58.140 --> 01:12:00.000
and you've lost
your sense of self.

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:05.237
I mean, to talk to yet
another example of what

01:12:05.237 --> 01:12:07.320
it's like as a grown up
to lose our sense of self.

01:12:07.320 --> 01:12:10.500
And one of the things I
think we find so fascinating

01:12:10.500 --> 01:12:13.770
about television and film
is that when we get totally

01:12:13.770 --> 01:12:17.670
absorbed in it, we're in this
very unusual circumstance where

01:12:17.670 --> 01:12:20.520
our brain is
basically reading it

01:12:20.520 --> 01:12:23.280
as we're in the classic
social circumstance.

01:12:23.280 --> 01:12:27.910
We're presented with the facial
displays of other people.

01:12:27.910 --> 01:12:31.620
In fact, sometimes these
people are 10 feet tall

01:12:31.620 --> 01:12:33.300
or their faces are 10 feet tall.

01:12:33.300 --> 01:12:35.200
You have a close-up
in a movie theater.

01:12:35.200 --> 01:12:39.510
So it's like a super stimulus,
in terms of evolution.

01:12:39.510 --> 01:12:43.890
And they can be making direct
eye contact with a camera,

01:12:43.890 --> 01:12:46.530
so you have this gigantic
face staring at you.

01:12:46.530 --> 01:12:48.870
And yet, you're totally
uncomplicated socially.

01:12:48.870 --> 01:12:50.250
You can't be seen.

01:12:50.250 --> 01:12:52.780
And something you know
you can't be seen.

01:12:52.780 --> 01:12:57.000
And so you completely
lose self consciousness.

01:12:57.000 --> 01:13:01.770
And yet, you're able to examine
with completely free attention,

01:13:01.770 --> 01:13:04.980
again, because you're
totally unimplicated,

01:13:04.980 --> 01:13:11.550
the facial minutia and the
mimetic facial play of people

01:13:11.550 --> 01:13:14.250
at a very close range.

01:13:14.250 --> 01:13:20.970
I mean, you have physically
just about to kiss your spouse,

01:13:20.970 --> 01:13:23.890
like that's what a
close-up is in a film.

01:13:23.890 --> 01:13:26.520
You never get that
close to people.

01:13:26.520 --> 01:13:28.680
And yet, here you're
in a situation

01:13:28.680 --> 01:13:29.740
where you're unobserved.

01:13:29.740 --> 01:13:30.490
And you know that.

01:13:30.490 --> 01:13:32.910
And so I mean, this
is a bit of a tangent,

01:13:32.910 --> 01:13:35.340
but it's the other side
of what's happening

01:13:35.340 --> 01:13:38.970
developmentally for a kid.

01:13:38.970 --> 01:13:41.190
When you're in a movie
theater watching a movie,

01:13:41.190 --> 01:13:42.990
you are truly invisible.

01:13:42.990 --> 01:13:45.990
And yet, you're right there.

01:13:45.990 --> 01:13:48.660
However harrowing
the human drama

01:13:48.660 --> 01:13:50.220
is, you're seeing it play out.

01:13:50.220 --> 01:13:52.020
And you're seeing it up close.

01:13:52.020 --> 01:13:55.350
And it is, in principle,
a social encounter

01:13:55.350 --> 01:13:58.230
that your genes are ready
for, but they're not ready

01:13:58.230 --> 01:13:59.460
for you to be invisible.

01:13:59.460 --> 01:14:02.770
And so that's what's
so magical about it.

01:14:02.770 --> 01:14:05.820
But what happens developmentally
for a kid is that you're not

01:14:05.820 --> 01:14:08.400
invisible, you are an
object that is constantly

01:14:08.400 --> 01:14:15.120
being overrun, the boundaries
of your sensory engagement

01:14:15.120 --> 01:14:18.120
with the world are constantly
being impinged upon by others.

01:14:18.120 --> 01:14:21.780
And at a certain point,
you recognize, OK, I'm

01:14:21.780 --> 01:14:22.890
at the center of this.

01:14:22.890 --> 01:14:28.830
And the way this gets
enshrined as a self, I think

01:14:28.830 --> 01:14:32.430
is probably coincident
with our learning

01:14:32.430 --> 01:14:35.970
the language game we
learn to play with others.

01:14:35.970 --> 01:14:37.110
We're talking to others.

01:14:37.110 --> 01:14:38.400
People are talking to us.

01:14:38.400 --> 01:14:42.330
And at a certain point,
we're talking to ourselves,

01:14:42.330 --> 01:14:44.760
even when the other
people leave the room.

01:14:44.760 --> 01:14:45.660
And you can hear it.

01:14:45.660 --> 01:14:47.370
If you ever have
been with a toddler

01:14:47.370 --> 01:14:51.870
when they're externalizing
their self-talk,

01:14:51.870 --> 01:14:53.460
you hear them talking
to themselves.

01:14:53.460 --> 01:14:54.127
They're playing.

01:14:54.127 --> 01:14:56.110
And they're having
a conversation.

01:14:56.110 --> 01:14:58.020
They were talking
to you, the parent.

01:14:58.020 --> 01:15:00.330
But then you left the room,
and they're still talking.

01:15:00.330 --> 01:15:02.490
You come back in, and
they're still talking.

01:15:02.490 --> 01:15:07.720
And what happens
to us, strangely,

01:15:07.720 --> 01:15:12.170
and this comes back to
the logic of evolution,

01:15:12.170 --> 01:15:15.950
we never stop because
evolution never

01:15:15.950 --> 01:15:18.200
thought to build us an
off-switch for this.

01:15:18.200 --> 01:15:20.690
I mean, language is so useful.

01:15:20.690 --> 01:15:24.260
And it gets tuned up
so strongly for us.

01:15:24.260 --> 01:15:26.178
And there was never a
reason to shut it off.

01:15:26.178 --> 01:15:28.220
There was never a reason
to give you this ability

01:15:28.220 --> 01:15:30.950
to say, oh, wouldn't it be nice
to have four hours of quiet

01:15:30.950 --> 01:15:33.320
now, like no self-talk.

01:15:33.320 --> 01:15:35.000
And so for most of
us, I mean, I think

01:15:35.000 --> 01:15:37.880
there are people who, for
whatever neurological reason

01:15:37.880 --> 01:15:41.390
or idiosyncratic reason,
undoubtedly there'd

01:15:41.390 --> 01:15:46.130
be a neurological reason for
it, don't have any self-talk.

01:15:46.130 --> 01:15:50.360
But for most of
us, we are covertly

01:15:50.360 --> 01:15:53.360
talking basically all the time.

01:15:53.360 --> 01:15:56.110
And there's an
imagistic component

01:15:56.110 --> 01:15:57.110
of this for many people.

01:15:57.110 --> 01:15:58.640
You're visualizing
things as well.

01:15:58.640 --> 01:16:05.060
But there's just a ton of
white noise in the mind that

01:16:05.060 --> 01:16:06.500
feels a certain way.

01:16:06.500 --> 01:16:09.770
And what you discover
in meditation,

01:16:09.770 --> 01:16:14.230
ultimately, is that
the self is what

01:16:14.230 --> 01:16:16.540
it feels like to be
thinking without knowing

01:16:16.540 --> 01:16:17.640
that you're thinking.

01:16:17.640 --> 01:16:22.720
A thought arises uninspected
and seems to just become you.

01:16:22.720 --> 01:16:24.250
Like you and I are talking now.

01:16:24.250 --> 01:16:27.090
And people are listening to us.

01:16:27.090 --> 01:16:30.450
They're struggling to follow
the train of this conversation

01:16:30.450 --> 01:16:33.000
because it is competing
with the conversation that's

01:16:33.000 --> 01:16:34.470
happening in their heads.

01:16:34.470 --> 01:16:35.970
So I'll be saying something.

01:16:35.970 --> 01:16:40.950
And a person listening will
say, well, what does that mean?

01:16:40.950 --> 01:16:44.520
Or like, oh, but wait a minute,
he just contradicted himself.

01:16:44.520 --> 01:16:46.350
And there's a voice
in your head that

01:16:46.350 --> 01:16:51.510
is also vying for your
attention much of the time.

01:16:51.510 --> 01:16:54.630
So the first discovery
people make in meditation

01:16:54.630 --> 01:16:56.610
is that it's just so
hard to pay attention

01:16:56.610 --> 01:16:59.760
to anything, the breath,
or a mantra, or a sound,

01:16:59.760 --> 01:17:04.273
whatever it is, because
you're thinking.

01:17:04.273 --> 01:17:06.690
You're thinking about the thing
you need to do in an hour.

01:17:06.690 --> 01:17:08.927
And oh, it's so good that
I downloaded this app.

01:17:08.927 --> 01:17:09.760
This is really good.

01:17:09.760 --> 01:17:11.093
This is going to be good for me.

01:17:11.093 --> 01:17:15.000
But that chatter
isn't showing up.

01:17:15.000 --> 01:17:21.280
You're not far back enough in
the theater of consciousness,

01:17:21.280 --> 01:17:22.710
so as to see it emerge.

01:17:22.710 --> 01:17:24.870
It is just sneaking
up behind you.

01:17:24.870 --> 01:17:26.880
And it feels like me again.

01:17:26.880 --> 01:17:30.760
It feels like when someone is
thinking the thought, well,

01:17:30.760 --> 01:17:32.670
what the hell does that mean?

01:17:32.670 --> 01:17:35.070
They're not seeing
it as an emerging

01:17:35.070 --> 01:17:36.390
object in consciousness.

01:17:36.390 --> 01:17:37.740
It just feels like me.

01:17:41.760 --> 01:17:45.060
Subjectively, is like
the mind contracts

01:17:45.060 --> 01:17:48.330
around this appearance
in consciousness.

01:17:48.330 --> 01:17:52.560
And it really is just a sound
with the voice of the mind.

01:17:52.560 --> 01:17:57.780
If you actually can inspect
it, it is deeply inscrutable

01:17:57.780 --> 01:18:00.030
that we ever feel identified
with our thoughts.

01:18:00.030 --> 01:18:03.420
I mean, how is it that
we could be a thought?

01:18:03.420 --> 01:18:05.310
A thought just arises
and passes away.

01:18:05.310 --> 01:18:09.940
And when you inspect it,
when you go to inspect it,

01:18:09.940 --> 01:18:12.070
it unravels.

01:18:12.070 --> 01:18:17.110
It's the least substantial
possible thing.

01:18:17.110 --> 01:18:21.370
But yet, it could be a
thought of self-hatred.

01:18:21.370 --> 01:18:24.880
It could be a thought,
that unrecognized,

01:18:24.880 --> 01:18:27.385
totally defines your mood.

01:18:31.470 --> 01:18:34.777
I mean, again, this all
can seem kind of abstract.

01:18:34.777 --> 01:18:36.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, no, but I think

01:18:36.360 --> 01:18:38.820
it's extremely concrete
from the perspective

01:18:38.820 --> 01:18:40.620
of the neural circuits
that will return

01:18:40.620 --> 01:18:42.310
to maybe in a few minutes.

