01:19:46.830 --> 01:19:50.370
If you could elaborate a bit on
this notion of internal chatter

01:19:50.370 --> 01:19:54.030
and external stimuli and
the bridge between them

01:19:54.030 --> 01:19:57.390
because I think for some
people that might be intuitive.

01:19:57.390 --> 01:20:02.340
I think for others,
it's not so obvious

01:20:02.340 --> 01:20:08.738
that language is
ongoing in the backdrop.

01:20:08.738 --> 01:20:10.530
And then sometimes, I
think some people are

01:20:10.530 --> 01:20:14.280
more tuned into that language.

01:20:14.280 --> 01:20:16.020
For some people,
it's louder volume.

01:20:16.020 --> 01:20:19.612
For some people,
it's more structured.

01:20:19.612 --> 01:20:22.770
I have a colleague
at Stanford who's

01:20:22.770 --> 01:20:24.540
been on this podcast
called Deisseroth.

01:20:24.540 --> 01:20:26.190
He's one of, like, the
preeminent bioengineers.

01:20:26.190 --> 01:20:27.232
He's also a psychiatrist.

01:20:27.232 --> 01:20:27.960
And he has a--

01:20:27.960 --> 01:20:29.440
he doesn't call it a
meditative practice,

01:20:29.440 --> 01:20:31.148
but he has a practice
where each evening,

01:20:31.148 --> 01:20:34.270
after his five kids are put
down to sleep, you know--

01:20:34.270 --> 01:20:37.020
they're older now--

01:20:37.020 --> 01:20:41.440
and in the quiet of the
late hours of the night,

01:20:41.440 --> 01:20:43.110
early morning, he
sits and forces

01:20:43.110 --> 01:20:44.910
himself to think in
complete sentences,

01:20:44.910 --> 01:20:47.118
with punctuation, for an hour.

01:20:47.118 --> 01:20:49.410
This is the way that he has
taught himself to structure

01:20:49.410 --> 01:20:51.990
his thinking, because of
the very fact that you're

01:20:51.990 --> 01:20:54.060
describing, which is
that ordinarily, there

01:20:54.060 --> 01:20:56.490
is an underlying structure
to what's internal,

01:20:56.490 --> 01:21:00.360
but it's disrupted by external
events, And these are--

01:21:00.360 --> 01:21:03.240
typically, it's
not coherent enough

01:21:03.240 --> 01:21:05.012
to really make meaning from.

01:21:05.012 --> 01:21:06.720
So it's almost like
somebody sitting down

01:21:06.720 --> 01:21:08.512
to write in complete
sentences, but forcing

01:21:08.512 --> 01:21:10.110
himself to do it in his head.

01:21:10.110 --> 01:21:12.060
But for many people,
including myself,

01:21:12.060 --> 01:21:13.980
that's a foreign experience.

01:21:13.980 --> 01:21:17.340
And we only experience structure
through our interactions

01:21:17.340 --> 01:21:19.950
with the world and other people.

01:21:19.950 --> 01:21:24.480
I've taken the time to try and
explore ideas with eyes closed.

01:21:24.480 --> 01:21:26.827
And I've been able to do that.

01:21:26.827 --> 01:21:28.410
There are certain
pharmacologic states

01:21:28.410 --> 01:21:30.630
that we could talk about
that facilitate that.

01:21:30.630 --> 01:21:32.310
And no, those are
not amphetamines.

01:21:32.310 --> 01:21:34.980
Those do exactly the
opposite, by the way.

01:21:34.980 --> 01:21:40.500
But I think people
exist in varying degrees

01:21:40.500 --> 01:21:44.580
of structured and unstructured
internal dialogue,

01:21:44.580 --> 01:21:48.810
and in varying
depths of recognition

01:21:48.810 --> 01:21:50.530
of that internal dialogue.

01:21:50.530 --> 01:21:52.500
And so the question,
I suppose, is,

01:21:52.500 --> 01:21:55.890
just the recognition that
there's a dialogue ongoing,

01:21:55.890 --> 01:21:58.122
internally-- is that,
itself, valuable?

01:21:58.122 --> 01:21:58.830
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

01:21:58.830 --> 01:22:01.020
And that also can
take some time.

01:22:01.020 --> 01:22:02.970
So, I mean, here's
a claim I would

01:22:02.970 --> 01:22:05.910
make that some people
might find surprising.

01:22:05.910 --> 01:22:11.160
But I think this is an
objectively true claim about

01:22:11.160 --> 01:22:15.872
the subjectivity of most people,
which is that unless you have

01:22:15.872 --> 01:22:18.330
a fair amount of training--
let's say you just happen to be

01:22:18.330 --> 01:22:20.910
some kind of savant in this
area, which most people,

01:22:20.910 --> 01:22:24.150
by definition, aren't, or
you have a remarkable amount

01:22:24.150 --> 01:22:27.630
of training in what's called
concentration practice

01:22:27.630 --> 01:22:30.210
in meditation--

01:22:30.210 --> 01:22:35.160
I believe this is a true claim--
that if we just put a stopwatch

01:22:35.160 --> 01:22:39.480
on this table, and people
could just watch it 30 seconds

01:22:39.480 --> 01:22:45.900
elapse, and I set all of our
listeners, or your viewers,

01:22:45.900 --> 01:22:49.990
the task, for the
next 30 seconds,

01:22:49.990 --> 01:22:53.070
just pay attention to anything--
your breath, you know,

01:22:53.070 --> 01:22:56.940
or the sight of your hand,
or the sight of the clock,

01:22:56.940 --> 01:22:59.550
or any object--

01:22:59.550 --> 01:23:02.820
without getting lost in thought,
without getting momentarily

01:23:02.820 --> 01:23:06.075
distracted by this conversation
you're having with yourself.

01:23:10.027 --> 01:23:11.360
A couple of things would happen.

01:23:11.360 --> 01:23:14.500
One is no one would be
able to do it, right?

01:23:14.500 --> 01:23:20.260
And this is not just a
superficial inability.

01:23:20.260 --> 01:23:22.010
I mean, if your
life depended on it,

01:23:22.010 --> 01:23:23.260
you wouldn't be able to do it.

01:23:23.260 --> 01:23:25.960
I mean, if the fate of
civilization depended on it,

01:23:25.960 --> 01:23:30.460
none of our listeners
would be able to do this.

01:23:30.460 --> 01:23:36.220
And yet, some percentage of them
are so distracted by thought

01:23:36.220 --> 01:23:38.980
that they will actually
try this experiment

01:23:38.980 --> 01:23:40.022
and think they succeeded.

01:23:40.022 --> 01:23:40.522
Right?

01:23:40.522 --> 01:23:42.070
And for these people,
what happens is

01:23:42.070 --> 01:23:44.020
you put them on a
meditation retreat,

01:23:44.020 --> 01:23:46.360
and you have them
spend 12 hours a day

01:23:46.360 --> 01:23:48.040
in silence, doing
nothing but this.

01:23:48.040 --> 01:23:48.540
Right?

01:23:48.540 --> 01:23:51.040
So the practice is just
pay attention to the breath

01:23:51.040 --> 01:23:53.050
when they're sitting,
and then eventually, you

01:23:53.050 --> 01:23:55.330
incorporate everything--
sounds and other sensations.

01:23:55.330 --> 01:23:57.893
And then you interleave that
with walking meditation, where

01:23:57.893 --> 01:24:00.310
they're paying attention just
to the sensations of lifting

01:24:00.310 --> 01:24:01.960
and moving and
placing their feet.

01:24:01.960 --> 01:24:04.240
And then once the
practice is going,

01:24:04.240 --> 01:24:06.760
you incorporate sounds
and sights and everything.

01:24:06.760 --> 01:24:08.385
So you can pay
attention to everything,

01:24:08.385 --> 01:24:12.810
but the goal is,
for every moment,

01:24:12.810 --> 01:24:16.470
you are going to cultivate
this faculty of mind,

01:24:16.470 --> 01:24:19.090
which increasingly is
known as mindfulness.

01:24:19.090 --> 01:24:19.590
Right?

01:24:19.590 --> 01:24:23.340
And mindfulness is nothing
other than this very careful

01:24:23.340 --> 01:24:26.490
attention to the contents
of consciousness.

01:24:26.490 --> 01:24:31.110
But the crucial
piece is it is not

01:24:31.110 --> 01:24:32.830
a moment of being
lost in thought.

01:24:32.830 --> 01:24:33.330
Right?

01:24:33.330 --> 01:24:35.550
You're not blocking thoughts.

01:24:35.550 --> 01:24:36.930
Thoughts themselves can arise.

01:24:36.930 --> 01:24:39.210
But in those moments
of being truly mindful,

01:24:39.210 --> 01:24:41.430
you're noticing
thoughts as thoughts.

01:24:41.430 --> 01:24:44.280
Whether it's language
in the mind or images,

01:24:44.280 --> 01:24:47.490
you're noticing those, too,
as spontaneous appearances

01:24:47.490 --> 01:24:49.740
in consciousness.

01:24:49.740 --> 01:24:55.460
So if most people-- you know,
certainly anyone who thinks

01:24:55.460 --> 01:24:57.830
they can pay attention to--

01:24:57.830 --> 01:25:00.860
who can do the experiment
successfully that I just

01:25:00.860 --> 01:25:03.230
suggested-- pay attention
to something for 30 seconds

01:25:03.230 --> 01:25:05.060
without being lost in thought--

01:25:05.060 --> 01:25:07.940
you put those people on a
meditation retreat, what

01:25:07.940 --> 01:25:11.330
they're going to experience
is, you know, on the first day,

01:25:11.330 --> 01:25:13.070
they're going to
feel, like, oh, yeah.

01:25:13.070 --> 01:25:14.750
I was with the breath, or
I was walking-- you know,

01:25:14.750 --> 01:25:16.490
I was with the
sensations of walking--

01:25:16.490 --> 01:25:19.353
and I'd be there for, like,
five minutes solid, and then

01:25:19.353 --> 01:25:20.520
I would get lost in thought.

01:25:20.520 --> 01:25:21.440
And then I'd come back.

01:25:21.440 --> 01:25:23.150
And then five more minutes,
I'd be lost in thought,

01:25:23.150 --> 01:25:23.930
and then get back.

01:25:23.930 --> 01:25:26.780
But as the days progressed--

01:25:26.780 --> 01:25:30.110
even, you know, 10 days in to
a silent meditation retreat--

01:25:30.110 --> 01:25:34.190
they're going to experience
more and more distraction.

01:25:34.190 --> 01:25:36.830
It's going to seem
like, OK, wait a minute.

01:25:36.830 --> 01:25:39.080
Now I can't pay
attention to anything

01:25:39.080 --> 01:25:40.940
for more than 5 seconds.

01:25:40.940 --> 01:25:41.810
Right?

01:25:41.810 --> 01:25:43.310
That is progress, right?

01:25:43.310 --> 01:25:47.060
Because what they're discovering
is just how distractible

01:25:47.060 --> 01:25:47.600
they are.

01:25:47.600 --> 01:25:48.100
Right?

01:25:48.100 --> 01:25:51.593
And you know, for
some people, that

01:25:51.593 --> 01:25:52.760
will be immediately obvious.

01:25:52.760 --> 01:25:55.220
For some people, it'll
actually take a lot of practice

01:25:55.220 --> 01:25:57.523
to realize just how
distracted they are.

01:25:57.523 --> 01:25:59.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What you
just said, which was that,

01:25:59.690 --> 01:26:07.050
at some point, we can start
noticing our thoughts--

01:26:07.050 --> 01:26:09.720
I can notice my thoughts--
but what you're talking about,

01:26:09.720 --> 01:26:14.760
as a goal state, is not
being distracted by thoughts,

01:26:14.760 --> 01:26:16.260
but actually seeing
the relationship

01:26:16.260 --> 01:26:19.110
between thoughts, self, and
other types of perceptions.

01:26:19.110 --> 01:26:22.710
And here, I think recognizing
and seeing thoughts

01:26:22.710 --> 01:26:24.000
is a form of perception.

01:26:24.000 --> 01:26:27.420
It's just an
internally-directed perception.

01:26:27.420 --> 01:26:32.250
This raises a topic that
I'm also obsessed by,

01:26:32.250 --> 01:26:34.620
which I think neuroscience
can somewhat explain,

01:26:34.620 --> 01:26:38.160
but still incompletely--

01:26:38.160 --> 01:26:40.650
that the circuits and
mechanics, et cetera,

01:26:40.650 --> 01:26:44.610
are not yet known-- which
is about time perception.

01:26:44.610 --> 01:26:49.650
And you know, a
simple analogy would

01:26:49.650 --> 01:26:53.545
be that there are a lot of
small objects flying around

01:26:53.545 --> 01:26:55.920
in the space that we happen
to be having this discussion,

01:26:55.920 --> 01:26:58.440
but they're moving so fast
that I can't perceive them.

01:26:58.440 --> 01:27:00.930
Or they're entirely stationary,
so I can't perceive them

01:27:00.930 --> 01:27:02.513
because of the reasons
we talked about

01:27:02.513 --> 01:27:03.720
before in the visual system.

01:27:03.720 --> 01:27:05.580
My eyes are moving
in perfect concert

01:27:05.580 --> 01:27:08.520
with these small
object movements,

01:27:08.520 --> 01:27:11.850
and therefore, I
am blind to them.

01:27:11.850 --> 01:27:13.580
SAM HARRIS: Right.

01:27:13.580 --> 01:27:17.060
ANDREW HUBERMAN: A slight
shift in time perception--

01:27:17.060 --> 01:27:21.000
think of this, perhaps, as
a change in the frame rate,

01:27:21.000 --> 01:27:21.500
right?

01:27:21.500 --> 01:27:22.340
Camera frame rates.

01:27:22.340 --> 01:27:24.897
A faster frame rate, you
can capture slow motion.

01:27:24.897 --> 01:27:26.480
Slower frame rate,
you're going to get

01:27:26.480 --> 01:27:29.180
more of a strobe-type effect if
the frame rate is low enough.

01:27:29.180 --> 01:27:29.930
SAM HARRIS: Right.

01:27:29.930 --> 01:27:32.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right?

01:27:32.180 --> 01:27:37.420
Could it be that our time
perception is not one thing,

01:27:37.420 --> 01:27:40.630
but we have one rate of
perceiving time for external

01:27:40.630 --> 01:27:43.220
objects at a given distance--
which we know is true--

01:27:43.220 --> 01:27:46.390
another frame rate for
objects that are up close-- we

01:27:46.390 --> 01:27:48.640
know this to be true, even
if those objects are moving

01:27:48.640 --> 01:27:50.050
at the exact same speed, right?

01:27:50.050 --> 01:27:51.250
I mean, this would be
the sitting on a train,

01:27:51.250 --> 01:27:54.170
the rungs on the fence seem to
be going by very, very fast,

01:27:54.170 --> 01:27:56.450
but the ones in the distance
seem to be moving slowly.

01:27:56.450 --> 01:27:59.020
This is the way the visual
system and time perception

01:27:59.020 --> 01:28:01.638
interconnect at some level.

01:28:01.638 --> 01:28:03.430
You're up on a skyscraper--
the little ants

01:28:03.430 --> 01:28:06.097
of cars and people down below.

01:28:06.097 --> 01:28:07.930
You know they're moving
much faster than you

01:28:07.930 --> 01:28:10.360
perceive them to move, but
it's a distance effect.

01:28:10.360 --> 01:28:11.860
SAM HARRIS: I mean,
you see a plane,

01:28:11.860 --> 01:28:13.300
it's going to be going
300 miles an hour.

01:28:13.300 --> 01:28:13.780
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Exactly.

01:28:13.780 --> 01:28:14.020
SAM HARRIS: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:28:14.020 --> 01:28:15.070
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And it's not because

01:28:15.070 --> 01:28:16.210
of the lack of resolution.

01:28:16.210 --> 01:28:18.940
The lack of resolution
is incidental.

01:28:18.940 --> 01:28:22.180
We know this because in
animals, such as hawks,

01:28:22.180 --> 01:28:25.540
that have twice the degree
of acuity, as far as we know,

01:28:25.540 --> 01:28:29.350
they have the same
distance-associated shifts

01:28:29.350 --> 01:28:30.790
in time perception.

01:28:30.790 --> 01:28:32.440
So could it be
that we are running

01:28:32.440 --> 01:28:35.890
multiple streams of time
perception, multiple cones

01:28:35.890 --> 01:28:38.890
of attention, that include cones
of attention to our thoughts,

01:28:38.890 --> 01:28:40.600
and that somehow,
through meditation,

01:28:40.600 --> 01:28:43.690
we start to align the frame
rate for these different streams

01:28:43.690 --> 01:28:49.120
of attention so that they
all fall into the same movie,

01:28:49.120 --> 01:28:50.620
if you will--
although it's not just

01:28:50.620 --> 01:28:52.120
a movie with visual content.

01:28:52.120 --> 01:28:54.100
What I'm doing here
is clearly, I'm

01:28:54.100 --> 01:28:55.990
becoming a lumper
rather than a spreader.

01:28:55.990 --> 01:28:58.675
I'm sure this violates certain
rules of time perception

01:28:58.675 --> 01:29:00.850
and neural circuitry,
but I'm not sure

01:29:00.850 --> 01:29:04.060
that it's entirely
untrue, either.

01:29:04.060 --> 01:29:08.035
And does it survive at
all, as a possible model

01:29:08.035 --> 01:29:09.160
for what you're describing?

01:29:09.160 --> 01:29:10.743
And if the answer
is no, I'm perfectly

01:29:10.743 --> 01:29:12.440
comfortable with that.

01:29:12.440 --> 01:29:19.640
SAM HARRIS: Well, it's dependent
on what you mean by meditation.

01:29:19.640 --> 01:29:21.440
This is where, sort
of, the particularities

01:29:21.440 --> 01:29:23.750
of what one is doing
with one's attention

01:29:23.750 --> 01:29:26.660
under the frame of
meditation really

01:29:26.660 --> 01:29:30.830
matter, because there are
ways to practice mindfulness,

01:29:30.830 --> 01:29:34.040
in particular, where
the frame rate really

01:29:34.040 --> 01:29:35.610
does seem to go way, way up.

01:29:35.610 --> 01:29:36.110
Right?

01:29:36.110 --> 01:29:38.690
And there's actually been
some research done on this,

01:29:38.690 --> 01:29:42.380
where you take people before
and after a three-month silent

01:29:42.380 --> 01:29:46.070
meditation retreat, and you
give them some kind of visual

01:29:46.070 --> 01:29:50.390
discrimination task where
they have to detect--

01:29:50.390 --> 01:29:53.690
I think they used
a tachistoscope.

01:29:53.690 --> 01:29:54.913
Is that the tool for?

01:29:54.913 --> 01:29:56.330
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I'm not familiar.

01:29:56.330 --> 01:29:58.986
SAM HARRIS: Something
that presents, you know,

01:29:58.986 --> 01:30:00.860
very quick pulses of light.

01:30:00.860 --> 01:30:07.263
And in any case, just
in any sensory channel,

01:30:07.263 --> 01:30:09.680
I would imagine you can make
finer-grained discriminations

01:30:09.680 --> 01:30:13.340
if you're practicing mindfulness
in a very specific way, which

01:30:13.340 --> 01:30:16.430
is to be making these
fine-grained discriminations

01:30:16.430 --> 01:30:20.362
more and more, and do nothing
else for three months, which

01:30:20.362 --> 01:30:21.320
is a way of practicing.

01:30:21.320 --> 01:30:25.490
So the classic
mindfulness practice

01:30:25.490 --> 01:30:28.910
in what's called
Vipassana meditation

01:30:28.910 --> 01:30:34.620
is to pay scrupulous attention
to seeing, hearing, smelling,

01:30:34.620 --> 01:30:40.190
tasting, touching in a way
that breaks everything down

01:30:40.190 --> 01:30:44.270
into these kind of
microscopic sensory moments.

01:30:44.270 --> 01:30:50.900
So you know, rather than feel
your hands pressing together,

01:30:50.900 --> 01:30:52.910
what you're trying to
feel with your attention

01:30:52.910 --> 01:30:54.830
and you're feeling
more and more is

01:30:54.830 --> 01:30:58.700
all of the sensations of
pressure and temperature

01:30:58.700 --> 01:31:03.440
and movement such that
the feeling of hands

01:31:03.440 --> 01:31:04.460
completely disappears.

01:31:04.460 --> 01:31:06.200
You realize that a
hand is a concept,

01:31:06.200 --> 01:31:11.660
and all you have is this cloud
of punctate and very brief

01:31:11.660 --> 01:31:12.450
sensations.

01:31:12.450 --> 01:31:17.870
And so anything you think you
have as a datum of experience,

01:31:17.870 --> 01:31:22.210
as you bore into it
with your attention,

01:31:22.210 --> 01:31:29.350
it resolves into this kind of
diaphanous cloud of changing

01:31:29.350 --> 01:31:29.980
sensation.

01:31:29.980 --> 01:31:35.640
And that can be even something
as captivating as, like,

01:31:35.640 --> 01:31:37.530
a serious pain in your body.

01:31:37.530 --> 01:31:40.650
I mean, you can have could have
injured your neck, you know?

01:31:40.650 --> 01:31:43.080
And so you have some
excruciating pain in your neck.

01:31:43.080 --> 01:31:45.735
If you just are willing to
pay attention to it, you know,

01:31:45.735 --> 01:31:50.980
and just pay 100% attention to
it, a couple of things happen.

01:31:50.980 --> 01:31:54.390
One is your resistance
to feeling it goes away,

01:31:54.390 --> 01:31:57.030
by definition, because now your
goal is to just pay attention

01:31:57.030 --> 01:31:57.530
to it.

01:31:57.530 --> 01:32:00.930
And you recognize that so much
of the suffering associated

01:32:00.930 --> 01:32:04.200
with the pain was borne of
the resistance to feeling it.

01:32:04.200 --> 01:32:07.060
You're bracing against it and
all of your thinking about it,

01:32:07.060 --> 01:32:07.560
you know?

01:32:07.560 --> 01:32:09.143
You're thinking,
like, well, you know,

01:32:09.143 --> 01:32:10.920
why did I do this to
myself, or should I

01:32:10.920 --> 01:32:13.590
see an orthopedist, or how
long is this going to last?

01:32:13.590 --> 01:32:16.650
And maybe I herniated a disk.

01:32:16.650 --> 01:32:19.590
Like, all of that self-talk
is producing anxiety.

01:32:19.590 --> 01:32:22.290
And I'm not saying there's never
anything to think about there.

01:32:22.290 --> 01:32:25.540
But either you can do something
about it in the moment or you

01:32:25.540 --> 01:32:26.040
can't.

01:32:26.040 --> 01:32:30.360
And so much of our suffering
in the presence of pain is

01:32:30.360 --> 01:32:34.050
the result of resisting it,
worrying about it, thinking--

01:32:34.050 --> 01:32:35.280
just all of the--

01:32:35.280 --> 01:32:36.930
everything we're
doing with our minds,

01:32:36.930 --> 01:32:40.470
but just feeling it, right?

01:32:40.470 --> 01:32:44.220
So when you just
feel it, again, it

01:32:44.220 --> 01:32:50.670
breaks apart into this
ever shifting collection

01:32:50.670 --> 01:32:52.260
of different sensations.

01:32:52.260 --> 01:32:56.880
And it's not one thing, and
it never stays the same.

01:32:56.880 --> 01:32:58.510
So two things happen there.

01:32:58.510 --> 01:33:01.380
One is there can be a
tremendous amount of relief that

01:33:01.380 --> 01:33:05.700
happens there where you can
achieve a level of equanimity,

01:33:05.700 --> 01:33:11.080
even in the presence of really
unpleasant physical sensation.

01:33:11.080 --> 01:33:13.050
And this is true of
mental sensation as well.

01:33:13.050 --> 01:33:14.160
As it's true of emotions.

01:33:14.160 --> 01:33:16.050
The classically
negative emotions

01:33:16.050 --> 01:33:19.110
like anger, depression, or fear.

01:33:19.110 --> 01:33:21.450
The moment you become
willing to just feel them

01:33:21.450 --> 01:33:28.650
in all of their punctate
and changeable qualities,

01:33:28.650 --> 01:33:33.150
they cease to be what
they were a moment ago.

01:33:33.150 --> 01:33:35.910
When you're talking
about emotional states,

01:33:35.910 --> 01:33:39.330
they cease to map back on
to you and your self concept

01:33:39.330 --> 01:33:40.990
as meaningful in the same way.

01:33:40.990 --> 01:33:44.580
So that suddenly, the
anxiety you feel, let's say,

01:33:44.580 --> 01:33:50.070
before going out on stage to
give a talk, a moment ago,

01:33:50.070 --> 01:33:53.700
it had psychological meaning,
it felt like, I'm anxious.

01:33:53.700 --> 01:33:54.870
How do I get rid of this?

01:33:54.870 --> 01:33:56.230
Why am I this sort of person?

01:33:56.230 --> 01:33:58.290
Should I have taken
a beta blocker?

01:33:58.290 --> 01:34:01.275
This is the conversation
you're having with yourself.

01:34:01.275 --> 01:34:03.150
The moment you just
become willing to feel it

01:34:03.150 --> 01:34:09.200
as the pure energy of the
physiology of cortisol release,

01:34:09.200 --> 01:34:12.170
it ceases to have any meaning.

01:34:12.170 --> 01:34:14.720
It ceases to be a
problem in that moment,

01:34:14.720 --> 01:34:16.100
because it's no more--

01:34:16.100 --> 01:34:18.020
it no more maps onto
the kind of person

01:34:18.020 --> 01:34:22.040
you are then a feeling
of indigestion or a pain

01:34:22.040 --> 01:34:25.565
in your knee maps onto the
kind of person you are.

01:34:25.565 --> 01:34:28.483
It's just sensation, anyway.

01:34:28.483 --> 01:34:29.900
Back to the main
point here, which

01:34:29.900 --> 01:34:33.470
is that, if you train
your attention in this way

01:34:33.470 --> 01:34:37.970
to notice the particularities
of sensory experience

01:34:37.970 --> 01:34:39.830
and emotional experience,
you're looking

01:34:39.830 --> 01:34:43.570
for the atoms of experience.

01:34:43.570 --> 01:34:48.720
You get better and better at
that and certain things happen.

01:34:48.720 --> 01:34:52.460
But one thing that I
really do think happens

01:34:52.460 --> 01:34:57.890
is there's a kind of frame
rate change in the data stream

01:34:57.890 --> 01:35:00.300
where you really are just--
you're just noticing much,

01:35:00.300 --> 01:35:02.900
much more.

01:35:02.900 --> 01:35:05.510
All of that is a very
interesting way of training.

01:35:05.510 --> 01:35:08.060
It's not what I tend
to recommend now.

01:35:08.060 --> 01:35:09.950
It's a great
preliminary practice

01:35:09.950 --> 01:35:13.940
for what I do recommend,
because it really

01:35:13.940 --> 01:35:16.340
teaches you the difference
between being lost in thought

01:35:16.340 --> 01:35:18.950
and not, it really teaches
you what mindfulness is.

01:35:18.950 --> 01:35:28.250
But it tends to be done by 99.9%
of people in a dualistic way,

01:35:28.250 --> 01:35:31.370
which, again, you're set
up to think, OK, I'm over

01:35:31.370 --> 01:35:34.100
here as the locus of attention.

01:35:34.100 --> 01:35:36.650
And I'm continually getting
distracted by thought.

01:35:36.650 --> 01:35:39.470
And the project is to not
do that anymore and actually

01:35:39.470 --> 01:35:44.540
pay attention to the breath
and sounds and sensations.

01:35:44.540 --> 01:35:46.250
And every time I
get lost in thought,

01:35:46.250 --> 01:35:47.333
I'm going to go back here.

01:35:47.333 --> 01:35:52.280
But this whole dance
of I'm lost in thought,

01:35:52.280 --> 01:35:56.290
now I'm strategically directing
my attention again, all of this

01:35:56.290 --> 01:35:58.540
seems to ramify
this sense of self.

01:35:58.540 --> 01:36:01.390
The of there's one
to be doing this.

01:36:01.390 --> 01:36:04.180
There's somebody holding
the spotlight of attention

01:36:04.180 --> 01:36:06.640
and getting better
at coming back

01:36:06.640 --> 01:36:10.000
to the object of meditation.

01:36:10.000 --> 01:36:13.775
Again, it's inevitable
that 99.9% of people

01:36:13.775 --> 01:36:15.400
are going to start
there and stay there

01:36:15.400 --> 01:36:17.450
for some considerable
period of time.

01:36:17.450 --> 01:36:21.910
But the thing I like to do
when I talk about all of this

01:36:21.910 --> 01:36:25.540
is undercut the false
assumptions that are anchoring

01:36:25.540 --> 01:36:27.550
all of that as
early as possible,

01:36:27.550 --> 01:36:30.760
because where, I
think, you want to be

01:36:30.760 --> 01:36:34.750
is recognizing that there
is no place from which

01:36:34.750 --> 01:36:36.220
to aim attention.

01:36:36.220 --> 01:36:39.130
This whole dualistic setup
of subject and object

01:36:39.130 --> 01:36:41.560
is the thing that is
already not there.

01:36:41.560 --> 01:36:44.800
And it's not that it's there
and you meditate it out

01:36:44.800 --> 01:36:46.450
of existence successfully.

01:36:46.450 --> 01:36:48.130
It's really not there.

01:36:48.130 --> 01:36:50.380
And if you learn
how to look for it,

01:36:50.380 --> 01:36:53.140
you can see that it's not there
and feel that it's not there.

01:36:53.140 --> 01:36:55.360
And it no longer
seems to be there.

01:36:55.360 --> 01:36:56.830
It's like it's not--

01:36:56.830 --> 01:36:59.785
and it becomes like, again,
like a bi-stable percept where

01:36:59.785 --> 01:37:01.660
you looked at it long
enough and you thought,

01:37:01.660 --> 01:37:07.740
OK, now I see the vase and
the face and I can't unsee it.

01:37:07.740 --> 01:37:10.220
And every time I look,
it's there again, right?

01:37:13.060 --> 01:37:14.170
So yeah.

01:37:14.170 --> 01:37:17.020
So to come back
to the example you

01:37:17.020 --> 01:37:21.680
gave with your
colleague at Stanford

01:37:21.680 --> 01:37:22.970
whose book I know I have.

01:37:22.970 --> 01:37:23.870
I haven't read it.

01:37:23.870 --> 01:37:26.460
This is a-- he wrote
a book, Projections.

01:37:26.460 --> 01:37:27.832
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:37:27.832 --> 01:37:29.960
SAM HARRIS: So it's
on my stack to read.

01:37:29.960 --> 01:37:34.227
But it's the opposite--

01:37:34.227 --> 01:37:35.810
what I'm recommending
is, essentially,

01:37:35.810 --> 01:37:37.400
the opposite end
of the continuum

01:37:37.400 --> 01:37:40.820
of the internal
exercise he was doing.

01:37:40.820 --> 01:37:43.520
So rather than-- so
he's doing something

01:37:43.520 --> 01:37:49.100
very deliberate and controlled,
and he is deliberately

01:37:49.100 --> 01:37:54.945
thinking in complete
sentences and commandeering

01:37:54.945 --> 01:37:59.600
the machinery of thought
and attention in a way

01:37:59.600 --> 01:38:00.620
that I would imagine.

01:38:00.620 --> 01:38:02.703
I mean, I'd be interested
to talk to him about it.

01:38:02.703 --> 01:38:07.025
But I would imagine, he really
feels like he's doing that.

01:38:07.025 --> 01:38:08.675
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
He's an engineer--

01:38:08.675 --> 01:38:12.150
as you've describe it in
this way, it reminds me,

01:38:12.150 --> 01:38:14.900
he's a physician, but
he's also an engineer.

01:38:14.900 --> 01:38:17.570
So it's really about
taking the raw materials

01:38:17.570 --> 01:38:20.160
of thought and engineering
something structured from it.

01:38:20.160 --> 01:38:20.910
SAM HARRIS: Right.

01:38:20.910 --> 01:38:21.410
Right.

01:38:21.410 --> 01:38:24.032
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I haven't
been in Carl's mind.

01:38:24.032 --> 01:38:24.740
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

01:38:24.740 --> 01:38:26.207
But if we got him
talking on that,

01:38:26.207 --> 01:38:28.040
I'm sure we would get
a sense of what it is.

01:38:28.040 --> 01:38:31.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: We'll do that
conversation at some point.

01:38:31.250 --> 01:38:32.480
It's the exact opposite
of what you're describing.

01:38:32.480 --> 01:38:33.938
SAM HARRIS: Exact
opposite would be

01:38:33.938 --> 01:38:41.480
to recognize that the sense of
control is a total illusion.

01:38:41.480 --> 01:38:44.600
Because you don't know what
you're going to think next.

01:38:44.600 --> 01:38:47.840
And even he, in the most
laborious way, I mean,

01:38:47.840 --> 01:38:52.250
he could just get as
muscular as he wants with it.

01:38:52.250 --> 01:38:56.870
He still doesn't know what
he's going to think next.

01:38:56.870 --> 01:39:00.140
Because thoughts simply arise.

01:39:00.140 --> 01:39:02.060
You can run this
experiment for yourself.

01:39:02.060 --> 01:39:04.880
And this connects up to
the topic of free will

01:39:04.880 --> 01:39:06.230
which we might want to touch.

01:39:06.230 --> 01:39:09.890
But I mean, just think
of any category of thing.

01:39:09.890 --> 01:39:12.890
If I asked you to think
of the names of cities

01:39:12.890 --> 01:39:18.500
or of friends you have or of
famous people you can remember

01:39:18.500 --> 01:39:25.220
exist or think of
nouns or anything.

01:39:25.220 --> 01:39:27.740
And just watch what
comes percolating

01:39:27.740 --> 01:39:29.345
into consciousness right now.

01:39:31.906 --> 01:39:33.427
There are things
you can't think of,

01:39:33.427 --> 01:39:35.260
there are things you
don't know the name of.

01:39:35.260 --> 01:39:37.150
There are languages
you don't speak,

01:39:37.150 --> 01:39:40.450
there are famous people you've
never seen or never heard of.

01:39:40.450 --> 01:39:42.160
So you have no control
over that part.

01:39:42.160 --> 01:39:44.740
Like those names
and faces are not

01:39:44.740 --> 01:39:48.130
going to suddenly come
streaming into consciousness.

01:39:48.130 --> 01:39:51.760
But the totality of
facts and figures

01:39:51.760 --> 01:39:55.350
and faces and names
that you do know,

01:39:55.350 --> 01:39:59.400
only some will come
vying for inclusion.

01:39:59.400 --> 01:40:02.100
And there's a sort of--

01:40:02.100 --> 01:40:05.433
we could make guess
and we know something

01:40:05.433 --> 01:40:06.600
about the neurology of this.

01:40:06.600 --> 01:40:11.712
But you depending
on what channel

01:40:11.712 --> 01:40:13.380
you're waiting for
thoughts in, I mean,

01:40:13.380 --> 01:40:15.088
it's going to be
different if it's visual

01:40:15.088 --> 01:40:17.840
or semantic or episodic memory.

01:40:17.840 --> 01:40:19.590
I mean, all of these
things are different.

01:40:19.590 --> 01:40:22.980
But wherever you
point your inner

01:40:22.980 --> 01:40:28.950
gaze of attention and wait
for the next face or name.

01:40:28.950 --> 01:40:31.830
Certain things are going
to come and certain things

01:40:31.830 --> 01:40:32.790
aren't going to come.

01:40:32.790 --> 01:40:35.670
And how you land on one--

01:40:35.670 --> 01:40:37.960
there'll be this process
if you're paying attention,

01:40:37.960 --> 01:40:42.990
you might think, let's say we
go with names of cities, right?

01:40:42.990 --> 01:40:45.150
So you'll think of Paris,
you'll think of London,

01:40:45.150 --> 01:40:48.480
you'll think of Rome,
you'll think of Sedona.

01:40:48.480 --> 01:40:50.440
So these names will come.

01:40:50.440 --> 01:40:53.710
And if I ask you to
just say one, right?

01:40:53.710 --> 01:40:54.210
So just--

01:40:54.210 --> 01:40:55.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Minneapolis
is what came to mind.

01:40:55.770 --> 01:40:56.830
For me, it was very
straightforward.

01:40:56.830 --> 01:40:59.080
It was Minneapolis, the
famous person was Joe Strummer

01:40:59.080 --> 01:41:00.090
and they just like--

01:41:00.090 --> 01:41:02.880
I can give you reasons why
I think those came to mind.

01:41:02.880 --> 01:41:04.010
Recent conversations.

01:41:04.010 --> 01:41:05.970
SAM HARRIS: OK.

01:41:05.970 --> 01:41:10.350
So we know a fair bit
about much of this.

01:41:10.350 --> 01:41:14.460
So one, we know that
your reasons, obviously,

01:41:14.460 --> 01:41:15.460
could be right or wrong.

01:41:15.460 --> 01:41:17.370
They're very likely
to be wrong because we

01:41:17.370 --> 01:41:20.460
have this confabulatory
storytelling mechanism even

01:41:20.460 --> 01:41:23.640
in an intact brain
where we just--

01:41:23.640 --> 01:41:25.740
we seem to never
lack for the reasons

01:41:25.740 --> 01:41:27.515
why something came
to mind, and we

01:41:27.515 --> 01:41:28.890
can know we can
manipulate people

01:41:28.890 --> 01:41:31.380
in ways that prove that
people are just reliably

01:41:31.380 --> 01:41:32.580
wrong and confident.

01:41:32.580 --> 01:41:34.742
You know, confidently
so about the reasons

01:41:34.742 --> 01:41:36.450
why they thought of
things or did things.

01:41:36.450 --> 01:41:41.715
But leaving that aside, even
if you're completely accurate,

01:41:41.715 --> 01:41:43.800
there are people's
names who you know

01:41:43.800 --> 01:41:48.030
and cities names that you
know that inexplicably just

01:41:48.030 --> 01:41:48.945
didn't come to mind.

01:41:48.945 --> 01:41:50.820
And if we ran this
experiment again and again

01:41:50.820 --> 01:41:52.500
and again, they
wouldn't come to mind

01:41:52.500 --> 01:41:54.300
if your brain was in
precisely the state

01:41:54.300 --> 01:41:55.725
it was in a moment ago.

01:41:55.725 --> 01:41:57.570
If we could return
your brain to the state

01:41:57.570 --> 01:41:59.610
it was in a moment
ago, correcting

01:41:59.610 --> 01:42:02.610
for all the deterministic
changes and all

01:42:02.610 --> 01:42:05.490
the random changes that would
have to be corrected for,

01:42:05.490 --> 01:42:07.350
it to just get--
all the synapses

01:42:07.350 --> 01:42:10.470
and the synaptic weights
and everything in the state

01:42:10.470 --> 01:42:13.980
it was in to produce Joe
Strummer in Minneapolis.

01:42:13.980 --> 01:42:17.700
If we rewind that movie, that
part of the movie of your life,

01:42:17.700 --> 01:42:20.160
you are going to say Joe
Strummer in Minneapolis

01:42:20.160 --> 01:42:21.570
a trillion times in a row.

01:42:21.570 --> 01:42:24.600
So this is why, in my view,
the notion of free will

01:42:24.600 --> 01:42:27.227
makes absolutely no sense.

01:42:27.227 --> 01:42:29.310
And you can add as much
randomness to that process

01:42:29.310 --> 01:42:30.030
as you want.

01:42:30.030 --> 01:42:33.722
It still doesn't get you the
freedom people think they have.

01:42:33.722 --> 01:42:36.180
There's another conversation
to have about why none of that

01:42:36.180 --> 01:42:37.830
matters and why
things only get better

01:42:37.830 --> 01:42:40.320
once you admit to yourself
that free will is an illusion.

01:42:40.320 --> 01:42:42.240
And yes, you can get in
shape and you can diet

01:42:42.240 --> 01:42:44.073
and you can do all the
things you want to do

01:42:44.073 --> 01:42:46.260
and you don't have to
think about free will.

01:42:46.260 --> 01:42:52.830
But from a contemplative,
meditative point of view,

01:42:52.830 --> 01:42:55.590
the thing to notice is
that everything is just

01:42:55.590 --> 01:42:57.810
springing into view.

01:42:57.810 --> 01:43:00.540
There's no place from
which you are authoring

01:43:00.540 --> 01:43:02.970
your next thought, because
you would have to think it

01:43:02.970 --> 01:43:04.290
before you think it.

01:43:04.290 --> 01:43:09.090
Like there is just this
fundamental mystery

01:43:09.090 --> 01:43:13.597
at our backs that is disgorging
everything that we experience.

01:43:13.597 --> 01:43:15.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
What if I'm speaking?

01:43:15.180 --> 01:43:17.220
So if I'm talking
about something

01:43:17.220 --> 01:43:20.490
and I have some command
of that information,

01:43:20.490 --> 01:43:23.250
I can often sense what I'm
going to say next and then

01:43:23.250 --> 01:43:24.510
find myself saying it.

01:43:24.510 --> 01:43:26.968
And hopefully, that's what they
say and not something else.

01:43:26.968 --> 01:43:29.070
I certainly said things
I didn't intend to say

01:43:29.070 --> 01:43:30.750
or never thought I
would say in life.

01:43:30.750 --> 01:43:34.980
But when engaged in
speech or action,

01:43:34.980 --> 01:43:37.680
it at least gives us
the illusion, I think,

01:43:37.680 --> 01:43:40.455
that we somehow have more
command over our thoughts.

01:43:40.455 --> 01:43:41.640
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

01:43:41.640 --> 01:43:42.940
Well you have a script.

01:43:42.940 --> 01:43:45.510
I mean, it's like there are
things you know a lot about

01:43:45.510 --> 01:43:48.210
and you've talked
about them a lot,

01:43:48.210 --> 01:43:50.340
and you know you
have the things you

01:43:50.340 --> 01:43:52.380
want to say about those
things and the things

01:43:52.380 --> 01:43:55.830
you don't want to say or
you wouldn't want to say.

01:43:55.830 --> 01:43:57.240
And you know you can--

01:43:57.240 --> 01:44:00.000
it still is a bit of a high wire
act, because you can misspeak

01:44:00.000 --> 01:44:01.958
or you can fail to get
to the end of a sentence

01:44:01.958 --> 01:44:04.260
in a grammatically correct way.

01:44:04.260 --> 01:44:07.620
And again, all of this--
objectively, this whole process

01:44:07.620 --> 01:44:09.490
is mysterious to you.

01:44:09.490 --> 01:44:13.440
You don't know how you follow
the rules of English grammar.

01:44:13.440 --> 01:44:19.740
Like your tongue is doing it
somehow and when it fails,

01:44:19.740 --> 01:44:22.800
it fails, and you're just
as surprised as the next guy

01:44:22.800 --> 01:44:23.580
that it failed.

01:44:23.580 --> 01:44:26.548
And you mispronounce
a word, and OK, I

01:44:26.548 --> 01:44:27.840
don't know what happened there.

01:44:27.840 --> 01:44:30.820
But if it keeps happening, I'm
going to worry I had a stroke.

01:44:30.820 --> 01:44:36.310
And if it stops, I'm not
going to worry about it.

01:44:36.310 --> 01:44:39.150
So it's still
mysterious even when

01:44:39.150 --> 01:44:45.990
you're doing it in a very rote,
deliberative, and repetitive

01:44:45.990 --> 01:44:46.530
way.

01:44:46.530 --> 01:44:49.650
But when you're
talking about something

01:44:49.650 --> 01:44:52.497
you've talked about a
lot and you know where

01:44:52.497 --> 01:44:53.580
you're going to go, right?

01:44:56.358 --> 01:45:01.020
We have many
conversations like this,

01:45:01.020 --> 01:45:03.240
it is somewhat analogous
to like a golf swing.

01:45:03.240 --> 01:45:04.860
Where it's like, you know
how you want to do it,

01:45:04.860 --> 01:45:06.000
it's going to be
all kinds of errors

01:45:06.000 --> 01:45:08.042
that are going to creep
into your execution of it

01:45:08.042 --> 01:45:08.980
in real time.

01:45:08.980 --> 01:45:11.820
But there's like you,
basically, have a pattern.

01:45:11.820 --> 01:45:14.460
And so you have certain
linguistic patterns

01:45:14.460 --> 01:45:16.950
which you're following.

01:45:16.950 --> 01:45:20.580
Again, none of this is
a proof of free will,

01:45:20.580 --> 01:45:23.160
but I will grant you that
phenomenologically, it

01:45:23.160 --> 01:45:26.010
feels different than just
waiting for the next thought

01:45:26.010 --> 01:45:27.090
to come.

01:45:27.090 --> 01:45:30.150
But my point is that,
even if you are--

01:45:33.230 --> 01:45:35.730
I mean, you can trim it down
to the simplest possible thing.

01:45:35.730 --> 01:45:41.520
Like you take two things
you like to drink.

01:45:41.520 --> 01:45:43.780
You like coffee
and you like tea.

01:45:43.780 --> 01:45:45.420
And you're deciding
which to have.

01:45:45.420 --> 01:45:46.260
Both are on offer.

01:45:46.260 --> 01:45:48.540
You've got two cups
in front of you.

01:45:48.540 --> 01:45:51.510
And the question is,
here I've got water

01:45:51.510 --> 01:45:54.390
and I've got coffee, which
am I going to drink next?

01:45:54.390 --> 01:45:57.870
Incredibly, it's as simple
as possible decision.

01:45:57.870 --> 01:46:02.370
And no matter how long I
make this decision process,

01:46:02.370 --> 01:46:04.620
I could literally sit for
an hour trying to figure out

01:46:04.620 --> 01:46:06.660
which to reach for next.

01:46:06.660 --> 01:46:08.610
And I could have my
reasons why, and I

01:46:08.610 --> 01:46:10.080
could have all my self talk.

01:46:12.715 --> 01:46:16.090
There's going to be a
final change in me that's

01:46:16.090 --> 01:46:18.550
going to be the proximate
cause of me deciding one

01:46:18.550 --> 01:46:19.540
over the other.

01:46:19.540 --> 01:46:22.570
And that, no matter
how laborious

01:46:22.570 --> 01:46:28.660
I can make it seem in terms
of my reasoning about it,

01:46:28.660 --> 01:46:32.800
it is going to be fundamentally
mysterious as to why I went

01:46:32.800 --> 01:46:34.810
with one rather than the other.

01:46:34.810 --> 01:46:35.920
Whatever story I have--

01:46:35.920 --> 01:46:39.790
because it's like-- it's still
going to be as mysterious

01:46:39.790 --> 01:46:45.700
as you thinking of Joe
Strummer when you absolutely--

01:46:45.700 --> 01:46:51.280
you know of the existence of
Marilyn Monroe just as much.

01:46:51.280 --> 01:46:53.560
And yet she simply
didn't occur to you.

01:46:53.560 --> 01:46:55.090
It's fundamentally mysterious.

01:46:55.090 --> 01:46:57.670
Like there are people who
are even more famous than Joe

01:46:57.670 --> 01:47:00.040
Strummer to you.

01:47:00.040 --> 01:47:03.250
I mean, I'm sure he may be
somebody who you have thought

01:47:03.250 --> 01:47:06.490
a lot about, but
there are people

01:47:06.490 --> 01:47:09.760
who like if we could just
inventory your conscious life

01:47:09.760 --> 01:47:11.723
going back the last
10 years, there

01:47:11.723 --> 01:47:14.140
are people who you've thought
about more than Joe Strummer

01:47:14.140 --> 01:47:16.090
yet they didn't appear.

01:47:16.090 --> 01:47:18.158
And that is mysterious.

01:47:18.158 --> 01:47:19.700
And they could have,
but they didn't.

01:47:22.720 --> 01:47:26.320
What I'm saying is that
this mystery never gets

01:47:26.320 --> 01:47:28.150
banished in our experience.

01:47:28.150 --> 01:47:29.240
Whatever stories.

01:47:29.240 --> 01:47:31.090
We have to tell about it.

01:47:31.090 --> 01:47:34.390
Because if the story is, oh,
well, I went for the water

01:47:34.390 --> 01:47:39.610
because I think I've been
drinking too much coffee.

01:47:39.610 --> 01:47:41.720
I listen to Andrew
Huberman's podcast

01:47:41.720 --> 01:47:43.720
and he was talking about
caffeine, and I think--

01:47:43.720 --> 01:47:44.500
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
It's good for us,

01:47:44.500 --> 01:47:45.440
but you don't want to overdo it.

01:47:45.440 --> 01:47:45.670
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

01:47:45.670 --> 01:47:45.940
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:47:45.940 --> 01:47:46.180
SAM HARRIS: OK.

01:47:46.180 --> 01:47:48.280
So let's say that is,
actually, the causal chain.

01:47:48.280 --> 01:47:49.738
Like, I listen to
your podcast, you

01:47:49.738 --> 01:47:51.460
say something
about caffeine, now

01:47:51.460 --> 01:47:54.310
I'm self conscious
about my coffee intake.

01:47:54.310 --> 01:47:58.740
But that's just adding a
couple of links to the chain.

01:47:58.740 --> 01:48:02.180
There's still this
fundamental mystery of, well,

01:48:02.180 --> 01:48:04.040
why did I find that persuasive?

01:48:04.040 --> 01:48:08.240
And why did I find it persuasive
now and not five minutes ago

01:48:08.240 --> 01:48:09.860
when I was drinking the coffee?

01:48:09.860 --> 01:48:14.070
Like why did I just remember it
now, or why was it effective?

01:48:14.070 --> 01:48:19.270
You only have-- your
experience in every moment

01:48:19.270 --> 01:48:24.220
is precisely what it is
and not one bit more.

01:48:24.220 --> 01:48:29.140
And this subsumes even moments
of real resolve and effort

01:48:29.140 --> 01:48:31.350
and picking yourself
up by your bootstraps

01:48:31.350 --> 01:48:32.350
and changing everything.

01:48:32.350 --> 01:48:34.810
It's like you're on
a diet and you're

01:48:34.810 --> 01:48:36.460
tempted to eat chocolate.

01:48:36.460 --> 01:48:38.000
And you think you're
about to reach

01:48:38.000 --> 01:48:40.480
and say, no, I'm not
breaking this diet.

01:48:40.480 --> 01:48:42.910
This diet is actually
going to stick.

01:48:42.910 --> 01:48:47.590
Why did that arise in
that moment and not

01:48:47.590 --> 01:48:50.260
at this analogous moment
on your last diet?

01:48:50.260 --> 01:48:53.470
And why did it arise now
to precisely the degree

01:48:53.470 --> 01:48:55.030
that it did?

01:48:55.030 --> 01:48:57.850
Why will it be as
effective as it

01:48:57.850 --> 01:48:59.890
will be and have the
half-life that it will

01:48:59.890 --> 01:49:03.760
have and not 10% more or less.

01:49:03.760 --> 01:49:06.045
All of those are always
mysterious to you.

01:49:06.045 --> 01:49:09.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, could
we give, as we did before,

01:49:09.610 --> 01:49:12.070
an evolutionary and a
developmental explanation?

01:49:12.070 --> 01:49:13.480
An evolutionary
explanation might

01:49:13.480 --> 01:49:17.920
be that directed attention
and action is metabolically

01:49:17.920 --> 01:49:18.700
demanding.

01:49:18.700 --> 01:49:22.330
It would be inefficient
or impossible

01:49:22.330 --> 01:49:26.110
for us to be in constant,
deliberate action with access

01:49:26.110 --> 01:49:29.030
to all the relevant information
as to why we would do anything.

01:49:29.030 --> 01:49:33.340
So our ideas, literally,
spring to the surface

01:49:33.340 --> 01:49:35.890
at the last possible
moment in order

01:49:35.890 --> 01:49:41.470
to offset the great metabolic
requirements of having ideas

01:49:41.470 --> 01:49:43.750
that are related to
goal-directed action

01:49:43.750 --> 01:49:45.700
or that goal-directed
action is expensive.

01:49:45.700 --> 01:49:46.420
That's one idea.

01:49:46.420 --> 01:49:48.010
The other idea would
be-- and we know

01:49:48.010 --> 01:49:50.830
this as a fact, which is
that, initially, the brain is

01:49:50.830 --> 01:49:52.078
fairly crudely wired.

01:49:52.078 --> 01:49:54.370
That's not true within the
neural circuits that control

01:49:54.370 --> 01:49:56.170
breathing, heart
rate, et cetera,

01:49:56.170 --> 01:49:59.210
but within the neural circuits
of sensory perception, thought,

01:49:59.210 --> 01:49:59.710
et cetera.

01:49:59.710 --> 01:50:01.240
They're fairly crudely wired.

01:50:01.240 --> 01:50:03.730
And then across
development, there's

01:50:03.730 --> 01:50:07.008
a progressive pruning
back and also in parallel

01:50:07.008 --> 01:50:08.800
to that, a strengthening
of the connections

01:50:08.800 --> 01:50:11.590
that underlie directed
action and thought.

01:50:11.590 --> 01:50:14.110
And here, I don't mean directed
as in free will, I mean,

01:50:14.110 --> 01:50:17.290
just that I can decide
to imagine an apple

01:50:17.290 --> 01:50:19.405
and imagine that
apple, for instance.

01:50:19.405 --> 01:50:21.783
SAM HARRIS: Right.

01:50:21.783 --> 01:50:22.575
But your decision--

01:50:22.575 --> 01:50:23.992
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
There seems to be

01:50:23.992 --> 01:50:28.560
some maintenance of the fine
random wiring in systems.

01:50:28.560 --> 01:50:30.810
I mean, we've seen
this even in worms,

01:50:30.810 --> 01:50:35.280
in flies, in so-called lower
invertebrates and lower

01:50:35.280 --> 01:50:35.790
vertebrates.

01:50:35.790 --> 01:50:37.200
And we see this in humans.

01:50:37.200 --> 01:50:40.230
And it seems to be that
there's a lot of background

01:50:40.230 --> 01:50:41.280
spontaneous activity.

01:50:41.280 --> 01:50:44.260
I mean, I've sunk electrodes
into the brains of humans,

01:50:44.260 --> 01:50:46.260
macaques, carnivores, and mice.

01:50:46.260 --> 01:50:48.667
And in every case, most of
what you hear is called hash

01:50:48.667 --> 01:50:50.250
and it has nothing
to do with hashish.

01:50:50.250 --> 01:50:52.320
Is just [VOCALIZING]
on the audio monitor

01:50:52.320 --> 01:50:55.260
which is picking up a
bunch of action potentials.

01:50:55.260 --> 01:50:58.630
As you're listening to a
chorus of action potentials.

01:50:58.630 --> 01:51:02.070
But it's rare to find a neuron
that faithfully [VOCALIZING]

01:51:02.070 --> 01:51:05.790
fires to represent some
sensory stimulus in the world.

01:51:05.790 --> 01:51:09.190
And you can arrange that
marriage experimentally

01:51:09.190 --> 01:51:12.100
so that you can arrive at those
strong signal to noise events.

01:51:12.100 --> 01:51:14.380
But I was always struck
by how much noise there

01:51:14.380 --> 01:51:17.050
is in the system all
around all the time.

01:51:17.050 --> 01:51:20.772
And people argue, is the
noise really noise, et cetera?

01:51:20.772 --> 01:51:22.480
There's still a lot
of debate about that.

01:51:22.480 --> 01:51:25.870
But I can imagine that some
of the spontaneous nature

01:51:25.870 --> 01:51:27.370
of thoughts just
relates to the fact

01:51:27.370 --> 01:51:29.320
that there's a lot of
background spontaneous activity

01:51:29.320 --> 01:51:30.110
in the brain.

01:51:30.110 --> 01:51:32.480
Now, why that is a
whole other discussion.

01:51:32.480 --> 01:51:35.290
But if I were to set up two
constraints that there's

01:51:35.290 --> 01:51:36.970
a lot of spontaneous
activity, it's

01:51:36.970 --> 01:51:39.270
going to generate
random thoughts.

01:51:39.270 --> 01:51:40.770
Thankfully, not
much random action,

01:51:40.770 --> 01:51:43.520
although there's a little bit
of random action in our daily

01:51:43.520 --> 01:51:44.020
lives.

01:51:44.020 --> 01:51:47.280
And then against that say,
well, any deliberate thought

01:51:47.280 --> 01:51:51.180
or motion is going
to be expensive.

01:51:51.180 --> 01:51:53.680
It's a metabolically
expensive organ to begin with.

01:51:53.680 --> 01:51:55.080
And so you just have to--

01:51:55.080 --> 01:52:00.090
evolution has arrived at a place
where spontaneous geysering up

01:52:00.090 --> 01:52:04.470
of things upon which like
deliberate thoughts and action

01:52:04.470 --> 01:52:07.500
are superimposed is
the best arrangement

01:52:07.500 --> 01:52:10.687
overall for this very
metabolically demanding organ.

01:52:10.687 --> 01:52:12.270
I mean, what I
basically gave was just

01:52:12.270 --> 01:52:16.060
kind of a biological description
of just one narrow aspect

01:52:16.060 --> 01:52:16.560
of it.

01:52:16.560 --> 01:52:18.760
But can we get
comfortable with that?

01:52:18.760 --> 01:52:21.480
And the reason I say get
comfortable is that I'm--

01:52:21.480 --> 01:52:23.430
and here, admittedly,
I'm forcing a little bit

01:52:23.430 --> 01:52:25.140
of a striptease
towards what I think I

01:52:25.140 --> 01:52:27.515
and everyone else wants to
know, which is how to meditate

01:52:27.515 --> 01:52:30.750
and why, in particular,
meditation convinces us

01:52:30.750 --> 01:52:32.485
that something
doesn't necessarily

01:52:32.485 --> 01:52:34.860
have to be eliminated, but
that was actually never there.

01:52:34.860 --> 01:52:39.840
I feel like we've now set
up a sort of almost like a--

01:52:39.840 --> 01:52:41.820
you're not contradicting
yourself by any means,

01:52:41.820 --> 01:52:43.680
but in my mind, there's
a contradiction.

01:52:43.680 --> 01:52:46.660
And here's the contradiction.

01:52:46.660 --> 01:52:50.440
I love this statement
that meditation over time

01:52:50.440 --> 01:52:52.840
or done properly reveals
to us that we're actually

01:52:52.840 --> 01:52:56.470
not trying to make the gap
between actor and observer

01:52:56.470 --> 01:52:57.010
go away.

01:52:57.010 --> 01:52:58.617
It was actually never there.

01:52:58.617 --> 01:53:00.700
To me, that's one of the
more important statements

01:53:00.700 --> 01:53:02.140
that I, perhaps,
have ever heard.

01:53:02.140 --> 01:53:04.780
And it inspires me to go further
down this path of meditation,

01:53:04.780 --> 01:53:06.470
because I've never
experienced that.

01:53:06.470 --> 01:53:07.220
SAM HARRIS: Right.

01:53:07.220 --> 01:53:09.262
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Not
deliberately, and certainly,

01:53:09.262 --> 01:53:10.630
not through meditation.

01:53:10.630 --> 01:53:13.690
If I ever experienced it,
it was transient enough

01:53:13.690 --> 01:53:16.288
that I'm intrigued to
experience it more.

01:53:16.288 --> 01:53:17.830
So on the one hand,
you're telling me

01:53:17.830 --> 01:53:19.040
something was never there.

01:53:19.040 --> 01:53:20.920
And there's a
profound experience

01:53:20.920 --> 01:53:23.860
to be heard by anyone that's
willing to do the work

01:53:23.860 --> 01:53:27.940
to arrive at that experience
of the loss of that illusion.

01:53:27.940 --> 01:53:31.430
On the other hand, I'm
hearing that there's

01:53:31.430 --> 01:53:34.520
a profound gap that
really does exist,

01:53:34.520 --> 01:53:40.270
which is that we believe
that our thoughts are somehow

01:53:40.270 --> 01:53:41.980
from us.

01:53:41.980 --> 01:53:45.370
And indeed, they're from in the
cranial vault someplace, maybe,

01:53:45.370 --> 01:53:47.410
in the body a bit as well.

01:53:47.410 --> 01:53:55.540
But that we over attribute the
degree to which we are that

01:53:55.540 --> 01:53:59.740
and that is us in a way that's
volitional, that we control.

01:53:59.740 --> 01:54:01.885
And so once I'm hearing
that there's something--

01:54:01.885 --> 01:54:04.510
there's an illusion that we can
eliminate and on the other hand

01:54:04.510 --> 01:54:06.593
I'm hearing that there's
an illusion that we can't

01:54:06.593 --> 01:54:08.980
eliminate, and maybe
these are unrelated

01:54:08.980 --> 01:54:12.520
and I'm bridging them in
an unimportant way, that

01:54:12.520 --> 01:54:13.780
seems only important to me.

01:54:13.780 --> 01:54:16.420
But somehow, I can't
resolve these two

01:54:16.420 --> 01:54:19.000
and maybe the thing
to do then is, can we

01:54:19.000 --> 01:54:22.900
separate them in terms of
a practice to witness them?

01:54:22.900 --> 01:54:26.860
That would allow us to
resolve them separately.

01:54:26.860 --> 01:54:27.640
SAM HARRIS: Right.

01:54:27.640 --> 01:54:31.360
So I think I'm
hearing the problem.

01:54:31.360 --> 01:54:36.040
There's this-- let me bracket
the whole free will discussion

01:54:36.040 --> 01:54:41.950
because it really is the flip
side of this coin that I'm--

01:54:41.950 --> 01:54:44.770
the obverse of which is
the illusion of the self.

01:54:44.770 --> 01:54:46.120
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So at least--

01:54:46.120 --> 01:54:47.020
I might be on the right track.

01:54:47.020 --> 01:54:47.340
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

01:54:47.340 --> 01:54:47.540
Yeah.

01:54:47.540 --> 01:54:49.750
ANDREW HUBERMAN: They are
the opposite sides of a coin.

01:54:49.750 --> 01:54:50.245
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

01:54:50.245 --> 01:54:50.410
Yeah.

01:54:50.410 --> 01:54:50.545
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

01:54:50.545 --> 01:54:51.045
Great.

01:54:51.045 --> 01:54:53.385
Because to me they seem
very different in essence.

01:54:53.385 --> 01:54:54.010
SAM HARRIS: No.

01:54:54.010 --> 01:54:56.560
Because what I'm calling
the sense of self

01:54:56.560 --> 01:54:58.510
and what I think
most people feel

01:54:58.510 --> 01:55:03.880
as their core sense of
self is this feeling of--

01:55:03.880 --> 01:55:07.240
I mean, it's the feeling of
being the locus of attention,

01:55:07.240 --> 01:55:10.210
but it's also the feeling of
being the locus of agency.

01:55:10.210 --> 01:55:12.580
I can do the next thing.

01:55:12.580 --> 01:55:13.900
Like, who's doing this?

01:55:13.900 --> 01:55:15.040
Who's reaching for the cup?

01:55:15.040 --> 01:55:15.610
I am.

01:55:15.610 --> 01:55:16.110
Right?

01:55:16.110 --> 01:55:19.570
I intended this, and
now I'm doing the thing.

01:55:19.570 --> 01:55:23.170
And my conscious intention
is the proximate cause

01:55:23.170 --> 01:55:26.738
of my reaching, right?

01:55:26.738 --> 01:55:28.780
And so I'm the author of
my thoughts and actions,

01:55:28.780 --> 01:55:30.190
essentially.

01:55:30.190 --> 01:55:32.980
And my specific uses
of attention, right?

01:55:32.980 --> 01:55:35.950
So I can pay attention to the
breath, I get lost in thought,

01:55:35.950 --> 01:55:37.360
I come back to the breath.

01:55:37.360 --> 01:55:44.050
But on some level, the thoughts
themselves are more of my

01:55:44.050 --> 01:55:49.900
doing something with
almost authorial intent.

01:55:49.900 --> 01:55:53.020
Like I'm thinking like, what the
hell is this guy talking about?

01:55:55.820 --> 01:55:57.070
Who's thinking these thoughts?

01:55:57.070 --> 01:55:58.870
I am.

01:55:58.870 --> 01:56:00.970
The person who really
doesn't get what I'm saying

01:56:00.970 --> 01:56:03.010
is thinking something like that.

01:56:03.010 --> 01:56:04.735
What the fuck is this
guy talking about?

01:56:04.735 --> 01:56:06.670
I know I'm here.

01:56:06.670 --> 01:56:10.330
I'm a self, I'm a
body, I'm a mind.

01:56:10.330 --> 01:56:11.500
I can reach for things.

01:56:11.500 --> 01:56:13.660
That these intentional
actions are

01:56:13.660 --> 01:56:16.480
different from things
that happen to me.

01:56:16.480 --> 01:56:19.630
A voluntary action is different
from an involuntary one.

01:56:19.630 --> 01:56:22.630
So having a tremor is
different from consciously

01:56:22.630 --> 01:56:25.420
deciding to pick up a glass.

01:56:25.420 --> 01:56:30.430
So obviously, everything
I'm saying about meditation

01:56:30.430 --> 01:56:32.560
and the self and
free will in order

01:56:32.560 --> 01:56:35.860
to be a sane picture of a
human mind and of reality

01:56:35.860 --> 01:56:38.530
has to conserve the
data of experience

01:56:38.530 --> 01:56:40.330
such that, yes,
I can acknowledge

01:56:40.330 --> 01:56:44.170
the difference between a tremor
and a deliberative voluntary

01:56:44.170 --> 01:56:45.025
motor action.

01:56:48.706 --> 01:56:50.920
And the things you
do volitionally

01:56:50.920 --> 01:56:53.590
are different not just
psychologically and

01:56:53.590 --> 01:56:56.410
behaviorally, but they just
have different implications

01:56:56.410 --> 01:56:57.940
for like in a court of law.

01:56:57.940 --> 01:57:00.047
You accidentally hit
someone with your car

01:57:00.047 --> 01:57:01.630
or you did it on
purpose, that's still

01:57:01.630 --> 01:57:03.840
a distinction that matters.

01:57:03.840 --> 01:57:07.750
Importantly, it tells us a lot
about the global properties

01:57:07.750 --> 01:57:10.090
of your mind such that we
have a sense of what you're

01:57:10.090 --> 01:57:11.050
likely to do in the future.

01:57:11.050 --> 01:57:13.008
If you're someone who
likes running over people

01:57:13.008 --> 01:57:14.935
with your car,
you're a psychopath

01:57:14.935 --> 01:57:16.060
who we need to worry about.

01:57:16.060 --> 01:57:17.893
If you're is someone
who did it by accident,

01:57:17.893 --> 01:57:22.900
well then you may be culpable
for the level of negligence

01:57:22.900 --> 01:57:24.340
that allow that to happen.

01:57:24.340 --> 01:57:27.430
But you're a very different
person and we you differently

01:57:27.430 --> 01:57:29.680
and we're wise to.

01:57:29.680 --> 01:57:32.170
So anyway, we can
bracket all of that.

01:57:36.100 --> 01:57:38.790
There's this-- I mean,
there are some fundamental--

01:57:38.790 --> 01:57:43.080
there are some false
assumptions about the underlying

01:57:43.080 --> 01:57:47.490
logic of this process, which,
I think, is worth addressing.

01:57:47.490 --> 01:57:51.270
Is actually-- there was a kind
of found object in the news

01:57:51.270 --> 01:57:54.030
that I talk about at one point.

01:57:54.030 --> 01:57:56.220
I forget where it
is in Waking Up app.

01:57:56.220 --> 01:58:00.310
But there was a story that
I stumbled on the internet.

01:58:00.310 --> 01:58:02.550
I think it's about
12 or 13 years old.

01:58:02.550 --> 01:58:05.088
Of a tourist bus in--

01:58:05.088 --> 01:58:06.130
I think it was in Norway.

01:58:06.130 --> 01:58:09.900
It was somewhere
in Northern Europe.

01:58:09.900 --> 01:58:11.470
And it had about
30 people on it.

01:58:11.470 --> 01:58:15.360
And one person was
described as an Asian woman.

01:58:15.360 --> 01:58:20.860
And they went to a rest stop
and everyone got off the bus.

01:58:20.860 --> 01:58:23.250
And shopped and had lunch.

01:58:23.250 --> 01:58:27.300
And this Asian woman changed her
clothing for whatever reason.

01:58:27.300 --> 01:58:29.650
And they all got
back on the bus.

01:58:29.650 --> 01:58:31.650
I think the relevance of
it being an Asian woman

01:58:31.650 --> 01:58:33.900
is that there were
language barriers that

01:58:33.900 --> 01:58:36.270
explain what later happened.

01:58:36.270 --> 01:58:37.800
So everyone gets
back on the bus,

01:58:37.800 --> 01:58:39.930
the Asian woman has
changed her clothing.

01:58:39.930 --> 01:58:42.930
And the bus is about to leave
but then someone notices, hey,

01:58:42.930 --> 01:58:45.630
there was an Asian woman who
got off the bus who isn't--

01:58:45.630 --> 01:58:46.890
it hasn't come back yet.

01:58:46.890 --> 01:58:49.530
And they tell the driver this.

01:58:49.530 --> 01:58:51.093
And this poses a problem.

01:58:51.093 --> 01:58:53.260
So now everyone's waiting
for this person to return.

01:58:53.260 --> 01:58:54.942
But in fact, everyone
was on the bus.

01:58:54.942 --> 01:58:56.650
This woman had just
changed her clothing,

01:58:56.650 --> 01:59:00.510
and was not recognized
by her fellow travelers.

01:59:00.510 --> 01:59:06.390
So everyone gets concerned as
this tourist doesn't show up.

01:59:06.390 --> 01:59:09.450
And they start looking for her.

01:59:09.450 --> 01:59:10.780
And they can't find her.

01:59:10.780 --> 01:59:12.360
And so a search party is formed.

01:59:12.360 --> 01:59:18.295
And the Asian woman, because
of whatever language barrier,

01:59:18.295 --> 01:59:19.920
heard that there was
a missing tourist,

01:59:19.920 --> 01:59:22.050
so she joins the
search party, which,

01:59:22.050 --> 01:59:23.970
in fact, is looking for her.

01:59:23.970 --> 01:59:25.770
And this goes on into the night.

01:59:25.770 --> 01:59:29.280
And they're readying
helicopters for a dawn patrol

01:59:29.280 --> 01:59:31.680
to find the missing tourist.

01:59:31.680 --> 01:59:33.370
Now, at some point
along the way,

01:59:33.370 --> 01:59:35.340
I think it was at like
3:00 in the morning,

01:59:35.340 --> 01:59:41.070
this tourist realizes that she
is the object of this search.

01:59:41.070 --> 01:59:44.070
And obviously, the
whole thing unravels.

01:59:44.070 --> 01:59:47.880
She confesses that she changed
her clothes and the problem

01:59:47.880 --> 01:59:48.640
is solved.

01:59:48.640 --> 01:59:53.610
But the problem is not
solved by the logic

01:59:53.610 --> 01:59:55.840
that the seeker is expected.

01:59:55.840 --> 01:59:58.530
So it's like, it's
not true to say

01:59:58.530 --> 02:00:01.350
that the missing
tourist was found

02:00:01.350 --> 02:00:02.882
in the way that was expected.

02:00:02.882 --> 02:00:04.590
Because a missing
tourist was never lost.

02:00:04.590 --> 02:00:07.847
The missing tourist was
part of the search party.

02:00:07.847 --> 02:00:09.930
So when you think about
it from her point of view,

02:00:09.930 --> 02:00:12.810
like what happened, she's
part of the search party.

02:00:12.810 --> 02:00:16.200
She's looking for the
missing tourist not knowing

02:00:16.200 --> 02:00:18.990
that she, in fact, is
the missing tourist.

02:00:18.990 --> 02:00:25.470
So what happens at the moment
she realizes that everyone's

02:00:25.470 --> 02:00:26.370
looking for her?

02:00:28.942 --> 02:00:32.370
The search isn't
consummated in the way

02:00:32.370 --> 02:00:36.210
that is implied by the logic
of everyone's use of attention.

02:00:36.210 --> 02:00:38.850
And yet the problem evaporates.

02:00:38.850 --> 02:00:42.300
And there's something
deeply analogous

02:00:42.300 --> 02:00:48.030
about the structure of that
and the meditative journey.

02:00:48.030 --> 02:00:52.283
Precisely in, again, not talking
about all the possible changes

02:00:52.283 --> 02:00:54.450
in the contents of consciousness
that could be good,

02:00:54.450 --> 02:00:57.000
which, again, they
come along for the ride

02:00:57.000 --> 02:01:01.480
anyway when you do the
thing I'm talking about.

02:01:01.480 --> 02:01:03.970
It's on this point of
looking for the self

02:01:03.970 --> 02:01:05.060
and not finding it.

02:01:05.060 --> 02:01:10.330
And there is this sense
that, OK, the self is here,

02:01:10.330 --> 02:01:11.290
and it's a problem.

02:01:11.290 --> 02:01:15.430
It is the string upon which
all of my conscious states,

02:01:15.430 --> 02:01:17.850
mostly unhappy ones, are strong.

02:01:17.850 --> 02:01:22.177
It's the thing that is at
the center of my anxiety.

02:01:22.177 --> 02:01:24.010
It's the thing that I
don't feel good about.

02:01:24.010 --> 02:01:29.620
It's the thing that when
criticized, I let implode.

02:01:29.620 --> 02:01:31.553
It's the center of my
problem, and now I'm

02:01:31.553 --> 02:01:32.470
trying to feel better.

02:01:32.470 --> 02:01:34.570
And meditation has
been handed to me

02:01:34.570 --> 02:01:39.190
as a possible remedy
for my situation.

02:01:39.190 --> 02:01:40.630
And it's billed as a remedy.

02:01:40.630 --> 02:01:43.480
And in fact, I'm
hearing from this guy

02:01:43.480 --> 02:01:46.750
that this is the thing that is
going to cause me to realize

02:01:46.750 --> 02:01:49.000
that my self isn't where--

02:01:49.000 --> 02:01:51.590
or as I thought it was.

02:01:51.590 --> 02:01:53.020
So now I'm going to look.

02:01:53.020 --> 02:01:59.360
And so again, the
sense is, I start out

02:01:59.360 --> 02:02:00.800
far away from the goal here.

02:02:00.800 --> 02:02:02.780
I start out with a problem.

02:02:02.780 --> 02:02:06.170
I'm now meditating on the
evidence of my enlightenment.

02:02:06.170 --> 02:02:07.610
I can feel my problem.

02:02:07.610 --> 02:02:10.130
I feel that I'm distracted
and distractible.

02:02:10.130 --> 02:02:13.040
And I feel as this sort
of cramp at the center

02:02:13.040 --> 02:02:14.690
of my life, that's me.

02:02:14.690 --> 02:02:16.610
And I'm not as happy as
I want to be, I'm not

02:02:16.610 --> 02:02:17.975
as confident as I want to be.

02:02:17.975 --> 02:02:19.770
I'm more distractible
than I want to be.

02:02:19.770 --> 02:02:24.520
And now, I'm paying
attention to the breath.

02:02:24.520 --> 02:02:26.590
This is what the search
party feels like.

02:02:26.590 --> 02:02:29.560
This is what the
confused tourist feels

02:02:29.560 --> 02:02:31.150
like in her own search party.

02:02:31.150 --> 02:02:36.670
And she's looking for
the missing person.

02:02:36.670 --> 02:02:38.710
So the angle of--

02:02:38.710 --> 02:02:41.800
the inclination of
all of this is--

02:02:41.800 --> 02:02:47.440
and the logic of it is all
wrong, understandably so,

02:02:47.440 --> 02:02:50.290
given how we all get
into this situation.

02:02:50.290 --> 02:02:53.930
But it's useful
to continually try

02:02:53.930 --> 02:03:00.940
to undercut it and recognize
that the thing that's

02:03:00.940 --> 02:03:04.780
being looked for is actually
right on the surface, which

02:03:04.780 --> 02:03:07.060
is, there is no one looking.

02:03:07.060 --> 02:03:09.580
There is no place
from which you are--

02:03:09.580 --> 02:03:12.640
if you're paying attention
to the breath or to sounds

02:03:12.640 --> 02:03:15.670
or noticing the
next thought arise,

02:03:15.670 --> 02:03:19.120
this sense that you
are over here doing

02:03:19.120 --> 02:03:23.130
that thing is actually what
it's like to be thinking

02:03:23.130 --> 02:03:25.793
and not knowing that
you're thinking.

02:03:25.793 --> 02:03:27.210
There's an
undercurrent of thought

02:03:27.210 --> 02:03:29.530
that's going uninspected
in that moment.

02:03:29.530 --> 02:03:32.500
And so there is just--

02:03:32.500 --> 02:03:35.310
there's a continually
looking for the mind,

02:03:35.310 --> 02:03:37.070
looking for the
center of experience,

02:03:37.070 --> 02:03:39.930
looking for the one who is
looking, which, again, which

02:03:39.930 --> 02:03:43.230
is the orienting practice here.

02:03:43.230 --> 02:03:46.710
And there's a lot more I
say about this, obviously,

02:03:46.710 --> 02:03:48.090
over waking up.

02:03:48.090 --> 02:03:52.810
But it's the experiment you
have to perform in order

02:03:52.810 --> 02:03:58.975
to get ready to recognize that
the search party was formed

02:03:58.975 --> 02:04:00.370
in error, essentially.

02:04:00.370 --> 02:04:03.610
And the problem
that you're trying

02:04:03.610 --> 02:04:07.600
to solve with this
practice does evaporate

02:04:07.600 --> 02:04:10.930
in a similar way, which is
like, you don't actually

02:04:10.930 --> 02:04:15.520
get there in the way
that you're hoping for.

02:04:15.520 --> 02:04:17.890
It's like you drop out
the bottom of this thing

02:04:17.890 --> 02:04:20.680
in an unexpected way.

02:04:20.680 --> 02:04:26.200
There's actually another
similar parable or anecdote

02:04:26.200 --> 02:04:28.300
that I don't remember
if it's Zen or Sufi or--

02:04:28.300 --> 02:04:30.050
I mean, I'm sure it's
been re-appropriated

02:04:30.050 --> 02:04:33.190
in many different ways or by
many different traditions.

02:04:33.190 --> 02:04:38.080
But the case of somebody who's
lost in a town and they're

02:04:38.080 --> 02:04:42.252
asking for directions and you
can put this in Manhattan.

02:04:42.252 --> 02:04:44.710
Let's say, you're wandering
Manhattan and you're a tourist,

02:04:44.710 --> 02:04:46.085
you don't know
where anything is.

02:04:46.085 --> 02:04:49.690
And you stop and ask someone,
where is Central Park?

02:04:49.690 --> 02:04:52.180
And the person thinks for a
second and they says, oh, yeah.

02:04:52.180 --> 02:04:56.020
Unfortunately, you can't get
to Central Park from here.

02:04:56.020 --> 02:04:58.150
Now, that is a very strange--

02:04:58.150 --> 02:04:59.900
I mean, you think about
that for a second.

02:04:59.900 --> 02:05:04.090
You realize, OK,
that's an absurd.

02:05:04.090 --> 02:05:06.970
There is no place that you can't
get to from the place you're

02:05:06.970 --> 02:05:08.717
starting on Earth.

02:05:08.717 --> 02:05:10.300
ANDREW HUBERMAN: A
failure to describe

02:05:10.300 --> 02:05:13.072
the physical relationships
between anything in the world.

02:05:13.072 --> 02:05:13.780
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

02:05:13.780 --> 02:05:15.520
That's just not the
world we live in.

02:05:15.520 --> 02:05:17.480
So but it's a funny thing.

02:05:17.480 --> 02:05:22.000
But on some level that
is true of meditation.

02:05:22.000 --> 02:05:25.660
It's like you can't
get there from here.

02:05:25.660 --> 02:05:29.680
The sense of view as
subject isn't brought along

02:05:29.680 --> 02:05:31.540
to this thing
you're looking for.

02:05:35.750 --> 02:05:38.230
It's almost like
you're making a fist

02:05:38.230 --> 02:05:43.030
and you're trying to
get to an open hand.

02:05:43.030 --> 02:05:48.700
The fist doesn't get to
take that journey as a fist.

02:05:48.700 --> 02:05:50.390
The fist doesn't go
along for the ride.

02:05:50.390 --> 02:05:53.350
The fist comes apart.

02:05:53.350 --> 02:05:55.900
And on some level,
the subjectivity

02:05:55.900 --> 02:06:00.805
is an attentional fist.

02:06:00.805 --> 02:06:03.190
It is a contraction of energy.

02:06:03.190 --> 02:06:08.560
Again, it's so much bound up
and thought for most of us,

02:06:08.560 --> 02:06:09.930
most of the time.

02:06:09.930 --> 02:06:13.440
And when properly
inspected, there's

02:06:13.440 --> 02:06:17.070
just this evaporation
of the starting point.

02:06:17.070 --> 02:06:20.820
But there's not this fulfillment
of, I'm going to get--

02:06:20.820 --> 02:06:24.270
this fixed is just going to--

02:06:24.270 --> 02:06:26.850
if life gets good enough, if
I get concentrated enough,

02:06:26.850 --> 02:06:30.210
focused enough, if
I austere enough,

02:06:30.210 --> 02:06:34.740
if I renounce enough,
if I desire less, if I--

02:06:34.740 --> 02:06:37.830
with enough good
intentions, this fist

02:06:37.830 --> 02:06:41.010
is going to move into some
sort of sublime condition.

02:06:41.010 --> 02:06:45.120
That's not the logic
of the process.

02:06:45.120 --> 02:06:48.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I really
appreciate these models

02:06:48.760 --> 02:06:50.650
and analogies for
conscious experience

02:06:50.650 --> 02:06:54.190
both as most people experience
them and harbor them

02:06:54.190 --> 02:06:58.000
and as a way to frame
what's possible through

02:06:58.000 --> 02:07:00.260
a proper meditation practice.

02:07:00.260 --> 02:07:02.260
I do want to talk about
what a proper meditation

02:07:02.260 --> 02:07:05.210
practice looks like a bit.

02:07:05.210 --> 02:07:11.110
But at some point, I do want to
raise a model of, maybe, even

02:07:11.110 --> 02:07:15.310
just perceptual awareness to
see if it survives the filters

02:07:15.310 --> 02:07:18.250
that you've provided.

02:07:18.250 --> 02:07:23.440
But first, briefly, and
then we can return to it.

02:07:23.440 --> 02:07:27.520
What does this meditation
practice or set of practices

02:07:27.520 --> 02:07:28.090
look like?

02:07:28.090 --> 02:07:30.580
Obviously, the app
is a wonderful tool.

02:07:30.580 --> 02:07:31.540
I've started using it.

02:07:31.540 --> 02:07:33.430
As I mentioned at the
beginning, my father's

02:07:33.430 --> 02:07:35.410
been using it for a
while and many people

02:07:35.410 --> 02:07:37.250
have derived great
benefit from it.

02:07:37.250 --> 02:07:41.260
But if we were to break
it down, meditation

02:07:41.260 --> 02:07:47.260
into some basic component
parts as we have broken down

02:07:47.260 --> 02:07:50.425
normal perceptual experience in
some of its component parts--

02:07:50.425 --> 02:07:51.645
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

02:07:51.645 --> 02:07:53.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --I can
just throw out some things

02:07:53.770 --> 02:07:55.450
that I associate
with meditation,

02:07:55.450 --> 02:07:58.420
and maybe you can elaborate
on how these may or may not

02:07:58.420 --> 02:07:59.000
be applied.

02:07:59.000 --> 02:08:03.250
For instance, there
is almost always

02:08:03.250 --> 02:08:07.540
a ceasing of robust
motor movement.

02:08:07.540 --> 02:08:09.620
I know they're walking
meditations and so forth.

02:08:09.620 --> 02:08:13.270
But it seems like sitting or
lying down and, perhaps, not

02:08:13.270 --> 02:08:16.870
always but often limiting
our visual perception,

02:08:16.870 --> 02:08:17.695
closing the eyes.

02:08:17.695 --> 02:08:18.650
SAM HARRIS: Right.

02:08:18.650 --> 02:08:22.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Directing
a mind's eye someplace.

02:08:22.690 --> 02:08:27.010
Is there a dedicated effort
toward generating imagery?

02:08:27.010 --> 02:08:28.480
What are the component parts?

02:08:28.480 --> 02:08:31.090
And where I'm really going
with this is, why would those

02:08:31.090 --> 02:08:34.420
component parts, eventually,
allow for this dissolution

02:08:34.420 --> 02:08:37.630
of the fist or the
realization that there

02:08:37.630 --> 02:08:41.572
is no distinction between
actor and observer and so on.

02:08:41.572 --> 02:08:42.280
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

02:08:42.280 --> 02:08:42.780
Yeah.

02:08:42.780 --> 02:08:46.000
Well, so to answer the second
question first, ultimately,

02:08:46.000 --> 02:08:50.260
meditation is not a
practice that you're

02:08:50.260 --> 02:08:52.360
adding to your life.

02:08:52.360 --> 02:08:53.860
It's not a doing
more of anything.

02:08:53.860 --> 02:08:55.810
It's actually ceasing
to do something.

02:08:55.810 --> 02:08:58.780
It's ultimately non-distraction.

02:08:58.780 --> 02:09:01.420
I mean, ultimately, you're
recognizing what consciousness

02:09:01.420 --> 02:09:03.640
is like when you're
no longer distracted

02:09:03.640 --> 02:09:07.293
by the automatic
arising of thought.

02:09:07.293 --> 02:09:08.710
It's not the
thoughts don't arise,

02:09:08.710 --> 02:09:10.085
it's not that you
can't use them,

02:09:10.085 --> 02:09:15.430
it's not that you become
irrational or unintelligent,

02:09:15.430 --> 02:09:17.950
I mean, all of that-- you
still have all of your tools,

02:09:17.950 --> 02:09:21.930
but everything is in plain view.

02:09:21.930 --> 02:09:24.600
I mean, there's an analogy in
Tibetan Buddhism which I love,

02:09:24.600 --> 02:09:30.020
which is you're kind of in
the final stage of meditation,

02:09:30.020 --> 02:09:32.990
thoughts are like thieves
entering an empty house.

02:09:32.990 --> 02:09:34.910
There's nothing
for them to steal.

02:09:34.910 --> 02:09:38.630
So in the usual case,
thoughts are-- there really

02:09:38.630 --> 02:09:40.010
is something in jeopardy.

02:09:40.010 --> 02:09:42.710
Every time a thought comes,
I'm not meditating anymore,

02:09:42.710 --> 02:09:44.840
and not only that,
I feel terrible

02:09:44.840 --> 02:09:48.860
because of what I'm thinking
about most of the time.

02:09:48.860 --> 02:09:52.595
And so it's totally
understandable

02:09:52.595 --> 02:09:54.720
that thoughts seem like a
problem in the beginning.

02:09:54.720 --> 02:09:56.630
And for certain
types of meditation,

02:09:56.630 --> 02:09:59.090
they are explicitly
thought of as a problem

02:09:59.090 --> 02:10:01.940
because you're trying
to focus on one thing

02:10:01.940 --> 02:10:04.370
to the exclusion of everything
else, including thought.

02:10:04.370 --> 02:10:09.070
And that is what I called the
concentration practice earlier.

02:10:09.070 --> 02:10:12.760
And that's a training
that can be good to do.

02:10:12.760 --> 02:10:17.110
It becomes a tool that you can
use for other kinds of insight.

02:10:17.110 --> 02:10:20.650
But it's a very specific
and it's kind of brittle

02:10:20.650 --> 02:10:21.790
skill in the end.

02:10:21.790 --> 02:10:22.898
I mean, it's a skill.

02:10:22.898 --> 02:10:24.940
Just like I'm going to
pay attention to one thing

02:10:24.940 --> 02:10:27.400
and I'm going to do that so
well that everything else is

02:10:27.400 --> 02:10:28.655
going to fade out.

02:10:28.655 --> 02:10:30.280
And it's somewhat
analogous to what you

02:10:30.280 --> 02:10:32.620
described in the visual system.

02:10:32.620 --> 02:10:36.160
If have a laser focus
to one fixation point,

02:10:36.160 --> 02:10:41.430
everything else in your visual
field begins to fade out.

02:10:41.430 --> 02:10:45.050
But meditatively, if you have
a laser focus on any one thing,

02:10:45.050 --> 02:10:50.690
whether it's the breath or a
candle flame or whatever it is,

02:10:50.690 --> 02:10:52.040
not only does--

02:10:52.040 --> 02:10:56.712
I mean, let's use the
breath for a second.

02:10:56.712 --> 02:10:58.045
Because your eyes can be closed.

02:11:00.610 --> 02:11:02.832
I mean, you can lose
all sense of everything.

02:11:02.832 --> 02:11:04.540
I mean, you can lose
all sense of hearing

02:11:04.540 --> 02:11:06.430
and your physical
body can disappear.

02:11:06.430 --> 02:11:09.280
I mean, like, literally, it
can become incredibly subtle

02:11:09.280 --> 02:11:12.280
and vast and drug-like.

02:11:12.280 --> 02:11:15.940
And many people
approach meditation

02:11:15.940 --> 02:11:19.330
thinking climbing the
ladder of those changes

02:11:19.330 --> 02:11:23.410
into subtlety and vastness,
that's the whole game.

02:11:23.410 --> 02:11:27.280
And it can be a deeply
rewarding game to play.

02:11:27.280 --> 02:11:30.730
And it also does come with all
kinds of ancillary benefits.

02:11:30.730 --> 02:11:32.515
I mean, all the
focus and the calm

02:11:32.515 --> 02:11:35.980
and the smoothness
of emotional states.

02:11:35.980 --> 02:11:38.260
I mean, all of that comes
with greater concentration.

02:11:38.260 --> 02:11:40.960
And it can be quite wonderful.

02:11:40.960 --> 02:11:45.220
But again, at best, that's a
tool to aim in the direction

02:11:45.220 --> 02:11:48.890
that I'm talking about now
with respect to meditation,

02:11:48.890 --> 02:11:52.540
which relates to more what
I would call mindfulness

02:11:52.540 --> 02:11:55.030
generically.

02:11:55.030 --> 02:11:57.910
And ultimately,
non-dual mindfulness.

02:11:57.910 --> 02:12:01.450
So mindfulness
generically and for most

02:12:01.450 --> 02:12:04.080
people, certainly
in the beginning,

02:12:04.080 --> 02:12:07.170
dualistically is
just the practice

02:12:07.170 --> 02:12:14.230
of paying careful attention to
whatever is arising on its own.

02:12:14.230 --> 02:12:17.860
Now in the beginning, it's
natural to take a single object

02:12:17.860 --> 02:12:19.930
like the breath as
a starting point.

02:12:19.930 --> 02:12:22.180
It's an anchor.

02:12:22.180 --> 02:12:26.140
But very, very quickly, over the
course of even your first week

02:12:26.140 --> 02:12:31.990
of doing this, teachers and
various sources of information

02:12:31.990 --> 02:12:35.570
will recommend that once you get
some facilities-- once you know

02:12:35.570 --> 02:12:37.570
the difference between
being lost in thought

02:12:37.570 --> 02:12:40.060
and actually paying attention
to the breath, well,

02:12:40.060 --> 02:12:41.912
then you can open
it up to everything.

02:12:41.912 --> 02:12:43.870
You can open up the sounds
and other sensations

02:12:43.870 --> 02:12:47.350
in the body and
moods and emotions.

02:12:47.350 --> 02:12:51.550
And even, ultimately,
thoughts themselves.

02:12:51.550 --> 02:12:53.530
So very quickly,
you can recognize

02:12:53.530 --> 02:12:56.910
that thoughts are
not intrinsically

02:12:56.910 --> 02:12:58.290
the enemy to this practice.

02:12:58.290 --> 02:13:01.500
They are also just spontaneous
appearances in consciousness

02:13:01.500 --> 02:13:03.780
that can be observed.

02:13:03.780 --> 02:13:06.810
But for some considerable
period of time,

02:13:06.810 --> 02:13:09.420
people will feel that
there is a place from which

02:13:09.420 --> 02:13:11.220
that observation is happening.

02:13:11.220 --> 02:13:16.200
There's just, I'm now the
one who's being mindful.

02:13:16.200 --> 02:13:20.780
And however attenuated that
sense of self can be, I mean,

02:13:20.780 --> 02:13:22.760
again, it can get
very expansive.

02:13:22.760 --> 02:13:25.490
I mean, you can lose--

02:13:25.490 --> 02:13:29.570
as you get anything, just
a modicum of concentration,

02:13:29.570 --> 02:13:31.610
you know it becomes
very drug-like

02:13:31.610 --> 02:13:35.030
and you get the boundaries
of your body dissolve.

02:13:35.030 --> 02:13:38.900
And your feeling of having
a body can disappear.

02:13:38.900 --> 02:13:42.543
And you if your eyes are closed,
you know your visual field--

02:13:42.543 --> 02:13:44.960
I mean, most people, when they
close their eyes initially,

02:13:44.960 --> 02:13:46.710
they just forget about
their visual field.

02:13:46.710 --> 02:13:48.770
But if you close
your eyes right now,

02:13:48.770 --> 02:13:51.740
you notice your visual
field is fully present.

02:13:51.740 --> 02:13:55.670
And we call it dark,
but it's not quite dark.

02:13:55.670 --> 02:14:02.840
There is a sort of scintillating
some field of color and shadow

02:14:02.840 --> 02:14:05.570
that's there in the darkness
of your closed eyes.

02:14:05.570 --> 02:14:10.100
And that can become
a sky-like domain

02:14:10.100 --> 02:14:17.180
of vast visual
expression that opens up

02:14:17.180 --> 02:14:21.050
as you get more concentrated
with your eyes closed right.

02:14:21.050 --> 02:14:24.620
So you can very much be aware
of seeing with your eyes closed

02:14:24.620 --> 02:14:28.330
in a meditative practice.

02:14:28.330 --> 02:14:31.770
But from the point of
view of mindfulness,

02:14:31.770 --> 02:14:36.570
the logic is not to care about
any of the interesting changes

02:14:36.570 --> 02:14:39.540
and experience that come
as a result of practicing

02:14:39.540 --> 02:14:43.190
in this way, because
the underlying goal

02:14:43.190 --> 02:14:49.390
is to be more and more
equanimous with changes.

02:14:49.390 --> 02:14:51.690
So it's not to grasp at
what's pleasant or interesting

02:14:51.690 --> 02:14:56.040
and not to push what's
unpleasant or boring

02:14:56.040 --> 02:15:00.960
or otherwise not
engaging in a way.

02:15:00.960 --> 02:15:05.070
What you want is just a kind
of a sky-like mind that just

02:15:05.070 --> 02:15:06.510
allows everything to appear.

02:15:06.510 --> 02:15:08.600
And you're not
clinging to anything

02:15:08.600 --> 02:15:11.160
or reacting to anything.

02:15:11.160 --> 02:15:13.470
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Could I ask
you what your thoughts are

02:15:13.470 --> 02:15:19.590
about the differences between
nouns, adjectives, and verbs

02:15:19.590 --> 02:15:21.925
in the context of what
we're talking about

02:15:21.925 --> 02:15:22.800
in you're describing?

02:15:22.800 --> 02:15:26.940
And the reason I bring this
up is that as you know,

02:15:26.940 --> 02:15:29.100
I know everything in
biology is a process.

02:15:29.100 --> 02:15:32.850
We would never ever
say, oh, the perception

02:15:32.850 --> 02:15:37.770
of that red line on
a painting is a noun.

02:15:37.770 --> 02:15:39.870
I mean, it's an event
in the visual system.

02:15:39.870 --> 02:15:41.910
You're abstracting
some understanding

02:15:41.910 --> 02:15:44.020
about that thing in
the outside world.

02:15:44.020 --> 02:15:49.080
And I think it's very useful
in thinking about the brain.

02:15:49.080 --> 02:15:50.340
People will notice I notice--

02:15:50.340 --> 02:15:53.370
I, excuse me, actively avoid
the use of the word mind,

02:15:53.370 --> 02:15:56.250
because I figure, especially,
with sitting across from me,

02:15:56.250 --> 02:15:59.340
that I'll step in it if I do.

02:15:59.340 --> 02:16:04.220
But the brain generates
a series of perceptions

02:16:04.220 --> 02:16:08.330
or what have you by through
processes, not nouns.

02:16:08.330 --> 02:16:11.290
And so when thinking about
biology, I think of development

02:16:11.290 --> 02:16:13.303
is an arc of processes.

02:16:13.303 --> 02:16:15.470
Aging is an arc-- perception
is an arc of processes.

02:16:15.470 --> 02:16:17.300
They just exist on
different time scales.

02:16:17.300 --> 02:16:20.210
And so a little bit
of what I'm hearing

02:16:20.210 --> 02:16:24.030
is that inside of an
effective meditation practice,

02:16:24.030 --> 02:16:27.350
there's a little bit of
certainly non-judgment

02:16:27.350 --> 02:16:31.100
but discarding of the
noun and the adjective

02:16:31.100 --> 02:16:32.750
modes of language.

02:16:32.750 --> 02:16:33.629
Like red apple.

02:16:33.629 --> 02:16:34.129
OK.

02:16:34.129 --> 02:16:36.590
It's a red apple,
but then you need

02:16:36.590 --> 02:16:38.930
to eliminate some other
adjectives about it.

02:16:38.930 --> 02:16:42.049
It's a rotten apple,
it's a ripe apple.

02:16:42.049 --> 02:16:44.870
And instead view the
appearance and disappearance

02:16:44.870 --> 02:16:49.860
of that apple as just a thing, a
process as opposed to an event.

02:16:49.860 --> 02:16:51.379
And now, events,
we could really get

02:16:51.379 --> 02:16:52.745
into the language aspect of it.

02:16:52.745 --> 02:16:55.760
That just reveals how
diminished language

02:16:55.760 --> 02:17:00.730
is to describe the workings
of the brain at some level.

02:17:00.730 --> 02:17:02.680
I don't know if any
of these resonates.

02:17:02.680 --> 02:17:05.080
But it seems to me, the
goal or one of the goals

02:17:05.080 --> 02:17:07.959
is to start to understand
the algorithm that

02:17:07.959 --> 02:17:11.410
is the fleeting nature of
perception but to not focus

02:17:11.410 --> 02:17:13.180
on any one single perception.

02:17:13.180 --> 02:17:15.700
And then to not even focused
on one single algorithm,

02:17:15.700 --> 02:17:19.120
but to, at some
level, there's a--

02:17:19.120 --> 02:17:21.910
what is revealed to
the meditator over time

02:17:21.910 --> 02:17:24.969
is some sort of
macroscopic principle

02:17:24.969 --> 02:17:30.129
about the way perceptions
work at a deeper level.

02:17:30.129 --> 02:17:34.780
That there's a deeper principle
there that sits below our--

02:17:34.780 --> 02:17:36.950
certainly our normal
everyday awareness.

02:17:36.950 --> 02:17:39.670
But that in paying attention to
the mechanics of all this stuff

02:17:39.670 --> 02:17:42.549
and not judging those mechanics,
not naming those mechanics,

02:17:42.549 --> 02:17:44.530
or just naming them
and let them pass by.

02:17:44.530 --> 02:17:46.540
That there's some
action function,

02:17:46.540 --> 02:17:48.219
some verb is revealed.

02:17:48.219 --> 02:17:50.200
And that maybe that
verb-- may be the word

02:17:50.200 --> 02:17:52.510
to describe that
verb is mindfulness.

02:17:52.510 --> 02:17:56.440
Maybe mindfulness is really
just a verb to describe that.

02:17:56.440 --> 02:17:58.362
I don't know.

02:17:58.362 --> 02:17:59.320
Is there anything here?

02:17:59.320 --> 02:18:02.530
Or am I-- I don't know
if I'm creating just

02:18:02.530 --> 02:18:04.959
like useless straw or
if there's actually

02:18:04.959 --> 02:18:06.350
a seed here of something real.

02:18:06.350 --> 02:18:08.740
But to me, any time I want
to understand something

02:18:08.740 --> 02:18:12.711
in biology or psychology, I
try and broaden the time domain

02:18:12.711 --> 02:18:14.919
and think in terms of verbs,
not nouns or adjectives.

02:18:14.919 --> 02:18:15.700
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

02:18:15.700 --> 02:18:16.270
Yeah, yeah.

02:18:16.270 --> 02:18:16.990
No.

02:18:16.990 --> 02:18:19.990
That's very useful,
and that's somewhat

02:18:19.990 --> 02:18:21.820
adjacent to this
distinction I'm making

02:18:21.820 --> 02:18:24.520
between dualistic and
non-dualistic ways

02:18:24.520 --> 02:18:26.299
of experiencing the world.

02:18:26.299 --> 02:18:29.920
So even dualistically,
everything is still a process.

02:18:29.920 --> 02:18:32.889
And we're misled
by the reification

02:18:32.889 --> 02:18:36.250
that noun talk gives us.

02:18:36.250 --> 02:18:38.980
And this applies not just to
something like mindfulness,

02:18:38.980 --> 02:18:41.080
but even to something
like the self.

02:18:41.080 --> 02:18:45.040
So the sense of self
is also a process.

02:18:45.040 --> 02:18:48.219
I mean, it's a verb,
it's not-- so we're self

02:18:48.219 --> 02:18:52.840
in more than we are ourselves.

02:18:52.840 --> 02:18:59.260
And there are-- even appropriate
uses of the term self

02:18:59.260 --> 02:19:02.650
that don't go away
even when you recognize

02:19:02.650 --> 02:19:06.669
that the core subject,
self, is an illusion.

02:19:06.669 --> 02:19:11.799
There are states of self where
you can recognize in your life

02:19:11.799 --> 02:19:16.330
that you inhabit very different
modes of being depending

02:19:16.330 --> 02:19:17.150
on the context.

02:19:17.150 --> 02:19:19.180
So like there are
moments where you--

02:19:19.180 --> 02:19:22.090
just by walking into
a certain building,

02:19:22.090 --> 02:19:24.790
you suddenly transition into
a different state of self.

02:19:24.790 --> 02:19:27.370
Like suddenly, you
pass through a door

02:19:27.370 --> 02:19:30.160
and now you are a customer
in a store, right?

02:19:30.160 --> 02:19:32.590
So we know what that
customer feeling is.

02:19:32.590 --> 02:19:35.230
Like, you're now the person
who's getting the attention.

02:19:35.230 --> 02:19:37.180
It's a very formalized
type of attention

02:19:37.180 --> 02:19:42.129
from the person who is running
the store or a restaurant.

02:19:42.129 --> 02:19:43.990
You're a customer
at a restaurant.

02:19:43.990 --> 02:19:49.030
That's a-- I just remembered
something that's funny.

02:19:49.030 --> 02:19:51.880
That was born of a
mismatch of this.

02:19:51.880 --> 02:19:54.910
I'll come back to
that in a second.

02:19:54.910 --> 02:20:00.410
So there are-- so
we go through it.

02:20:00.410 --> 02:20:02.870
You can be a student in
the presence of a teacher,

02:20:02.870 --> 02:20:04.662
you're going to be a
parent in the presence

02:20:04.662 --> 02:20:07.538
of a son or a daughter,
you can be a spouse

02:20:07.538 --> 02:20:08.830
in the presence of your spouse.

02:20:08.830 --> 02:20:12.340
And all of those shadings of--

02:20:12.340 --> 02:20:20.800
like the change in context
really does usher in some

02:20:20.800 --> 02:20:22.930
fundamental
psychological changes

02:20:22.930 --> 02:20:25.120
in just the states
of consciousness

02:20:25.120 --> 02:20:27.490
that are available to you.

02:20:27.490 --> 02:20:28.720
And some of this is really--

02:20:28.720 --> 02:20:32.770
I mean, I'm sure we could
understand a lot about this

02:20:32.770 --> 02:20:35.410
personally and generically.

02:20:35.410 --> 02:20:38.720
But it is pretty mysterious.

02:20:38.720 --> 02:20:41.430
I mean, there are
people who I know

02:20:41.430 --> 02:20:44.730
who I'm with them
in a certain way,

02:20:44.730 --> 02:20:49.740
and based on something
I'm getting off of them,

02:20:49.740 --> 02:20:51.570
I can't be that--

02:20:51.570 --> 02:20:54.300
I'm effortlessly
one way with them,

02:20:54.300 --> 02:20:58.350
and there's no way I could be
that way with somebody else.

02:20:58.350 --> 02:21:01.142
I don't know if it's the
pheromones or their facial,

02:21:01.142 --> 02:21:03.100
just the way they are,
their facial expression.

02:21:03.100 --> 02:21:07.050
But I mean, there are
people with whom I'm really

02:21:07.050 --> 02:21:09.300
kind of effortlessly
funny, and there

02:21:09.300 --> 02:21:10.950
are people with whom
I couldn't even--

02:21:10.950 --> 02:21:14.280
it would never occur to me to be
funny no matter what happened.

02:21:14.280 --> 02:21:16.740
And I have long
standing relationships

02:21:16.740 --> 02:21:22.140
with these people, so all
of that's very mysterious.

02:21:22.140 --> 02:21:25.410
But anyway, the
difference there is not

02:21:25.410 --> 02:21:29.250
in this core sense of
subject in relationship

02:21:29.250 --> 02:21:34.440
to all the objects, it's in
kind of the states of self.

02:21:34.440 --> 02:21:36.990
And all of that is very verby.

02:21:36.990 --> 02:21:39.430
All this is a
pattern of changes.

02:21:39.430 --> 02:21:40.980
It's a pattern of
what's available

02:21:40.980 --> 02:21:44.400
and what's not available,
the capacities there that

02:21:44.400 --> 02:21:48.630
come online or not in
those various contexts.

02:21:48.630 --> 02:21:50.370
But no, the memory
I just had, which

02:21:50.370 --> 02:21:52.920
I hadn't had in a long time.

02:21:52.920 --> 02:21:54.840
But it was one of
these moments where

02:21:54.840 --> 02:21:59.550
I realized the power of
these shifts in context

02:21:59.550 --> 02:22:00.840
for states of self.

02:22:00.840 --> 02:22:04.770
So once, I was a young man.

02:22:04.770 --> 02:22:11.040
I think I was probably
22 or so, and single.

02:22:11.040 --> 02:22:13.380
And like, you're just
like, trying to figure out

02:22:13.380 --> 02:22:15.000
how do you meet women?

02:22:15.000 --> 02:22:17.880
And how does one get
confident to do this well?

02:22:17.880 --> 02:22:26.550
And I walked into a restaurant
and a kind of a woman

02:22:26.550 --> 02:22:30.840
was walking toward me, toward
the front door of the restroom.

02:22:30.840 --> 02:22:33.450
But she was walking toward me
in a way where I just by default

02:22:33.450 --> 02:22:37.410
assumed she was the
hostess in the restaurant.

02:22:37.410 --> 02:22:41.620
But she wasn't the
hostess, she was just

02:22:41.620 --> 02:22:43.740
someone who had just
eaten there, I guess.

02:22:43.740 --> 02:22:45.390
So I walked through,
and she comes out.

02:22:45.390 --> 02:22:47.880
And so there's a fundamental
misunderstanding in me

02:22:47.880 --> 02:22:51.420
that's set up by literally just
this change in architecture.

02:22:51.420 --> 02:22:53.730
And so I just said
hi to her in a way

02:22:53.730 --> 02:22:56.070
that I presumably I would
say hi to any hostess who

02:22:56.070 --> 02:22:59.940
was coming up to ask me
where I wanted to sit.

02:22:59.940 --> 02:23:01.650
But what had
actually happened is

02:23:01.650 --> 02:23:04.560
I had said hi to a
total stranger in a way

02:23:04.560 --> 02:23:06.423
that I tended at
that point never

02:23:06.423 --> 02:23:08.340
to say hi to total
strangers because I was shy

02:23:08.340 --> 02:23:10.230
and it was just like that.

02:23:10.230 --> 02:23:14.370
But apparently, I gave her
like a 10,000 watt high

02:23:14.370 --> 02:23:17.130
of all of the confidence
you would have if you

02:23:17.130 --> 02:23:18.270
were that sort of person.

02:23:18.270 --> 02:23:21.420
And it just ushered
in a complete like--

02:23:21.420 --> 02:23:23.400
so I went to my
table, and this woman,

02:23:23.400 --> 02:23:26.610
I came back into the restaurant
and gave me her phone number,

02:23:26.610 --> 02:23:28.530
which was something that
was just a completely

02:23:28.530 --> 02:23:32.010
foreign experience to me,
and it was based completely

02:23:32.010 --> 02:23:35.640
on my misunderstanding of
the situation I was in.

02:23:35.640 --> 02:23:38.547
And so anyway--

02:23:38.547 --> 02:23:40.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Among
the misunderstandings

02:23:40.380 --> 02:23:43.680
that one can have and then
action and engage in life,

02:23:43.680 --> 02:23:46.350
I would say that was a
somewhat adaptive one.

02:23:46.350 --> 02:23:48.540
SAM HARRIS: Yeah, but
then you realize that, OK,

02:23:48.540 --> 02:23:50.160
but then there
are certain people

02:23:50.160 --> 02:23:53.730
who recognize this
machinery to whatever degree

02:23:53.730 --> 02:23:56.160
or have kind of natural
aptitudes for bringing

02:23:56.160 --> 02:24:02.550
certain things online or not
such that they can consciously

02:24:02.550 --> 02:24:06.360
make these states of self,
this level of gregariousness,

02:24:06.360 --> 02:24:10.650
say, available to them
in the circumstances

02:24:10.650 --> 02:24:12.270
where it's is actually
useful to them.

02:24:12.270 --> 02:24:14.430
So if you're single and
you want to meet people,

02:24:14.430 --> 02:24:18.600
well, it's actually very
helpful to feel confident enough

02:24:18.600 --> 02:24:21.330
to just go say hi to strangers
and ask them how they're doing

02:24:21.330 --> 02:24:25.980
and to be online in that way.

02:24:25.980 --> 02:24:30.785
Where at that point in my
life, in that circumstance,

02:24:30.785 --> 02:24:32.160
by default, I was
going to ignore

02:24:32.160 --> 02:24:34.680
this stranger who I was
passing by in the doorway

02:24:34.680 --> 02:24:35.590
of a restaurant.

02:24:35.590 --> 02:24:37.530
But thinking she
was the hostess,

02:24:37.530 --> 02:24:40.710
I was engaging her fully.

02:24:40.710 --> 02:24:42.810
So anyway, you can
consciously-- again,

02:24:42.810 --> 02:24:44.550
this does not invoke
free will at all.

02:24:44.550 --> 02:24:46.200
But yes, you can
consciously decide

02:24:46.200 --> 02:24:48.300
to play with these
mechanisms such

02:24:48.300 --> 02:24:53.610
that you can decide
what states of self

02:24:53.610 --> 02:24:57.000
would be more normative to have
given what you want in life.

02:24:57.000 --> 02:25:01.560
And you can become increasingly
attentive to the ways in which

02:25:01.560 --> 02:25:03.543
you get played by the world.

02:25:03.543 --> 02:25:04.710
You're a kind of instrument.

02:25:04.710 --> 02:25:06.127
Your mind is a
kind of instrument.

02:25:06.127 --> 02:25:09.000
Your brain is a kind of
instrument that is continually

02:25:09.000 --> 02:25:12.510
getting played by the
situations you are in,

02:25:12.510 --> 02:25:16.230
and you can become more
of an intelligent curator

02:25:16.230 --> 02:25:19.360
of your conscious states and
your conscious capacities

02:25:19.360 --> 02:25:21.120
just by noticing
the changes in you.

02:25:21.120 --> 02:25:24.420
Like, in graduate school,
it's something I talk about.

02:25:24.420 --> 02:25:27.070
I think at some
point in waking up,

02:25:27.070 --> 02:25:32.960
this became very stark for me
because I was an old graduate

02:25:32.960 --> 02:25:33.460
student.

02:25:33.460 --> 02:25:37.120
I had taken 11 years off at
Stanford between my sophomore

02:25:37.120 --> 02:25:37.960
and junior year.

02:25:37.960 --> 02:25:39.752
So when I went back to school--

02:25:39.752 --> 02:25:41.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Talk
about a leave of absence.

02:25:41.710 --> 02:25:43.750
SAM HARRIS: Yeah, but I
mean, Stanford had this,

02:25:43.750 --> 02:25:45.653
you might know this.

02:25:45.653 --> 02:25:48.070
They have this stop out policy
where you never really drop

02:25:48.070 --> 02:25:49.362
out, you just stop as you want.

02:25:49.362 --> 02:25:50.410
You can always go back.

02:25:50.410 --> 02:25:51.993
You don't have to
write letters saying

02:25:51.993 --> 02:25:54.220
that you still exist
every two years as you

02:25:54.220 --> 02:25:55.150
do in other schools.

02:25:55.150 --> 02:26:00.100
So anyway, I showed
up after 11 years.

02:26:00.100 --> 02:26:03.370
So I was really on a deadline,
and I felt late for everything.

02:26:03.370 --> 02:26:05.440
So I'm kind of
finishing my degree

02:26:05.440 --> 02:26:07.498
as quickly as I can
as an undergraduate,

02:26:07.498 --> 02:26:09.040
and then I jump into
graduate school,

02:26:09.040 --> 02:26:12.724
and I'm an old graduate student.

02:26:12.724 --> 02:26:14.620
There's a real sense of urgency.

02:26:14.620 --> 02:26:15.380
Like, I'm late.

02:26:15.380 --> 02:26:16.713
I should have done this earlier.

02:26:16.713 --> 02:26:18.370
I want to get this stuff done.

02:26:18.370 --> 02:26:21.190
But then 9/11 happened.

02:26:21.190 --> 02:26:26.470
And just as I had finished
my coursework getting my PhD,

02:26:26.470 --> 02:26:30.160
and I was just getting
into my research but 9/11

02:26:30.160 --> 02:26:31.777
intersected with my
life in such a way

02:26:31.777 --> 02:26:33.610
that I just had to drop
everything and write

02:26:33.610 --> 02:26:34.520
my first book.

02:26:34.520 --> 02:26:35.210
And I did that.

02:26:35.210 --> 02:26:37.210
And then I just had to
drop everything and write

02:26:37.210 --> 02:26:39.640
my second book because of the
response to the first book.

02:26:39.640 --> 02:26:41.890
And so essentially, I
had like, four years,

02:26:41.890 --> 02:26:45.520
where I was AWOL doing my PhD.

02:26:45.520 --> 02:26:47.770
But I still had a toe in
the lab and I was still

02:26:47.770 --> 02:26:48.790
showing up occasionally.

02:26:48.790 --> 02:26:51.100
But I was becoming this
kind of cautionary tale

02:26:51.100 --> 02:26:52.780
from the point of
view of grad school,

02:26:52.780 --> 02:26:57.160
but I was also becoming kind of
a famous or semi-famous writer

02:26:57.160 --> 02:27:01.240
because my first book had been
a New York Times best seller,

02:27:01.240 --> 02:27:04.710
so I was getting some
notoriety as a writer.

02:27:04.710 --> 02:27:07.127
And so I was doing things
like, I was giving a Ted Talk

02:27:07.127 --> 02:27:08.960
but I still hadn't
finished graduate school.

02:27:08.960 --> 02:27:12.363
So it was I think that
timing's is right, maybe

02:27:12.363 --> 02:27:14.030
I had just finished
graduate school when

02:27:14.030 --> 02:27:14.870
I gave the Ted Talk.

02:27:14.870 --> 02:27:19.010
But anyway, so I was
rowing in two boats

02:27:19.010 --> 02:27:21.230
and one boat was
sinking or showing

02:27:21.230 --> 02:27:23.480
every sign of being damaged.

02:27:23.480 --> 02:27:25.910
And I was literally
like, getting letters

02:27:25.910 --> 02:27:27.635
from the head of the
department saying,

02:27:27.635 --> 02:27:28.910
we're concerned about you.

02:27:28.910 --> 02:27:34.070
But on the other hand, I was
becoming a quasi celebrity

02:27:34.070 --> 02:27:38.540
in that world too, at least in
a world that was overlapping.

02:27:38.540 --> 02:27:43.310
So I was having the experience
of going in the moment where

02:27:43.310 --> 02:27:47.960
this crystallized for me
in a fairly peculiar way

02:27:47.960 --> 02:27:50.480
was, I had a meeting
at like 3:00 o'clock

02:27:50.480 --> 02:27:52.910
with my advisor who
was just this guy,

02:27:52.910 --> 02:27:55.760
Mark Cohen, in the brain
mapping center at UCLA who's

02:27:55.760 --> 02:27:58.310
a fantastic guy.

02:27:58.310 --> 02:27:59.390
Great advisor.

02:27:59.390 --> 02:28:03.590
I did not extract as much wisdom
from him as I should have.

02:28:03.590 --> 02:28:04.700
Brilliant scientist.

02:28:04.700 --> 02:28:09.380
And for him, I'm late.

02:28:09.380 --> 02:28:10.310
At least, in my head--

02:28:10.310 --> 02:28:12.650
he is not that he was riding
me so hard but in my head,

02:28:12.650 --> 02:28:14.450
I'm very self
conscious about how

02:28:14.450 --> 02:28:19.110
I'm not living up to his
expectations at this point.

02:28:19.110 --> 02:28:21.110
So I have a meeting with
him at 3:00 o'clock,

02:28:21.110 --> 02:28:25.970
and I'm just kind of
wilting under his gaze

02:28:25.970 --> 02:28:31.360
and my own imagined
inner gaze of his.

02:28:31.360 --> 02:28:34.430
But two hours later,
I have a meeting

02:28:34.430 --> 02:28:37.730
with his boss, a dinner
meeting with his boss

02:28:37.730 --> 02:28:41.383
who wants to meet with me to get
advice on launching his book.

02:28:41.383 --> 02:28:42.800
We have the same
publisher but I'm

02:28:42.800 --> 02:28:46.325
like, the much bigger
author at Norton,

02:28:46.325 --> 02:28:50.120
and he's coming
to me for advice.

02:28:50.120 --> 02:28:55.520
And so I'm ricocheting between
two diametrically opposite self

02:28:55.520 --> 02:28:58.412
states that are-- again, this
comes down to architecture.

02:28:58.412 --> 02:28:59.870
It's literally like
the state I was

02:28:59.870 --> 02:29:02.540
in walking into one building
and then leaving and walking

02:29:02.540 --> 02:29:06.710
into another building
on the same campus.

02:29:06.710 --> 02:29:12.200
And they were completely
opposite self concepts.

02:29:12.200 --> 02:29:17.000
Like, in one context,
I am a fuckup.

02:29:17.000 --> 02:29:19.520
In another context,
I'm a celebrity.

02:29:19.520 --> 02:29:22.370
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And you
have mastery and virtuosity,

02:29:22.370 --> 02:29:24.590
and we're developing
it very quickly.

02:29:24.590 --> 02:29:26.930
SAM HARRIS: But so
again, this is a kind

02:29:26.930 --> 02:29:28.440
of a stark version of that.

02:29:28.440 --> 02:29:31.040
But everyone has
some version of this

02:29:31.040 --> 02:29:34.610
just in bouncing between
talking to their mom

02:29:34.610 --> 02:29:36.290
and then talking to
their best friend

02:29:36.290 --> 02:29:40.052
and then talking to a stranger
and talking to someone who's

02:29:40.052 --> 02:29:41.510
very successful,
talking to someone

02:29:41.510 --> 02:29:43.010
who's not very successful.

02:29:43.010 --> 02:29:45.860
You notice your vulnerability
to all of this stuff.

02:29:45.860 --> 02:29:51.540
And ultimately, what
you want is a level

02:29:51.540 --> 02:29:56.760
of psychological integrity that
is truly divorceable from that.

02:29:56.760 --> 02:29:59.070
Now, I'm not I'm not
saying you're ever

02:29:59.070 --> 02:30:03.620
going to get it perfect, there's
always going to be some--

02:30:03.620 --> 02:30:07.640
I mean, I can't talk about
the ultimate fulfillment

02:30:07.640 --> 02:30:09.410
of this process.

02:30:09.410 --> 02:30:12.860
I'm not a Buddha, I'm not saying
I've finished the project.

02:30:12.860 --> 02:30:20.810
But I think there's more and
more as you become sensitive

02:30:20.810 --> 02:30:23.480
to these changes and you become
sensitive to what it's like

02:30:23.480 --> 02:30:27.320
to actually not be
psychologically reactive

02:30:27.320 --> 02:30:32.708
and not be definable by your
own self-concept, your own--

02:30:32.708 --> 02:30:34.250
you're not identifying
with anything,

02:30:34.250 --> 02:30:35.917
you're not hanging
your hat on anything,

02:30:35.917 --> 02:30:42.410
you're not thinking about
yourself in the kind of terms

02:30:42.410 --> 02:30:44.570
that you would export
to others and then

02:30:44.570 --> 02:30:47.330
care about what they
think about you.

02:30:47.330 --> 02:30:50.900
There's a kind of
invulnerability

02:30:50.900 --> 02:30:57.180
that arises that's not borne
of being well defended,

02:30:57.180 --> 02:31:00.110
it's born of being evaporated.

02:31:00.110 --> 02:31:03.185
It's like, you're no longer
keeping score in those ways.

02:31:03.185 --> 02:31:05.810
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Once
again, I really appreciate

02:31:05.810 --> 02:31:08.090
that description
because these days,

02:31:08.090 --> 02:31:10.850
I'm really intrigued
by something

02:31:10.850 --> 02:31:13.400
we've known for a long
time that you're certainly

02:31:13.400 --> 02:31:16.730
familiar with is the prefrontal
cortex's ability to establish

02:31:16.730 --> 02:31:18.380
context dependent rule sets.

02:31:18.380 --> 02:31:20.960
A Stroop task would
be basic example

02:31:20.960 --> 02:31:23.600
of reading numbers
or letters on cards

02:31:23.600 --> 02:31:25.190
and then switching
to having to report

02:31:25.190 --> 02:31:27.230
the colors that the letters
and numbers are written in.

02:31:27.230 --> 02:31:28.190
It's a basic task.

02:31:28.190 --> 02:31:31.640
But prefrontal cortex,
obviously important for setting

02:31:31.640 --> 02:31:35.000
context dependent thought and
behavior and directed action.

02:31:35.000 --> 02:31:38.660
But within the context of all
these different variations

02:31:38.660 --> 02:31:44.420
of the self, depending on
graduate school or relationship

02:31:44.420 --> 02:31:46.360
where sitting alone
in one's room,

02:31:46.360 --> 02:31:48.100
there are different
rule sets arise

02:31:48.100 --> 02:31:52.090
and somehow, we are able to have
a coherent sense of self that

02:31:52.090 --> 02:31:53.920
encompasses all of those.

02:31:53.920 --> 02:31:58.330
Functional people can toggle
between them as needed and not

02:31:58.330 --> 02:31:59.770
overlap them inappropriately.

02:31:59.770 --> 02:32:01.660
At least not to the
extent that it's

02:32:01.660 --> 02:32:03.310
career failing or life failing.

02:32:03.310 --> 02:32:08.200
Although there are sad
examples of that, many of which

02:32:08.200 --> 02:32:10.630
exist in the Twitter space.

02:32:10.630 --> 02:32:13.840
I know several colleagues,
not directly of mine,

02:32:13.840 --> 02:32:17.140
but people who threw mistakes
made with their thumbs,

02:32:17.140 --> 02:32:20.890
where they forgot
context or forgot

02:32:20.890 --> 02:32:25.000
to realize that the context on
social media is near infinite.

02:32:25.000 --> 02:32:26.770
But the context that
existed in their head

02:32:26.770 --> 02:32:29.260
might not be clear in the
way that they communicated

02:32:29.260 --> 02:32:31.480
something, and they
lost their jobs

02:32:31.480 --> 02:32:34.270
by saying what were perceived
as insensitive things.

02:32:34.270 --> 02:32:37.450
In some cases were, in fact,
offensive, insensitive things.

02:32:37.450 --> 02:32:40.970
In some cases, it's debatable.

02:32:40.970 --> 02:32:45.230
In any case, I think that the
image that now comes to mind

02:32:45.230 --> 02:32:48.440
relates to something you've said
several times, that it's not

02:32:48.440 --> 02:32:50.000
about eliminating
something, it's

02:32:50.000 --> 02:32:53.055
about revealing that something
was never actually there.

02:32:53.055 --> 02:32:54.680
And then in terms of
sensory experience

02:32:54.680 --> 02:32:56.570
and these different
aspects of the self,

02:32:56.570 --> 02:32:58.970
I have this image
in my mind of--

02:32:58.970 --> 02:33:00.650
I'm not an experienced
scuba diver,

02:33:00.650 --> 02:33:01.840
but I've done enough of it.

02:33:01.840 --> 02:33:02.870
I've worn a wetsuit.

02:33:02.870 --> 02:33:04.670
You wear a complete
wetsuit with the hood.

02:33:04.670 --> 02:33:08.540
And this idea if you were
born into that wetsuit,

02:33:08.540 --> 02:33:12.470
you might think that yeah,
you nudge up or lean up

02:33:12.470 --> 02:33:16.730
against a wall and you
experience it one way.

02:33:16.730 --> 02:33:18.680
But were you to
shed that wetsuit,

02:33:18.680 --> 02:33:21.140
you go, wow, there's
this incredible landscape

02:33:21.140 --> 02:33:24.710
of somatosensory experience
that I had no idea.

02:33:24.710 --> 02:33:26.660
It goes way beyond
levels of sensitivity.

02:33:26.660 --> 02:33:29.160
Right now you're talking about
fine two point discrimination

02:33:29.160 --> 02:33:30.590
and light strokes,
and this could

02:33:30.590 --> 02:33:33.230
be positive or negative
pain in other ways too.

02:33:33.230 --> 02:33:35.120
But what you're
describing is essentially

02:33:35.120 --> 02:33:37.880
that the wetsuit was
never really there,

02:33:37.880 --> 02:33:41.900
but was created through
a series of action steps.

02:33:41.900 --> 02:33:44.300
And I think what we're
migrating towards here

02:33:44.300 --> 02:33:51.200
is a set of for most,
non-intuitive or non-reflexive

02:33:51.200 --> 02:33:55.250
action steps that reveal
to us that in fact, we're

02:33:55.250 --> 02:33:56.720
not wearing the wetsuit.

02:33:56.720 --> 02:34:00.542
Now, you raised one
topic, which I think

02:34:00.542 --> 02:34:02.000
is analogous to
this wetsuit, which

02:34:02.000 --> 02:34:03.470
is this notion of distraction.

02:34:03.470 --> 02:34:06.110
That normally, distraction is
masking what would otherwise

02:34:06.110 --> 02:34:08.750
be a better experience of life.

02:34:11.650 --> 02:34:14.050
I can think of
distraction as falling

02:34:14.050 --> 02:34:15.400
into two different bins.

02:34:15.400 --> 02:34:17.380
One would be the
kind of distraction

02:34:17.380 --> 02:34:19.060
that is internally generated.

02:34:19.060 --> 02:34:21.460
Like, the fact that
thoughts arise and pull me

02:34:21.460 --> 02:34:25.060
down different alleyways
and avenues of my brain

02:34:25.060 --> 02:34:29.260
and my thoughts
and my experience.

02:34:29.260 --> 02:34:32.020
And that would compete
with my ability

02:34:32.020 --> 02:34:34.830
to really focus on something.

02:34:34.830 --> 02:34:36.840
And then another
form of distraction

02:34:36.840 --> 02:34:40.620
which captures my ability
to focus intensely

02:34:40.620 --> 02:34:43.170
but has me focusing
on the wrong things.

02:34:43.170 --> 02:34:46.080
And here, I think
the judgment of wrong

02:34:46.080 --> 02:34:49.650
is reasonable to include
if, for instance, I'm

02:34:49.650 --> 02:34:54.600
being impulsively yanked to
something on social media.

02:34:54.600 --> 02:34:56.850
I'm being impulsively yanked
to someone else's pain

02:34:56.850 --> 02:34:58.620
and experience and
somehow confusing

02:34:58.620 --> 02:35:00.120
that with my own experience.

02:35:00.120 --> 02:35:02.290
This isn't empathy but
just being yanked around.

02:35:02.290 --> 02:35:04.290
My attention as a spotlight
is kind of like over

02:35:04.290 --> 02:35:05.160
here, over there.

02:35:05.160 --> 02:35:06.945
I'm not feeling
as if I'm the one

02:35:06.945 --> 02:35:08.820
standing behind that
spotlight controlling it

02:35:08.820 --> 02:35:11.913
or I'm not the spotlight,
just to keep with what

02:35:11.913 --> 02:35:13.080
we've been building up here.

02:35:13.080 --> 02:35:16.800
So could you tell us a
little bit about distraction

02:35:16.800 --> 02:35:20.080
and tell me whether
or not these two

02:35:20.080 --> 02:35:23.170
forms are in any way
accurate or inaccurate?

02:35:23.170 --> 02:35:24.868
I'd be happy for them
to be inaccurate.

02:35:24.868 --> 02:35:27.160
And whether or not there are
other forms of distraction

02:35:27.160 --> 02:35:28.850
that we need to be
on the lookout for.

02:35:28.850 --> 02:35:31.210
And again, I think
what most people

02:35:31.210 --> 02:35:34.480
are seeking is, what is the way
to not just enhance our ability

02:35:34.480 --> 02:35:38.800
to focus but to shed this
wetsuit-like cloak that limits

02:35:38.800 --> 02:35:41.140
our experience that I'm
calling and that you've

02:35:41.140 --> 02:35:42.685
called distraction?

02:35:42.685 --> 02:35:47.990
SAM HARRIS: Yeah, distraction
is one component of it.

02:35:47.990 --> 02:35:51.640
The other aspect of it is
identification with thought.

02:35:51.640 --> 02:35:55.330
And the feeling of self
is bound up in the sense

02:35:55.330 --> 02:35:59.030
that I'm the thinker,
I'm the one attending,

02:35:59.030 --> 02:36:00.610
I'm the one vulnerable.

02:36:00.610 --> 02:36:05.660
I'm the inner homunculus that's
vulnerable to experience.

02:36:05.660 --> 02:36:07.960
And it can be
gratified by it or not,

02:36:07.960 --> 02:36:11.320
and it's constantly trying
to improve it or mitigate

02:36:11.320 --> 02:36:13.945
negative aspects of it.

02:36:13.945 --> 02:36:16.163
It's the sense that
there's kind of a rider

02:36:16.163 --> 02:36:17.830
on the horse of
consciousness as opposed

02:36:17.830 --> 02:36:20.420
to just consciousness
and its contents.

02:36:20.420 --> 02:36:26.600
So again, it rides atop this
illusion of control, et cetera.

02:36:26.600 --> 02:36:31.720
So to go all the way back to the
question you asked about, just

02:36:31.720 --> 02:36:35.110
what is in it, what I
recommend as a starting point

02:36:35.110 --> 02:36:38.797
for meditation.

02:36:38.797 --> 02:36:40.630
Some of your assumptions
are, in fact, true.

02:36:40.630 --> 02:36:43.510
Yes, I often recommend
at the beginning people

02:36:43.510 --> 02:36:46.420
close their eyes and you
do a sitting practice

02:36:46.420 --> 02:36:48.500
and it's different from
a walk in practice.

02:36:48.500 --> 02:36:49.958
I mean, you can do
both, but people

02:36:49.958 --> 02:36:53.200
tend to start sitting
with their eyes closed.

02:36:53.200 --> 02:36:55.870
But again, ultimately
where this is going

02:36:55.870 --> 02:36:59.350
is it's not an art
of-- meditation

02:36:59.350 --> 02:37:02.050
properly recognized is not
an artifice that you're

02:37:02.050 --> 02:37:03.370
adding to your life.

02:37:03.370 --> 02:37:08.710
It's not even a practice,
it is less rather than more.

02:37:08.710 --> 02:37:13.630
And therefore, it is also
coincident with potentially

02:37:13.630 --> 02:37:15.700
every waking moment.

02:37:15.700 --> 02:37:19.520
There's nothing that you
can do with your attention

02:37:19.520 --> 02:37:20.770
once you know how to meditate.

02:37:20.770 --> 02:37:23.650
That in principle
excludes meditation

02:37:23.650 --> 02:37:27.580
because meditation
is just a recognition

02:37:27.580 --> 02:37:30.693
of an intrinsic character of
consciousness in each moment.

02:37:30.693 --> 02:37:32.110
And all you have
in each moment is

02:37:32.110 --> 02:37:34.450
consciousness and its contents,
whatever you're doing.

02:37:37.140 --> 02:37:41.550
So in the beginning, you'll be
very deliberate and precious

02:37:41.550 --> 02:37:44.032
about deciding to
practice meditation,

02:37:44.032 --> 02:37:45.990
and you'll set aside 10
minutes in the morning,

02:37:45.990 --> 02:37:47.280
and you'll do that.

02:37:47.280 --> 02:37:49.650
And it'll seem very different
from the next 10 minutes

02:37:49.650 --> 02:37:53.310
when you're spilling
out onto your to-do list

02:37:53.310 --> 02:37:56.280
and you're trying to figure
out what the day looks like.

02:37:56.280 --> 02:37:59.280
But ultimately,
you want to erase

02:37:59.280 --> 02:38:01.890
this boundary between formal
practice and the rest of life

02:38:01.890 --> 02:38:05.385
such that it's just
not remotely findable.

02:38:08.959 --> 02:38:10.950
And that's achievable.

02:38:10.950 --> 02:38:12.690
And I think even from
the very beginning,

02:38:12.690 --> 02:38:15.990
you can relax this conceptual
distinction between meditation

02:38:15.990 --> 02:38:20.490
and its antithesis, because it's
not at the level of anything

02:38:20.490 --> 02:38:25.680
you're doing, it's at
the level of what's

02:38:25.680 --> 02:38:27.990
happening in your
relationship to thought.

02:38:27.990 --> 02:38:30.810
Like, what can you notice?

02:38:30.810 --> 02:38:36.620
It's + transition from
the by-stable percept.

02:38:36.620 --> 02:38:41.500
You're looking at the
image and you see nothing.

02:38:41.500 --> 02:38:43.510
Let's say it's the
Dalmatian, it's

02:38:43.510 --> 02:38:44.710
just the spots on the paper.

02:38:44.710 --> 02:38:47.150
And you don't see anything.

02:38:47.150 --> 02:38:49.690
And then all of a sudden the
Dalmatian or the face of Jesus

02:38:49.690 --> 02:38:53.830
or whatever the image is
pops out and then you see it,

02:38:53.830 --> 02:38:56.830
it's the transition from
nothing to something.

02:38:56.830 --> 02:38:58.630
The practice becomes
the transition

02:38:58.630 --> 02:39:03.430
from being lost in thought
and then waking up.

02:39:03.430 --> 02:39:07.690
And breaking the
spell of thought,

02:39:07.690 --> 02:39:10.900
and identification of thought
is very much like waking up

02:39:10.900 --> 02:39:14.710
from a dream and having--

02:39:14.710 --> 02:39:17.230
it's like that transition.

02:39:17.230 --> 02:39:22.645
Like you're having a
dream and couple of things

02:39:22.645 --> 02:39:23.270
are true there.

02:39:23.270 --> 02:39:29.030
I mean, it's a psychosis
that is just not--

02:39:29.030 --> 02:39:31.120
we don't problematize
because you're safely in bed

02:39:31.120 --> 02:39:32.870
and you're not moving
or unless you've got

02:39:32.870 --> 02:39:35.120
some kind of sleep disorder.

02:39:35.120 --> 02:39:37.010
You're not walking
around harming

02:39:37.010 --> 02:39:39.230
yourself or anybody else.

02:39:39.230 --> 02:39:42.950
But to be in bed
and to not know it

02:39:42.950 --> 02:39:45.710
and to think you're
running along a beach

02:39:45.710 --> 02:39:49.040
or you're getting tried for
murder in a court of law

02:39:49.040 --> 02:39:50.570
or whatever the
thing is that you're

02:39:50.570 --> 02:39:53.780
completely delusional
about, that is psychosis.

02:39:53.780 --> 02:39:55.490
And it's like
you're fundamentally

02:39:55.490 --> 02:39:56.900
unaware of your circumstance.

02:39:56.900 --> 02:39:58.730
And then two things
can happen there.

02:39:58.730 --> 02:40:01.730
You can either become lucid
within the dream, which

02:40:01.730 --> 02:40:04.070
is interesting, and there's
a whole phenomenology

02:40:04.070 --> 02:40:07.220
of that which can be practiced.

02:40:07.220 --> 02:40:09.530
But more commonly,
you can just wake up

02:40:09.530 --> 02:40:11.510
from the dream and
all of a sudden,

02:40:11.510 --> 02:40:15.480
the problem you thought
you had is no longer there,

02:40:15.480 --> 02:40:18.170
and you have a completely
different context

02:40:18.170 --> 02:40:19.970
for your conscious life.

02:40:19.970 --> 02:40:23.570
Now, you know you're safely
in bed all the while.

02:40:23.570 --> 02:40:26.660
There really is
something analogous

02:40:26.660 --> 02:40:29.810
when you break this
identification with thought.

02:40:29.810 --> 02:40:32.090
You're having a
thought that seems

02:40:32.090 --> 02:40:45.040
to be some kind of moral or
psychological emergency and yet

02:40:45.040 --> 02:40:46.780
the moment you see
daylight around,

02:40:46.780 --> 02:40:50.080
the moment you see
that the mind is larger

02:40:50.080 --> 02:40:54.910
than this mere appearance,
then suddenly you

02:40:54.910 --> 02:40:57.400
have a degree of freedom
that a moment ago was

02:40:57.400 --> 02:40:58.570
just unthinkable.

02:40:58.570 --> 02:41:03.190
And you recognize, you
sort of come to in a way,

02:41:03.190 --> 02:41:05.117
you recognize your
circumstance in a way

02:41:05.117 --> 02:41:07.450
that you weren't a moment ago
when you were just talking

02:41:07.450 --> 02:41:11.480
to yourself, when you were just
identical to that conversation.

02:41:11.480 --> 02:41:17.050
So this is all to say that
ultimately, meditation--

02:41:17.050 --> 02:41:19.910
I mean, so again, there's
another apparent paradox here.

02:41:19.910 --> 02:41:21.910
Many people who don't
know much about meditation

02:41:21.910 --> 02:41:25.500
will say things
like, well, for me,

02:41:25.500 --> 02:41:29.160
running is my meditation
or skiing or rock climbing

02:41:29.160 --> 02:41:31.980
or playing the guitar
or something they like

02:41:31.980 --> 02:41:36.060
to do that gives them an
experience of flow, that's

02:41:36.060 --> 02:41:39.270
what they go to feel better
and that's the opposite of all

02:41:39.270 --> 02:41:43.380
the chaos of their lives
or their time on Twitter

02:41:43.380 --> 02:41:44.160
or whatever it is.

02:41:47.170 --> 02:41:49.300
And virtually every
case, it's not

02:41:49.300 --> 02:41:52.180
true to say that that is
effectively meditation.

02:41:52.180 --> 02:41:53.770
By learning to play
the guitar, you're

02:41:53.770 --> 02:41:56.890
not going to learn what
I'm calling meditation.

02:41:56.890 --> 02:41:59.650
And you're not going
to learn it by cycling

02:41:59.650 --> 02:42:02.380
and getting-- no matter how good
you get at any of those things,

02:42:02.380 --> 02:42:04.505
you're not going to learn
it by doing those things.

02:42:04.505 --> 02:42:08.720
But paradoxically, I
mean, not really, but it

02:42:08.720 --> 02:42:09.860
can seem like a paradox.

02:42:09.860 --> 02:42:12.290
Once you know how to
meditate, then you

02:42:12.290 --> 02:42:14.390
can meditate doing
all of those things.

02:42:14.390 --> 02:42:16.730
Meditation is totally compatible
with playing the guitar

02:42:16.730 --> 02:42:20.750
or skiing or doing any
ordinary thing you like to do.

02:42:20.750 --> 02:42:23.090
So once you know
how to meditate,

02:42:23.090 --> 02:42:25.280
and again, it's totally
natural in the beginning

02:42:25.280 --> 02:42:28.460
to formalize it and to set
aside time each day to do it

02:42:28.460 --> 02:42:29.693
because it is a training.

02:42:29.693 --> 02:42:31.610
I mean, it is something
that in the beginning,

02:42:31.610 --> 02:42:32.740
you have to get used to.

02:42:32.740 --> 02:42:36.240
But once you're
getting used to it,

02:42:36.240 --> 02:42:39.620
then there is no good reason
not to be experiencing

02:42:39.620 --> 02:42:41.570
this thing I'm
calling meditation,

02:42:41.570 --> 02:42:47.330
this insight into the center
looseness of consciousness,

02:42:47.330 --> 02:42:51.530
the non-selfhood
of consciousness.

02:42:51.530 --> 02:42:54.740
You should experience
it when you're

02:42:54.740 --> 02:42:57.080
playing your favorite
sport or when you're having

02:42:57.080 --> 02:43:00.600
a conversation with somebody.

02:43:00.600 --> 02:43:02.880
Then to come back to
your initial assumption

02:43:02.880 --> 02:43:06.670
about eyes closed,
a lot of practice,

02:43:06.670 --> 02:43:09.150
even formal practice
can be done eyes open.

02:43:09.150 --> 02:43:11.100
And it's important
to do it as open

02:43:11.100 --> 02:43:14.430
because so much of our
anchoring of our sense of self

02:43:14.430 --> 02:43:16.240
is based on visual cues.

02:43:16.240 --> 02:43:19.140
I mean, we know that you
can if you give people

02:43:19.140 --> 02:43:21.937
the right visual cues,
you can translocate

02:43:21.937 --> 02:43:22.770
their sense of self.

02:43:22.770 --> 02:43:24.840
You can give them an
out-of-body experience

02:43:24.840 --> 02:43:28.020
with a video display,
where you can literally

02:43:28.020 --> 02:43:31.398
make them feel like there's
a body swapping illusion.

02:43:31.398 --> 02:43:33.690
You can make them feel that
they're in another person's

02:43:33.690 --> 02:43:36.960
body looking back
at their body if you

02:43:36.960 --> 02:43:38.310
run the cameras the right way.

02:43:38.310 --> 02:43:41.340
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I've done this
in VR, seeing an image of--

02:43:41.340 --> 02:43:42.930
they create an avatar
for you and then

02:43:42.930 --> 02:43:45.780
your bodily movements generate
the movements of the avatar,

02:43:45.780 --> 02:43:50.520
and you start gaining presence
as they call it in the VR lingo

02:43:50.520 --> 02:43:51.510
very quickly.

02:43:51.510 --> 02:43:54.870
And then pretty
soon, you lose sense

02:43:54.870 --> 02:43:57.120
of your own bodily
representation.

02:43:57.120 --> 02:43:59.520
And it's a little eerie.

02:43:59.520 --> 02:44:03.780
What's eerie is to me
is going back into,

02:44:03.780 --> 02:44:07.110
of course never left, but back
into your actual body when

02:44:07.110 --> 02:44:08.820
the VR goggles pop off.

02:44:08.820 --> 02:44:10.650
The world seems
almost overwhelming

02:44:10.650 --> 02:44:12.660
the number of
sensory stimuli that

02:44:12.660 --> 02:44:17.100
are in a laboratory room,
which is actually quite sparse.

02:44:17.100 --> 02:44:18.690
So exactly what
you described, this

02:44:18.690 --> 02:44:22.620
translocation of notions of
self through visual experience.

02:44:22.620 --> 02:44:24.120
SAM HARRIS: But
conversely, when you

02:44:24.120 --> 02:44:26.760
lose the sense of
self, the sense of self

02:44:26.760 --> 02:44:29.490
I'm talking about,
it can be especially

02:44:29.490 --> 02:44:32.820
vivid and salient
with eyes open.

02:44:32.820 --> 02:44:36.090
Because so many of your
reference points to selfhood

02:44:36.090 --> 02:44:38.460
are delivered
visually, especially

02:44:38.460 --> 02:44:39.580
in a social situation.

02:44:39.580 --> 02:44:42.690
So like, I'm talking to you,
you're looking back at me.

02:44:42.690 --> 02:44:45.420
So the implication
of your gaze is

02:44:45.420 --> 02:44:50.280
that I'm over here behind my
face implicated by your gaze.

02:44:50.280 --> 02:44:53.280
So the sense that you're
looking at something

02:44:53.280 --> 02:44:56.580
is the sense of self
in that social context.

02:44:56.580 --> 02:44:59.790
And if your facial expression
changes, I'm saying something

02:44:59.790 --> 02:45:02.130
and if you kind of
furrow your brow, like,

02:45:02.130 --> 02:45:05.070
what the hell is he-- and I can
read into that facial change,

02:45:05.070 --> 02:45:08.250
some interstate of yours
that is salient to me.

02:45:08.250 --> 02:45:10.500
All of a sudden, we've
got this sort of dance of.

02:45:10.500 --> 02:45:13.080
Like, I'm noticing
you reacting to me,

02:45:13.080 --> 02:45:17.590
and that's changing the way
I'm feeling about what I'm--

02:45:17.590 --> 02:45:21.600
That's the purview of
every neurosis everyone

02:45:21.600 --> 02:45:22.560
didn't want, right?

02:45:22.560 --> 02:45:24.227
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
every relationship.

02:45:24.227 --> 02:45:28.320
I had a girlfriend when I was
a post-doc who was very, very--

02:45:28.320 --> 02:45:30.600
she was brilliant,
really, still is.

02:45:30.600 --> 02:45:34.035
And she always said that
every relationship, there

02:45:34.035 --> 02:45:34.950
are four arrows.

02:45:34.950 --> 02:45:37.080
She used to say she's a
neuroscientist, still is.

02:45:37.080 --> 02:45:40.206
And said there's the arrow of--

02:45:40.206 --> 02:45:42.735
she was talking to me,
so she said, me to you

02:45:42.735 --> 02:45:44.610
and kind of what you
perceive coming from me.

02:45:44.610 --> 02:45:45.930
And then there's
you to me, and then

02:45:45.930 --> 02:45:48.720
there's an arrow from the middle
going right back at of each one

02:45:48.720 --> 02:45:50.617
of us, which is our
own perception of what

02:45:50.617 --> 02:45:52.200
the other person is
thinking about us,

02:45:52.200 --> 02:45:54.000
and it's feeding back
on the other arrow.

02:45:54.000 --> 02:45:57.720
And she gave me this
very clear but model

02:45:57.720 --> 02:46:00.300
of basically relationships.

02:46:00.300 --> 02:46:02.910
The relationship failed, but
it was good while it lasted,

02:46:02.910 --> 02:46:05.250
I should say.

02:46:05.250 --> 02:46:07.680
But the four arrow
model of relationships

02:46:07.680 --> 02:46:11.640
actually shows up in every type
of one-on-one relationship,

02:46:11.640 --> 02:46:13.260
and is probably an
under description

02:46:13.260 --> 02:46:14.790
of the total number of arrows.

02:46:14.790 --> 02:46:16.290
But is I think it's exactly
what you're describing

02:46:16.290 --> 02:46:18.660
is that perception of self
through the eyes of other

02:46:18.660 --> 02:46:21.450
whether or not we're empathic
or not strongly shapes

02:46:21.450 --> 02:46:23.750
the way that we access
different context dependent rule

02:46:23.750 --> 02:46:25.500
sets about what we're
going to say or not.

02:46:25.500 --> 02:46:27.072
And it's very dynamic, right?

02:46:27.072 --> 02:46:27.780
SAM HARRIS: Yeah.

02:46:27.780 --> 02:46:32.700
But the freedom that I think we
want and people can sometimes

02:46:32.700 --> 02:46:34.390
experience this
just haphazardly,

02:46:34.390 --> 02:46:37.590
but the center of the bull's
eye from the meditative point

02:46:37.590 --> 02:46:42.150
of view is to get off that
ride entirely and to--

02:46:42.150 --> 02:46:44.880
so that losing the sense
of self in this context

02:46:44.880 --> 02:46:53.580
of a social encounter is to
give up your face, essentially.

02:46:53.580 --> 02:46:58.560
And what that entails
is or what that gives

02:46:58.560 --> 02:47:02.580
you is the free attention to
actually just pay attention

02:47:02.580 --> 02:47:03.690
to the other person.

02:47:03.690 --> 02:47:06.780
And the other person
is now no longer quite

02:47:06.780 --> 02:47:09.660
an object in the
world for you, there's

02:47:09.660 --> 02:47:12.705
really just a kind of a totality
of which that person is a part.

02:47:16.160 --> 02:47:23.060
And actually, Martin Buber,
the mystical Jewish philosopher

02:47:23.060 --> 02:47:25.250
talked about the
I-thou relationship.

02:47:25.250 --> 02:47:28.340
And I think it's been a long
time since I've read Buber.

02:47:28.340 --> 02:47:32.240
But and I don't know
if he goes far enough

02:47:32.240 --> 02:47:35.420
to be truly non-dualistic, but
there's distinction between I

02:47:35.420 --> 02:47:40.670
and thou because the thou
part of it is, I think,

02:47:40.670 --> 02:47:42.550
potentially this.

02:47:42.550 --> 02:47:46.130
Again, it's been several
decades since I read him.

02:47:46.130 --> 02:47:51.950
But there's a way of
beholding another person where

02:47:51.950 --> 02:47:57.440
you have the free attention
to simply behold them.

02:47:57.440 --> 02:48:00.830
You no longer care what
they think about you.

02:48:00.830 --> 02:48:04.790
You don't feel neurotically
implicated by their gaze.

02:48:04.790 --> 02:48:08.600
You don't feel-- you're simply
the space in which they're

02:48:08.600 --> 02:48:10.550
appearing, and so you're free.

02:48:14.600 --> 02:48:20.570
So by definition, you're
no longer self-conscious.

02:48:20.570 --> 02:48:22.400
And this phrase,
self-consciousness, really

02:48:22.400 --> 02:48:25.850
does get at this,
what I'm calling

02:48:25.850 --> 02:48:29.330
the self, the illusory self
as a kind of contraction.

02:48:29.330 --> 02:48:31.610
And you can notice
this for yourself.

02:48:31.610 --> 02:48:35.510
Just imagine what
it's like to go

02:48:35.510 --> 02:48:39.740
from not being self-conscious to
suddenly being self-conscious.

02:48:39.740 --> 02:48:44.120
And the proximate
cause of this almost

02:48:44.120 --> 02:48:48.110
invariably is suddenly
recognizing that somebody

02:48:48.110 --> 02:48:49.100
is looking at you.

02:48:49.100 --> 02:48:51.935
It's like you're in a
Starbucks and you're alone

02:48:51.935 --> 02:48:54.060
and you're reading the
newspaper or whatever it is.

02:48:54.060 --> 02:48:56.750
And this now sounds
highly anachronistic.

02:48:56.750 --> 02:48:59.840
It's been three years since
I've held a physical newspaper

02:48:59.840 --> 02:49:01.430
in a Starbucks.

02:49:01.430 --> 02:49:04.370
But you're just minding
your own business.

02:49:04.370 --> 02:49:07.400
And you look up,
and you're seeing

02:49:07.400 --> 02:49:09.360
a room full of strangers.

02:49:09.360 --> 02:49:13.460
But then you notice that
someone is just looking at you.

02:49:13.460 --> 02:49:16.250
And so that moment
of eye contact,

02:49:16.250 --> 02:49:20.600
suddenly that throws you back on
yourself as a kind of suddenly,

02:49:20.600 --> 02:49:23.990
you're the object in the
world for that other person.

02:49:23.990 --> 02:49:29.730
That recognition is
the tightening there,

02:49:29.730 --> 02:49:36.510
the kind of contraction there
is a further ramification

02:49:36.510 --> 02:49:42.900
of this feeling most of us
have most of the time of being

02:49:42.900 --> 02:49:46.935
the center of experience.

02:49:46.935 --> 02:49:49.950
It's like we're all
walking around with a fist.

02:49:49.950 --> 02:49:51.690
And in moments of
self-consciousness,

02:49:51.690 --> 02:49:53.820
the fist gets really tight.

02:49:53.820 --> 02:49:59.070
And that's the thing that gets
fully relaxed when you discover

02:49:59.070 --> 02:50:03.870
this, what in various point's
called the nature of mind

02:50:03.870 --> 02:50:06.690
or the non-dual nature
of consciousness.

02:50:06.690 --> 02:50:09.630
It's just that there is no
center to this experience.

02:50:09.630 --> 02:50:11.970
And when you
recognize no center,

02:50:11.970 --> 02:50:15.960
then even when your gaze is
aimed at another person's gaze,

02:50:15.960 --> 02:50:18.615
there is no implication
going back to the center

02:50:18.615 --> 02:50:19.740
because there is no center.

02:50:19.740 --> 02:50:25.410
And rather than that being an
experience of weird detachment

02:50:25.410 --> 02:50:29.760
or confusion, it's
actually an experience

02:50:29.760 --> 02:50:34.820
of greater relationship because
you're no longer defending it.

02:50:34.820 --> 02:50:37.350
You're not defending
anything over here.

02:50:37.350 --> 02:50:39.240
You're not braced
against anything,

02:50:39.240 --> 02:50:42.730
you're just the space in which
that person is showing up.

02:50:42.730 --> 02:50:46.140
And so it's an
experience of being

02:50:46.140 --> 02:50:50.580
much more comfortable in the
presence of another person,

02:50:50.580 --> 02:50:56.190
whatever your relationship,
because you're not contracting.

02:50:56.190 --> 02:50:57.660
Again, and this is meditation.

02:50:57.660 --> 02:51:00.180
This is meditation that is
totally compatible with having

02:51:00.180 --> 02:51:02.310
a conversation with somebody.

02:51:02.310 --> 02:51:04.860
And then when you notice
your self contracting,

02:51:04.860 --> 02:51:08.760
when you notice you're
not meditating anymore,

02:51:08.760 --> 02:51:09.840
you're actually reacting.

02:51:09.840 --> 02:51:12.210
Like, they just said something
or looked a certain way

02:51:12.210 --> 02:51:15.210
and now, you're cast
back upon yourself

02:51:15.210 --> 02:51:19.100
in relationship to
them, that becomes

02:51:19.100 --> 02:51:21.110
a kind of mindfulness alarm.

02:51:21.110 --> 02:51:27.050
Then it becomes the
unsatisfactoriness of that.

02:51:27.050 --> 02:51:29.405
Psychologically becomes
more and more salient.

02:51:33.330 --> 02:51:35.947
Because one, that's not
the way you want to be.

02:51:35.947 --> 02:51:37.530
I mean, it's like,
it's the antithesis

02:51:37.530 --> 02:51:40.620
of it being as comfortable
as you were a moment ago.

02:51:40.620 --> 02:51:46.540
But two, it's something
you're doing unnecessarily.

02:51:46.540 --> 02:51:49.070
it's like, again, you're
making a fist when

02:51:49.070 --> 02:51:50.320
you don't have to make a fist.

02:51:53.020 --> 02:51:55.180
Again, you can leave aside
all those circumstances

02:51:55.180 --> 02:51:57.310
where it's appropriate
to react to someone.

02:51:57.310 --> 02:52:00.700
And I'm into martial
arts and self defense.

02:52:00.700 --> 02:52:03.580
And yes, you're not supposed
to be just this puddle of goo

02:52:03.580 --> 02:52:06.010
out in the world who can be
just mistreated by people

02:52:06.010 --> 02:52:08.650
and never put up resistance.

02:52:08.650 --> 02:52:16.430
But it's psychologically
even if a state like anger

02:52:16.430 --> 02:52:20.900
or contraction is sometimes
normative and appropriate,

02:52:20.900 --> 02:52:24.725
the question is, how long is it
normative and appropriate for?

02:52:24.725 --> 02:52:27.950
How long do you want
to stay angry for?

02:52:27.950 --> 02:52:32.670
In my experience, these kind of
classically negative emotions

02:52:32.670 --> 02:52:37.770
like anger and fear are
appropriate as salience cues.

02:52:37.770 --> 02:52:42.570
They orient you to an emergency
or a potential emergency.

02:52:42.570 --> 02:52:45.630
But then in dealing
with the emergency,

02:52:45.630 --> 02:52:50.100
they're almost never the
state you want to be in.

02:52:50.100 --> 02:52:54.720
Like, it's better to actually be
calm in an emergency in a way.

02:52:54.720 --> 02:52:57.120
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, absolutely.

02:52:57.120 --> 02:53:00.880
And again, the language is
insufficient to describe

02:53:00.880 --> 02:53:02.220
what you're telling us.

02:53:02.220 --> 02:53:04.740
But I think what
comes to mind for me

02:53:04.740 --> 02:53:07.380
is this distinction between
situational awareness

02:53:07.380 --> 02:53:08.340
and self-awareness.

02:53:08.340 --> 02:53:11.310
And we need both
but under conditions

02:53:11.310 --> 02:53:17.790
of emergency, true emergency,
or motivated desire.

02:53:17.790 --> 02:53:23.700
We need to dial down the
amount of self-awareness

02:53:23.700 --> 02:53:26.280
in order to be more effective
within the situational

02:53:26.280 --> 02:53:27.480
awareness.

02:53:27.480 --> 02:53:29.580
But you said something
very important.

02:53:29.580 --> 02:53:32.310
And my lab has been
working on fear-like states

02:53:32.310 --> 02:53:36.045
for a long time, so I confess
I'm going to rob this from you,

02:53:36.045 --> 02:53:37.920
but I'll credit you
every time I describe it,

02:53:37.920 --> 02:53:43.890
is that the fear of the
threat detection state

02:53:43.890 --> 02:53:48.450
or set of events acts
as a flag but is not

02:53:48.450 --> 02:53:52.260
meant to persist in the
way that the flag went up

02:53:52.260 --> 02:53:55.410
if one is to be in their
most adaptive state.

02:53:55.410 --> 02:53:57.660
Actually, Jocko Willink and
I were talking about this.

02:53:57.660 --> 02:54:00.240
He talks a lot about detachment
and open gaze, things

02:54:00.240 --> 02:54:02.730
that my lab is interested in
visual system and autonomic

02:54:02.730 --> 02:54:03.430
interactions.

02:54:03.430 --> 02:54:05.640
So why broadening
the gaze literally

02:54:05.640 --> 02:54:07.365
broadens the time
domain of thinking

02:54:07.365 --> 02:54:09.420
and you come up
with new solutions

02:54:09.420 --> 02:54:13.320
to complex problems in
real time and so on.

02:54:13.320 --> 02:54:15.420
And you're describing
an everyday set

02:54:15.420 --> 02:54:17.430
of interactions where
that could be very useful.

02:54:17.430 --> 02:54:21.480
And yet, there seems to be
something that about the way

02:54:21.480 --> 02:54:25.170
you describe meditation and
what you've managed to arrive at

02:54:25.170 --> 02:54:28.740
and what practitioners of
meditation can arrive at,

02:54:28.740 --> 02:54:31.090
which is something
more than that.

02:54:31.090 --> 02:54:33.997
It's not just about being
effective or optimizing,

02:54:33.997 --> 02:54:36.330
all the language we see thrown
around a lot in the space

02:54:36.330 --> 02:54:41.640
that I live in these days, but
something fundamentally more

02:54:41.640 --> 02:54:47.070
important about how to
experience life and the self.

02:54:47.070 --> 02:54:49.140
This realization
that what you thought

02:54:49.140 --> 02:54:51.090
was there was
never really there,

02:54:51.090 --> 02:54:54.670
but that there are
constraints that limit that.

02:54:54.670 --> 02:54:58.650
And so to try and fracture
those constraints one by one.

02:54:58.650 --> 02:55:01.260
Would you say that
meditation as a practice done

02:55:01.260 --> 02:55:03.660
for a few minutes each day
or with the app, that it's

02:55:03.660 --> 02:55:05.490
kind of a step function?

02:55:05.490 --> 02:55:07.815
Is it very non-linear in
terms of people's progress?

02:55:10.440 --> 02:55:14.970
I'm certainly going to go start
doing more meditation based

02:55:14.970 --> 02:55:16.560
on this discussion, truly.

02:55:16.560 --> 02:55:19.230
Because any time
someone describes

02:55:19.230 --> 02:55:23.700
that there's kind of a myth
that we've been living in,

02:55:23.700 --> 02:55:27.150
I become obsessed with the
idea of dissolving that myth.

02:55:27.150 --> 02:55:29.760
That's a very seductive
phrase, so thank you

02:55:29.760 --> 02:55:30.563
for using that one.

02:55:30.563 --> 02:55:31.980
There is no better
marketing tool,

02:55:31.980 --> 02:55:34.188
which is I realize what
you're not trying to do here.

02:55:34.188 --> 02:55:37.050
But that's for me to
capture my efforts.

02:55:37.050 --> 02:55:39.580
You tell me that there's
a myth that I'm living in

02:55:39.580 --> 02:55:41.970
and that it can be
dissolved, and that opens up

02:55:41.970 --> 02:55:44.610
a better landscape.

02:55:44.610 --> 02:55:46.740
What is the process like?

02:55:46.740 --> 02:55:49.320
Do some people make
progress very quickly?

02:55:49.320 --> 02:55:52.890
Do some people experience
kind of step functions

02:55:52.890 --> 02:55:54.120
towards progress?

02:55:54.120 --> 02:55:58.950
What does the meditation
practice look like over time?

02:55:58.950 --> 02:56:01.170
Do you still meditate or
have you just threaded it

02:56:01.170 --> 02:56:04.060
through your jujitsu, your
writing, your daily life,

02:56:04.060 --> 02:56:06.465
your coffee, your time
with your wife, et cetera?

02:56:06.465 --> 02:56:09.240
SAM HARRIS: Yeah,
also just to come back

02:56:09.240 --> 02:56:10.740
to talk about the
myth for a second.

02:56:10.740 --> 02:56:14.610
So what you just enunciated
was a kind of a second doorway

02:56:14.610 --> 02:56:16.000
into this whole project.

02:56:16.000 --> 02:56:21.787
So like, the usual door is
through the door of suffering,

02:56:21.787 --> 02:56:22.870
for lack of a better word.

02:56:22.870 --> 02:56:26.770
When people feel unhappy in
a variety of ways and they

02:56:26.770 --> 02:56:28.660
get more sensitized
to the mechanics

02:56:28.660 --> 02:56:30.040
of their own unhappiness.

02:56:30.040 --> 02:56:32.410
And meditation is
one of the things

02:56:32.410 --> 02:56:35.050
on the menu that
is explicitly built

02:56:35.050 --> 02:56:38.830
as a remedy for unhappiness.

02:56:38.830 --> 02:56:39.520
And it is.

02:56:39.520 --> 02:56:41.290
And that's I think
that's probably

02:56:41.290 --> 02:56:42.670
the most common path to this.

02:56:42.670 --> 02:56:45.562
But another path is just
intellectual interest.

02:56:45.562 --> 02:56:47.020
I mean, just wanting
to know what's

02:56:47.020 --> 02:56:51.940
real about the mind subjectively
in a first person way.

02:56:51.940 --> 02:56:54.250
And there's no contradiction
between those two things,

02:56:54.250 --> 02:56:55.670
and I'm motivated
by both of them.

02:56:55.670 --> 02:57:02.490
But it's a totally
valid doorway into this.

02:57:02.490 --> 02:57:03.990
There are definitely
step functions.

02:57:03.990 --> 02:57:08.100
I mean, I would say
there are at least two,

02:57:08.100 --> 02:57:11.880
and they really are
articulated along

02:57:11.880 --> 02:57:15.060
the lines of the
framework I've been

02:57:15.060 --> 02:57:18.750
describing of dualistic and
non-dualistic mindfulness.

02:57:18.750 --> 02:57:21.810
So in the beginning,
you're going to start out--

02:57:21.810 --> 02:57:26.460
99.9% of people will start out
dualistically paying attention

02:57:26.460 --> 02:57:28.740
and noticing the difference
between being distracted

02:57:28.740 --> 02:57:32.710
by thought and then being
on the object of attention,

02:57:32.710 --> 02:57:35.410
whether it's the breath
or sounds or whatever.

02:57:35.410 --> 02:57:39.000
And eventually, that opens
up to all possible objects

02:57:39.000 --> 02:57:41.070
of attention,
including thoughts.

02:57:41.070 --> 02:57:42.930
And there's still
this fluctuation

02:57:42.930 --> 02:57:47.520
between being distracted and
then being mindful of whatever.

02:57:47.520 --> 02:57:50.220
And the fact that it's open
to all possible objects

02:57:50.220 --> 02:57:51.990
differentiates this
type of practice

02:57:51.990 --> 02:57:54.330
from anything that is
narrowly focused on one

02:57:54.330 --> 02:57:57.060
object like a mantra
or a visualization

02:57:57.060 --> 02:57:59.010
as I was just saying.

02:57:59.010 --> 02:58:00.780
Those are other
paths of practice

02:58:00.780 --> 02:58:03.520
that are more
concentration-based and

02:58:03.520 --> 02:58:04.020
interesting.

02:58:04.020 --> 02:58:09.010
But the benefit of mindfulness
is that very quickly, you

02:58:09.010 --> 02:58:11.530
realize it's by definition
compatible with all possible

02:58:11.530 --> 02:58:15.070
experience, because
you're not artificially

02:58:15.070 --> 02:58:17.320
contracting your attention
down to something,

02:58:17.320 --> 02:58:19.570
you're just being aware
of the next thing.

02:58:19.570 --> 02:58:23.620
A sight, a sound,
a taste, a thought.

02:58:23.620 --> 02:58:29.550
So the first step function
is to very clearly experience

02:58:29.550 --> 02:58:32.460
the difference between
being lost in thought

02:58:32.460 --> 02:58:35.950
and being clearly aware
of any part of experience,

02:58:35.950 --> 02:58:36.960
including thought.

02:58:36.960 --> 02:58:41.520
And to notice the freedom,
the comparative psychological

02:58:41.520 --> 02:58:42.810
freedom that gives.

02:58:42.810 --> 02:58:45.893
So something's made you
angry and now you're

02:58:45.893 --> 02:58:47.310
thinking about all
the reasons why

02:58:47.310 --> 02:58:49.477
you should be angry and
have every right to be angry

02:58:49.477 --> 02:58:52.470
and what you're going to tell
that person when you see them,

02:58:52.470 --> 02:58:54.420
and then you notice
you're thinking.

02:58:54.420 --> 02:58:57.120
And you notice the connection
between the thought

02:58:57.120 --> 02:58:58.230
and the anger.

02:58:58.230 --> 02:59:02.760
Like, the minute spent lost
in thought about what's

02:59:02.760 --> 02:59:04.890
making you angry
is the thing that

02:59:04.890 --> 02:59:09.270
dragged through the
physiology of anger.

02:59:09.270 --> 02:59:13.170
And the moment you notice
that once you're mindful,

02:59:13.170 --> 02:59:16.200
once you can be mindful, you
can notice thought as thought

02:59:16.200 --> 02:59:18.570
and how quickly that dissipates.

02:59:18.570 --> 02:59:20.460
That's just the language
and the imagery.

02:59:20.460 --> 02:59:22.900
You couldn't hold on
to it if you wanted to.

02:59:22.900 --> 02:59:25.080
And then you notice the
physiology of the anger

02:59:25.080 --> 02:59:30.090
is just this kind
of meaningless,

02:59:30.090 --> 02:59:32.890
kind of inner incandescence
that has its own half

02:59:32.890 --> 02:59:34.890
life and degrades very,
very quickly when you're

02:59:34.890 --> 02:59:36.390
no longer thinking
about the reasons

02:59:36.390 --> 02:59:37.590
why you should be angry.

02:59:37.590 --> 02:59:41.460
You can't hold on to the anger,
the anger itself dissipates.

02:59:41.460 --> 02:59:44.790
And from the point of view of
the one who's being mindful,

02:59:44.790 --> 02:59:46.530
this is tremendous relief.

02:59:46.530 --> 02:59:48.600
And at minimum, it's
a degree of freedom.

02:59:48.600 --> 02:59:51.570
You can at that point
decide, well, how long

02:59:51.570 --> 02:59:53.070
do I want to be angry for?

02:59:53.070 --> 02:59:54.250
Is it useful to stay angry?

02:59:54.250 --> 02:59:56.542
Do I want to be angry for
one minute, two minutes, five

02:59:56.542 --> 02:59:58.020
minutes, 10 minutes?

02:59:58.020 --> 03:00:01.740
And before you have that
capacity to be mindful,

03:00:01.740 --> 03:00:03.488
you're going to
helplessly be as angry

03:00:03.488 --> 03:00:05.280
as you're going to be
for as long as you're

03:00:05.280 --> 03:00:08.910
going to be that way
just based on the time

03:00:08.910 --> 03:00:11.100
course of your thinking
about it, brooding about it,

03:00:11.100 --> 03:00:14.412
telling your wife about it.

03:00:14.412 --> 03:00:18.090
It's just going to be this
conversation-based misadventure

03:00:18.090 --> 03:00:23.940
in negative states
of mind, and you

03:00:23.940 --> 03:00:26.040
are going to be the
hostage of that for as long

03:00:26.040 --> 03:00:27.415
as you'll be the
hostage of that.

03:00:27.415 --> 03:00:29.430
You have nothing
you can do apart

03:00:29.430 --> 03:00:32.730
from just deciding to check
out and watch Game of Thrones

03:00:32.730 --> 03:00:34.650
again for the third time.

03:00:34.650 --> 03:00:38.460
You can divert your attention
to something else, which is

03:00:38.460 --> 03:00:40.290
sometimes a good thing to do.

03:00:40.290 --> 03:00:43.260
But mindfulness, even
dualistic mindfulness

03:00:43.260 --> 03:00:48.090
gives you this capacity to just
observe the mechanics of this

03:00:48.090 --> 03:00:52.400
and then get off the
ride whenever you want.

03:00:52.400 --> 03:00:53.980
So that really is
a step function.

03:00:53.980 --> 03:00:56.830
First, there was a time
before you could do that,

03:00:56.830 --> 03:01:00.110
and then there's a time
after which you can do that.

03:01:00.110 --> 03:01:05.220
The other step function
is noticing that there

03:01:05.220 --> 03:01:07.380
is no one who is doing that.

03:01:07.380 --> 03:01:10.500
I mean, this is the
non-duality, the selflessness,

03:01:10.500 --> 03:01:12.540
the centralessness of awareness.

03:01:12.540 --> 03:01:15.270
The fact that there's no place
from which the mindfulness is

03:01:15.270 --> 03:01:18.150
being aimed but the
fact that there's just

03:01:18.150 --> 03:01:20.820
this open condition in which
everything is appearing,

03:01:20.820 --> 03:01:23.390
thoughts included.

03:01:23.390 --> 03:01:29.940
To have you at that point, your
mindfulness no longer becomes--

03:01:29.940 --> 03:01:34.710
it's no longer this
dualistic effort

03:01:34.710 --> 03:01:37.890
to strategically pay
attention to anything

03:01:37.890 --> 03:01:40.440
as opposed to being
lost in thought,

03:01:40.440 --> 03:01:46.710
it's just what's left when
the present recognized

03:01:46.710 --> 03:01:48.600
thought unravels.

03:01:48.600 --> 03:01:51.180
Even before it unravels,
what's recognized

03:01:51.180 --> 03:01:54.990
is you are simply identical
to the condition in which

03:01:54.990 --> 03:01:56.590
everything is appearing.

03:01:56.590 --> 03:02:02.100
Now, again, I'm not making
a Deepak Chopra-like

03:02:02.100 --> 03:02:04.860
metaphysical claim
about the mind.

03:02:04.860 --> 03:02:07.530
I'm not saying the mind isn't
what the brain is doing,

03:02:07.530 --> 03:02:09.477
and I'm not saying
that you're recognizing

03:02:09.477 --> 03:02:11.560
the consciousness that
gave birth to the universe.

03:02:11.560 --> 03:02:14.970
I'm not making any broad
claims about metaphysics,

03:02:14.970 --> 03:02:19.360
I'm just talking about as
a matter of experience.

03:02:19.360 --> 03:02:22.533
There is just this condition in
which everything is appearing.

03:02:22.533 --> 03:02:23.950
And what you're
calling your body,

03:02:23.950 --> 03:02:25.367
again, as a matter
of experience--

03:02:25.367 --> 03:02:28.030
I'm not saying that we
can't have third person

03:02:28.030 --> 03:02:31.390
conversations about physical
bodies in the physical world.

03:02:31.390 --> 03:02:34.090
But as a matter of
experience, the only body

03:02:34.090 --> 03:02:38.710
you're ever going to directly
encounter as your own

03:02:38.710 --> 03:02:40.660
is in appearance
and consciousness.

03:02:40.660 --> 03:02:44.120
So consciousness is
not in your body,

03:02:44.120 --> 03:02:47.170
what you're calling your
body is in consciousness.

03:02:47.170 --> 03:02:50.980
Visually, proprioceptively,
it's like everything is just

03:02:50.980 --> 03:02:52.220
appearing in this condition.

03:02:52.220 --> 03:02:56.320
And again, this
is not a spotlight

03:02:56.320 --> 03:02:59.620
that you're aiming
at the body, there's

03:02:59.620 --> 03:03:03.250
just this condition in which
everything, including anything

03:03:03.250 --> 03:03:05.060
you could call
yourself is appearing.

03:03:05.060 --> 03:03:11.010
And so yeah, the
second step function

03:03:11.010 --> 03:03:16.090
is to recognize that
this is already true.

03:03:16.090 --> 03:03:18.160
Consciousness is already
without this thing

03:03:18.160 --> 03:03:22.990
you've been calling your
ego and hoping to unravel it

03:03:22.990 --> 03:03:25.740
through meditation.

03:03:25.740 --> 03:03:29.100
Consciousness is not going to
get any more selfless, any more

03:03:29.100 --> 03:03:31.860
centerless, any freer
than it always already

03:03:31.860 --> 03:03:34.990
is recognized as such.

03:03:34.990 --> 03:03:38.730
And so the step
function at that point

03:03:38.730 --> 03:03:41.410
is your mindfulness
at that point,

03:03:41.410 --> 03:03:44.200
the thing you come back to when
you're no longer distracted

03:03:44.200 --> 03:03:46.570
is that recognition
again and again.

03:03:46.570 --> 03:03:50.780
And then it becomes compatible
with anything you would do.

03:03:50.780 --> 03:03:52.360
And so to answer
your question, yes,

03:03:52.360 --> 03:03:57.610
I still practice formally,
sometimes, frequently.

03:03:57.610 --> 03:04:01.930
But I definitely miss days
and I don't do it for--

03:04:01.930 --> 03:04:04.510
I mean, I don't rule
out the possibility

03:04:04.510 --> 03:04:07.120
that I will go back on
retreat at various times

03:04:07.120 --> 03:04:10.240
just to check in with that and
see if that makes a difference.

03:04:10.240 --> 03:04:17.420
But I tend to sit for--

03:04:17.420 --> 03:04:20.840
I mean, I've designed
my life so that I

03:04:20.840 --> 03:04:24.080
can spend a lot
of time meditating

03:04:24.080 --> 03:04:26.070
without having to be
formally meditating.

03:04:26.070 --> 03:04:29.263
Like, so, I'll go for
a hike for two hours.

03:04:29.263 --> 03:04:30.680
And what I'm doing
when I'm hiking

03:04:30.680 --> 03:04:33.620
is identical to what I'm doing
when I'm quote, "meditating",

03:04:33.620 --> 03:04:38.360
sitting in a chair, doing
nothing but meditate.

03:04:38.360 --> 03:04:46.220
So yeah, I'm very
interested in erasing

03:04:46.220 --> 03:04:49.370
the boundary between what
people are calling meditation

03:04:49.370 --> 03:04:50.520
and the rest of life.

03:04:50.520 --> 03:04:53.782
And so in teaching
these things, I

03:04:53.782 --> 03:04:55.490
tend to emphasize that
from the beginning

03:04:55.490 --> 03:05:00.085
because I think it's
very easy to set up--

03:05:03.930 --> 03:05:06.870
to get gold by a
bunch of assumptions

03:05:06.870 --> 03:05:11.520
that cause you to be
very split in your sense

03:05:11.520 --> 03:05:13.110
of what your life is about.

03:05:13.110 --> 03:05:16.650
And I'm sort of banking
my meditation over here

03:05:16.650 --> 03:05:19.410
because I'm meditating two
hours a day diligently,

03:05:19.410 --> 03:05:21.720
and it's going to be
really good for me.

03:05:21.720 --> 03:05:25.850
And then over here, this is the
rest of my life, which is not

03:05:25.850 --> 03:05:29.000
nearly as wise or as
useful or as like,

03:05:29.000 --> 03:05:32.160
this is the stuff that is
still the area of my problems.

03:05:32.160 --> 03:05:39.370
And I think it's useful to
recognize you've got one life,

03:05:39.370 --> 03:05:43.750
and you've got this single
condition of consciousness,

03:05:43.750 --> 03:05:46.370
and its contents in
every mode of life.

03:05:46.370 --> 03:05:48.610
And there's something
to recognize about it.

03:05:48.610 --> 03:05:50.620
And you're always free
to recognize that.

03:05:50.620 --> 03:05:52.810
And truly even in your dreams.

03:05:52.810 --> 03:05:55.180
I mean, it never stops.

03:05:55.180 --> 03:05:57.803
So that's what I
tend to emphasize.

03:05:57.803 --> 03:05:59.470
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
earlier, you told us

03:05:59.470 --> 03:06:02.680
that meditation is not
about changing the content

03:06:02.680 --> 03:06:04.000
of conscious experience.

03:06:04.000 --> 03:06:07.090
And in a different
podcast that you were on,

03:06:07.090 --> 03:06:11.320
I heard you say something to
the effect of that normally we

03:06:11.320 --> 03:06:13.810
are in our daily experience
and unless we are trained

03:06:13.810 --> 03:06:17.350
in meditation, unless
we've dissolved

03:06:17.350 --> 03:06:24.550
this illusion of the gap between
actor and self and observer,

03:06:24.550 --> 03:06:31.000
that we require certain sensory
events to create collisions

03:06:31.000 --> 03:06:34.990
within us and with the natural
world that sort of blast us

03:06:34.990 --> 03:06:38.210
into a different mode of being.

03:06:38.210 --> 03:06:40.600
I want to use that
as a way to frame up

03:06:40.600 --> 03:06:44.440
this idea that some things
such as psychedelics

03:06:44.440 --> 03:06:51.520
but also a very long hike,
a very long fast, who knows?

03:06:51.520 --> 03:06:55.090
A banquet, different
types of life experiences

03:06:55.090 --> 03:06:58.270
do exactly the opposite of what
you're describing meditation

03:06:58.270 --> 03:07:00.550
does, which is
that they actively

03:07:00.550 --> 03:07:03.340
change the content of
our conscious experience

03:07:03.340 --> 03:07:05.410
so much so that we
often remember those

03:07:05.410 --> 03:07:08.500
for the rest of our lives.

03:07:08.500 --> 03:07:14.510
Could you tell us why
psychedelics can be useful?

03:07:14.510 --> 03:07:16.660
And here, I'll give the
caveats that maybe you'll

03:07:16.660 --> 03:07:20.470
feel obligated to give as well,
but we're talking about safely

03:07:20.470 --> 03:07:22.540
and responsibly,
age-appropriate,

03:07:22.540 --> 03:07:26.200
context-appropriate, ideally
with some clinical or other

03:07:26.200 --> 03:07:30.130
type of guidance, legality
issues, obeyed, et cetera.

03:07:30.130 --> 03:07:33.460
All that stated, it
was psychedelics to me

03:07:33.460 --> 03:07:36.970
are an experience of
altered perception,

03:07:36.970 --> 03:07:39.820
internal and external
perception, altered space time

03:07:39.820 --> 03:07:41.440
relationship,
somewhat dreamlike.

03:07:41.440 --> 03:07:43.690
I think it was Alan Hobson at
Harvard for a long time talked

03:07:43.690 --> 03:07:45.940
about the relationship between
psychedelic-like states

03:07:45.940 --> 03:07:50.470
and dream-like states because
of this distortion of space time

03:07:50.470 --> 03:07:52.630
dimensionality.

03:07:52.630 --> 03:07:57.610
And I haven't experimented
with them much.

03:07:57.610 --> 03:08:00.580
I've been part of a clinical
trial, three doses of MDMA,

03:08:00.580 --> 03:08:03.520
which certainly
altered the quality

03:08:03.520 --> 03:08:05.260
of my conscious
experience in ways

03:08:05.260 --> 03:08:07.630
that led to a lot of
lasting and at least for me,

03:08:07.630 --> 03:08:09.203
valuable learning.

03:08:09.203 --> 03:08:11.620
So what are your what are your
thoughts about psychedelics

03:08:11.620 --> 03:08:13.840
in terms of how they
intersect with the discussion

03:08:13.840 --> 03:08:15.370
that we've been having?

03:08:15.370 --> 03:08:20.170
And what utility do they play
in recognition of the self

03:08:20.170 --> 03:08:23.100
or in other sorts
of brain changes?

03:08:23.100 --> 03:08:27.520
SAM HARRIS: Well so, let's
just price in all those caveats

03:08:27.520 --> 03:08:30.840
that people can anticipate.

03:08:30.840 --> 03:08:32.880
These drugs are not
without their risks.

03:08:32.880 --> 03:08:38.310
And one problem is that we
have this single-term drugs

03:08:38.310 --> 03:08:40.920
or psychedelics, which
names many different types

03:08:40.920 --> 03:08:43.200
of substances, and
they're not all the same.

03:08:43.200 --> 03:08:47.490
So like, MDMA is not even
technically a psychedelic.

03:08:47.490 --> 03:08:49.980
I think it has immense
therapeutic value,

03:08:49.980 --> 03:08:51.900
and it actually
was my gateway drug

03:08:51.900 --> 03:08:55.185
to this whole area of concern.

03:08:55.185 --> 03:08:57.062
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Amphetamine pathogen, right?

03:08:57.062 --> 03:08:58.895
It's a sort of an
amphetamine and a pathogen

03:08:58.895 --> 03:08:59.340
at the same time.

03:08:59.340 --> 03:09:01.080
SAM HARRIS: Yeah, I mean
it's, often called--

03:09:01.080 --> 03:09:01.395
ANDREW HUBERMAN: M pathogens.

03:09:01.395 --> 03:09:02.910
SAM HARRIS: Yeah,
and a pathogen--

03:09:02.910 --> 03:09:05.235
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Not a
pathogen, M pathogen.

03:09:05.235 --> 03:09:10.080
SAM HARRIS: And a pathogen or
an entactogen, it's been called.

03:09:10.080 --> 03:09:12.900
But it doesn't tend to
change perception in the way

03:09:12.900 --> 03:09:14.430
that classic psychedelics do.

03:09:14.430 --> 03:09:20.760
And it's also serotonergic,
but it has to be in some part

03:09:20.760 --> 03:09:21.870
differently.

03:09:21.870 --> 03:09:25.570
And even LSD and psilocybin,
which are much more similar

03:09:25.570 --> 03:09:28.530
and classic psychedelics,
both are also serotonergic

03:09:28.530 --> 03:09:32.730
but they're not merely,
and they're also different.

03:09:32.730 --> 03:09:35.760
And the higher dose you take
of these drugs, the more--

03:09:35.760 --> 03:09:38.940
at lower doses everything,
can kind of seem the same.

03:09:38.940 --> 03:09:42.735
At higher doses, they
begin to diverge.

03:09:45.350 --> 03:09:48.700
And we can talk about the
pharmacology if you wanted to.

03:09:48.700 --> 03:09:54.120
But I would just say
that for many of us,

03:09:54.120 --> 03:09:56.430
and certainly for
me, psychedelics

03:09:56.430 --> 03:10:01.110
were indispensable in the
beginning in proving to me

03:10:01.110 --> 03:10:04.980
that this was--

03:10:04.980 --> 03:10:09.660
the first person interrogation
of the mind was worth doing.

03:10:09.660 --> 03:10:14.460
Because I was somebody
who at age 17 or 18,

03:10:14.460 --> 03:10:24.000
before I had any real experience
with MDMA or LSD or psilocybin,

03:10:24.000 --> 03:10:26.610
if you had taught me how
to meditate at that point,

03:10:26.610 --> 03:10:30.240
I think I would have just
bounced off the whole project.

03:10:30.240 --> 03:10:38.400
I think I was so cerebral
in just my engagement

03:10:38.400 --> 03:10:39.360
with anything.

03:10:39.360 --> 03:10:45.270
I was so skeptical of any of
the religious and spiritual

03:10:45.270 --> 03:10:52.170
traditions that have given us
most of our meditation talk

03:10:52.170 --> 03:10:54.450
that I think I just would have--

03:10:54.450 --> 03:10:56.370
And I know many of these people.

03:10:56.370 --> 03:10:59.707
I have tried to teach Richard
Dawkins to meditate and Daniel

03:10:59.707 --> 03:11:00.540
Dennett to meditate.

03:11:00.540 --> 03:11:03.000
I've ambushed them
with meditation

03:11:03.000 --> 03:11:06.120
both in a group
setting and one-on-one.

03:11:06.120 --> 03:11:10.620
Not Dan but Richard, I
ambushed on my own podcast

03:11:10.620 --> 03:11:12.360
with a guided meditation.

03:11:12.360 --> 03:11:20.650
And he closes his
eyes, he looks inside,

03:11:20.650 --> 03:11:25.630
and there's nothing
of interest to see.

03:11:25.630 --> 03:11:31.470
He doesn't have the
conceptual interest in him

03:11:31.470 --> 03:11:35.250
that would cause him to
persist long enough to find out

03:11:35.250 --> 03:11:36.990
that there's a there there.

03:11:36.990 --> 03:11:43.070
Now, this is not a problem
with LSD or psilocybin or MDMA.

03:11:43.070 --> 03:11:46.220
I know that if I gave
him 100 micrograms of LSD

03:11:46.220 --> 03:11:53.973
or 5 grams of mushrooms or
25 milligrams of psilocybin--

03:11:53.973 --> 03:11:55.640
that's probably not
the analogous dosage

03:11:55.640 --> 03:11:56.840
to the 5 grams of mushrooms.

03:11:56.840 --> 03:11:58.715
5 grams of mushrooms
would be more than that.

03:12:02.180 --> 03:12:06.265
I forget what it is of
MDMA, maybe 120 milligrams.

03:12:06.265 --> 03:12:08.570
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think
the maps dose, which

03:12:08.570 --> 03:12:11.660
is the one that's under clinical
trials is 125 milligrams

03:12:11.660 --> 03:12:14.390
with an option of a
75 milligram booster.

03:12:14.390 --> 03:12:15.530
Funny, I remember it.

03:12:15.530 --> 03:12:16.905
SAM HARRIS: Yeah,
that's strange,

03:12:16.905 --> 03:12:19.550
the facts that come to hand.

03:12:19.550 --> 03:12:25.190
But there's just no
possibility that nothing's

03:12:25.190 --> 03:12:26.300
going to happen right now.

03:12:26.300 --> 03:12:30.350
Something with a psychedelic,
with MDMA, most people

03:12:30.350 --> 03:12:32.480
tend to have certainly
under any kind of guidance,

03:12:32.480 --> 03:12:38.660
tend to have a very positive,
pro-social experience.

03:12:38.660 --> 03:12:41.240
But with a
psychedelic, you might

03:12:41.240 --> 03:12:47.740
have a somewhat
terrifying experience

03:12:47.740 --> 03:12:49.660
if you have quote, a bad trip.

03:12:49.660 --> 03:12:51.400
And I've certainly
had those experiences

03:12:51.400 --> 03:12:55.030
on LSD and to some
degree, on psilocybin.

03:12:55.030 --> 03:12:59.740
But the prospect that
nothing is going to happen

03:12:59.740 --> 03:13:05.200
is just in nearly a million
cases out of a million just

03:13:05.200 --> 03:13:06.020
not in the cards.

03:13:06.020 --> 03:13:08.740
I mean, just
neurophysiologically,

03:13:08.740 --> 03:13:11.890
something is going to happen
with the requisite dose of one

03:13:11.890 --> 03:13:13.510
of these drugs.

03:13:13.510 --> 03:13:19.510
And if that thing that
happens is psychologically

03:13:19.510 --> 03:13:24.500
at all normative and pleasant
and interesting and valuable,

03:13:24.500 --> 03:13:26.890
which it is so much of
the time and certainly

03:13:26.890 --> 03:13:32.320
under the appropriate set
and setting and guidance,

03:13:32.320 --> 03:13:36.880
it can be a lot of the time
for virtually everybody.

03:13:36.880 --> 03:13:38.785
Again, there are caveats.

03:13:38.785 --> 03:13:40.210
If you're prone,
if you think you

03:13:40.210 --> 03:13:44.830
have a proclivity for
schizophrenia or bipolar

03:13:44.830 --> 03:13:48.700
disorder, this is almost
certainly not for you.

03:13:48.700 --> 03:13:52.000
And anyone doing the
studies at Johns Hopkins

03:13:52.000 --> 03:13:56.200
for the therapeutic effects
of any of these drugs,

03:13:56.200 --> 03:13:58.420
they're there ruling
out people with--

03:13:58.420 --> 03:14:01.902
first degree relatives with any
of these clinical conditions.

03:14:05.250 --> 03:14:09.810
So for somebody
like me at 18 who

03:14:09.810 --> 03:14:15.180
didn't know that this was
an area of not only interest

03:14:15.180 --> 03:14:18.090
but would it be the
center of gravity

03:14:18.090 --> 03:14:21.090
for the rest of his life, if
only he could pay attention

03:14:21.090 --> 03:14:25.200
clearly enough to
see that it could be,

03:14:25.200 --> 03:14:27.240
I was someone who very likely--

03:14:27.240 --> 03:14:29.358
again, I don't have the
counterfactual in hand.

03:14:29.358 --> 03:14:30.900
I don't know what
would have happened

03:14:30.900 --> 03:14:33.360
if someone had
forced me to meditate

03:14:33.360 --> 03:14:34.920
for an hour at that point.

03:14:34.920 --> 03:14:42.350
But I know I wasn't interested
in it until I took MDMA.

03:14:42.350 --> 03:14:44.720
I know I wasn't having
these kinds of experiences

03:14:44.720 --> 03:14:48.050
spontaneously that
showed me that there

03:14:48.050 --> 03:14:51.650
was an inner landscape
that was worth exploring.

03:14:51.650 --> 03:14:55.250
I was a very hard-headed skeptic
who was very interested in lots

03:14:55.250 --> 03:14:59.630
of things, but there was no
alternative to me just thinking

03:14:59.630 --> 03:15:00.950
more about those things.

03:15:00.950 --> 03:15:05.120
I mean, the idea that there's
some other way of grasping

03:15:05.120 --> 03:15:10.400
cognitively at the
interesting parts of the world

03:15:10.400 --> 03:15:14.750
beyond thinking about the
world, that just wouldn't

03:15:14.750 --> 03:15:15.920
have computed for me at all.

03:15:19.700 --> 03:15:24.920
No one ever gave me a book
to read or I never had--

03:15:24.920 --> 03:15:27.830
The noun, meditation,
very likely

03:15:27.830 --> 03:15:33.590
meant absolutely nothing to me
before I took my first dose of,

03:15:33.590 --> 03:15:36.440
in this case it was MDMA.

03:15:36.440 --> 03:15:42.620
So what the drug experience
did for me is it just proved--

03:15:42.620 --> 03:15:45.380
So one of the
limitations of drug

03:15:45.380 --> 03:15:48.470
is that, obviously, no matter
how good the experience,

03:15:48.470 --> 03:15:50.120
the drug wears off
and then you're

03:15:50.120 --> 03:15:54.650
back to in more or
less your usual form,

03:15:54.650 --> 03:15:57.320
and now you have a
memory of the experience.

03:15:57.320 --> 03:15:58.855
And it can be a
fairly dim memory.

03:15:58.855 --> 03:16:00.230
I mean, some of
these experiences

03:16:00.230 --> 03:16:03.717
are so discontinuous with
normal waking consciousness

03:16:03.717 --> 03:16:05.300
that it can be like
trying to remember

03:16:05.300 --> 03:16:09.740
a dream that just degrades
over the course of seconds.

03:16:09.740 --> 03:16:12.470
And then it could have been the
most intense dream you've ever

03:16:12.470 --> 03:16:15.650
had and for whatever reason,
you can barely get a purchase

03:16:15.650 --> 03:16:17.132
on what it was about.

03:16:17.132 --> 03:16:21.320
And there are some
psychedelic experiences

03:16:21.320 --> 03:16:22.470
that are analogous to that.

03:16:22.470 --> 03:16:25.220
But for most people
most of the time,

03:16:25.220 --> 03:16:27.020
there's a residue
of this experience.

03:16:27.020 --> 03:16:29.795
And with something like MDMA,
they can be quite vivid.

03:16:32.440 --> 03:16:37.000
Where you recognize there
was a way of being that

03:16:37.000 --> 03:16:40.870
is quite different than what I'm
tending to access by default,

03:16:40.870 --> 03:16:50.100
and it is different
in ways that are just

03:16:50.100 --> 03:16:53.760
obviously better and
psychologically more healthy.

03:16:53.760 --> 03:16:57.600
I mean, it's possible to
be healthy psychologically

03:16:57.600 --> 03:17:01.620
in a way that I never imagined.

03:17:01.620 --> 03:17:06.840
And then when you link it up
to the traditional literature

03:17:06.840 --> 03:17:09.600
around any of this stuff,
again, so much of it

03:17:09.600 --> 03:17:12.570
is shot through
with superstition

03:17:12.570 --> 03:17:14.490
and other worldliness
of religion.

03:17:14.490 --> 03:17:18.900
And as you know and I think
you're listeners probably know,

03:17:18.900 --> 03:17:21.060
I've spent a lot of time
criticizing all that.

03:17:21.060 --> 03:17:24.270
But there is a baby in the
bathwater to all of that.

03:17:24.270 --> 03:17:29.760
It's not that somebody
like Jesus or the Buddha

03:17:29.760 --> 03:17:33.030
or any of the matriarchs and
patriarchs of the world's

03:17:33.030 --> 03:17:37.020
religions, it's not that they
were all conscious frauds

03:17:37.020 --> 03:17:43.200
or temporal lobe epileptics,
there's a pathological lens

03:17:43.200 --> 03:17:45.825
that you can put
on top of all that.

03:17:45.825 --> 03:17:49.680
But once you have one of these
experiences on psychedelics

03:17:49.680 --> 03:17:53.760
or on a drug like MDMA, you
know that there's a there there.

03:17:53.760 --> 03:17:57.060
You know that unconditional
love is a possibility.

03:17:57.060 --> 03:18:03.570
You know that feeling
truly one with nature.

03:18:03.570 --> 03:18:06.330
I mean, just so one
with nature that you

03:18:06.330 --> 03:18:09.270
could spend 10 hours
in front of a tree

03:18:09.270 --> 03:18:11.790
and find that to be the
most rewarding experience

03:18:11.790 --> 03:18:13.920
of your life.

03:18:13.920 --> 03:18:15.735
That's a possible
state of consciousness.

03:18:15.735 --> 03:18:17.610
Now, it may not be the
state of consciousness

03:18:17.610 --> 03:18:18.863
you want all the time.

03:18:18.863 --> 03:18:20.280
You don't want to
be the crazy guy

03:18:20.280 --> 03:18:22.050
by the tree you
know who can't have

03:18:22.050 --> 03:18:24.760
a conversation
about anything else.

03:18:24.760 --> 03:18:28.660
But once you have one
of these experiences,

03:18:28.660 --> 03:18:34.010
you recognize, OK, there's
some reason why I'm not having

03:18:34.010 --> 03:18:36.080
the beatific vision right now.

03:18:36.080 --> 03:18:39.440
And I can't even figure out
how to aim my attention so as

03:18:39.440 --> 03:18:41.040
to have anything like it.

03:18:41.040 --> 03:18:44.480
And that's a problem,
because it's available,

03:18:44.480 --> 03:18:50.780
and it is among the best things
that has ever happened to me.

03:18:50.780 --> 03:18:54.860
And now I can just only dimly
remember what that was like.

03:18:54.860 --> 03:18:58.010
So how do I get back
there on some level?

03:18:58.010 --> 03:19:01.760
So that invites, again,
a logic of changes.

03:19:01.760 --> 03:19:05.550
A logic of seeking changes in
the contents of consciousness,

03:19:05.550 --> 03:19:11.990
which sets someone up for
this protracted or seemingly

03:19:11.990 --> 03:19:15.530
protracted and fairly
frustrating search

03:19:15.530 --> 03:19:19.130
to game their
nervous system so as

03:19:19.130 --> 03:19:23.870
to have those kinds of
experiences more and more.

03:19:23.870 --> 03:19:27.320
And again, it's not that
that's in principle fruitless

03:19:27.320 --> 03:19:31.970
but it is from the point of
view of the core insight,

03:19:31.970 --> 03:19:34.100
the core wisdom of
what I would take

03:19:34.100 --> 03:19:36.990
from a tradition like
Buddhism, which is not

03:19:36.990 --> 03:19:38.990
the only tradition that
has given voice to this,

03:19:38.990 --> 03:19:41.540
but I would argue it's
given it voice to it

03:19:41.540 --> 03:19:45.080
in the most articulate way.

03:19:45.080 --> 03:19:47.960
Again, leaving aside
any of the superstition

03:19:47.960 --> 03:19:52.160
and other worldliness
and miracles that we

03:19:52.160 --> 03:19:53.820
don't have to talk
about at the moment.

03:19:53.820 --> 03:19:55.820
And you certainly don't
need to endorse in order

03:19:55.820 --> 03:19:57.112
to be interested in this stuff.

03:20:00.560 --> 03:20:06.030
And so that's the
bifurcation between all

03:20:06.030 --> 03:20:08.430
of the utility of
psychedelics and what

03:20:08.430 --> 03:20:12.150
I'm talking about under
the rubric of meditation

03:20:12.150 --> 03:20:16.530
is at this point of OK, once you
realize there's a there there,

03:20:16.530 --> 03:20:17.400
what do you do?

03:20:17.400 --> 03:20:20.130
And what's the logic by
which you're led to do it?

03:20:20.130 --> 03:20:23.370
And it's possible if
you're only framework

03:20:23.370 --> 03:20:26.100
is the good
experiences, the good

03:20:26.100 --> 03:20:29.700
feels you had on
whatever drug it was

03:20:29.700 --> 03:20:37.890
and a further discussion of
what that path of changes

03:20:37.890 --> 03:20:39.240
can look like--

03:20:39.240 --> 03:20:41.130
and that can become in
a religious context,

03:20:41.130 --> 03:20:43.730
it can come in just a
purely psychedelic context

03:20:43.730 --> 03:20:48.190
or some combination of the two.

03:20:48.190 --> 03:20:54.430
I think you can be
misled to just seek

03:20:54.430 --> 03:20:55.708
lots of peak experiences.

03:20:55.708 --> 03:20:57.250
You're just trying
to string together

03:20:57.250 --> 03:21:00.370
a lot of peak experiences hoping
they're going to change you,

03:21:00.370 --> 03:21:03.340
every one of which,
by definition is

03:21:03.340 --> 03:21:05.170
going to be impermanent.

03:21:05.170 --> 03:21:07.480
It's first it wasn't
there, then it's there,

03:21:07.480 --> 03:21:09.050
and then it's no longer there.

03:21:09.050 --> 03:21:12.170
And then you've
got a memory of it.

03:21:12.170 --> 03:21:14.560
What I think what
everyone really

03:21:14.560 --> 03:21:17.470
wants whether they know it or
not, and they're right to want,

03:21:17.470 --> 03:21:22.330
is a type of freedom that
is compatible with even

03:21:22.330 --> 03:21:25.030
ordinary states of
consciousness, which

03:21:25.030 --> 03:21:28.150
can ride along with them
into extraordinary states

03:21:28.150 --> 03:21:29.090
of consciousness.

03:21:29.090 --> 03:21:32.800
So I hadn't done
psychedelics for 25 years

03:21:32.800 --> 03:21:36.100
because, again, they were super
useful for me in the beginning,

03:21:36.100 --> 03:21:41.230
then I discovered meditation on
the basis of those experiences,

03:21:41.230 --> 03:21:43.000
got really into
meditation and realized,

03:21:43.000 --> 03:21:44.215
OK, this is a much more--

03:21:48.160 --> 03:21:50.260
conceptually, this makes
much more sense to me.

03:21:50.260 --> 03:21:55.600
This is delivering the goods
in terms of my experience.

03:21:55.600 --> 03:21:57.400
There's no need to
keep having these--

03:21:57.400 --> 03:22:01.210
seeking these peak
experiences with drugs.

03:22:01.210 --> 03:22:04.030
But it had been 25 years
since I had done that

03:22:04.030 --> 03:22:06.730
and there was this resurgence
in research on psychedelics.

03:22:06.730 --> 03:22:08.890
And I was being asked
about psychedelics,

03:22:08.890 --> 03:22:11.050
and I was talking about
their utility for me,

03:22:11.050 --> 03:22:13.390
but again, these were
distant memories.

03:22:13.390 --> 03:22:16.750
And there was also one type
of psychedelic experience

03:22:16.750 --> 03:22:18.400
I was aware that
I had never had.

03:22:18.400 --> 03:22:22.850
I had never done a high dose
of mushrooms blindfolded.

03:22:22.850 --> 03:22:24.460
Every mushroom trip
I'd ever had I'd

03:22:24.460 --> 03:22:27.770
been out in nature
and interacting with--

03:22:27.770 --> 03:22:30.400
it's just been a very
transformed sensory experience

03:22:30.400 --> 03:22:33.310
of the world and
of other people.

03:22:33.310 --> 03:22:35.230
But I'd never done it
alone, blindfolded,

03:22:35.230 --> 03:22:39.730
just purely inwardly directed,
and at a high dosage.

03:22:39.730 --> 03:22:46.720
I'd done high doses of
LSD but not mushrooms.

03:22:46.720 --> 03:22:50.080
So I did that and
it was very useful.

03:22:50.080 --> 03:22:53.230
And I spoke about it on my
podcast, and there's actually--

03:22:53.230 --> 03:22:55.480
I think if you search
"Sam Harris mushroom

03:22:55.480 --> 03:22:58.030
trip" on YouTube, you
get the 19 minute version

03:22:58.030 --> 03:23:00.820
of my describing that trip.

03:23:00.820 --> 03:23:05.050
It was incredibly useful,
but what was doubly useful

03:23:05.050 --> 03:23:11.290
was my mindfulness training in
the context of that explosion

03:23:11.290 --> 03:23:12.670
of synesthesia.

03:23:12.670 --> 03:23:15.280
I mean, it was such an
overwhelmingly strong

03:23:15.280 --> 03:23:21.550
experience, and there
were so many moments where

03:23:21.550 --> 03:23:23.410
it could have gone
one way or the other

03:23:23.410 --> 03:23:28.420
based on my sense
of just, OK, I'm

03:23:28.420 --> 03:23:30.520
going to try to resist this.

03:23:30.520 --> 03:23:33.800
It was in truth irresistible
because it was just so much,

03:23:33.800 --> 03:23:40.150
but there were moments where
I was aware of, OK, this is--

03:23:40.150 --> 03:23:44.800
letting go of self
in this context

03:23:44.800 --> 03:23:50.227
is the thing that
is going to make

03:23:50.227 --> 03:23:51.810
the difference between
heaven and hell

03:23:51.810 --> 03:23:54.480
here because there
are experiences that

03:23:54.480 --> 03:23:57.030
are so extreme
that you can't even

03:23:57.030 --> 03:24:00.180
tell if it's agony or ecstasy.

03:24:00.180 --> 03:24:02.040
Everything is turned up to 11.

03:24:02.040 --> 03:24:06.180
And the difference
between the two is--

03:24:06.180 --> 03:24:08.370
the tipping point is just--

03:24:08.370 --> 03:24:10.800
really it's a high
wire act in some sense.

03:24:10.800 --> 03:24:14.430
You could just fall to
one side or the other.

03:24:14.430 --> 03:24:20.570
Yeah, so what I think
people want is--

03:24:20.570 --> 03:24:22.400
they certainly want
to be able to extract

03:24:22.400 --> 03:24:25.880
from the psychedelic
experience, wisdom

03:24:25.880 --> 03:24:28.650
that is applicable to ordinary
states of consciousness.

03:24:28.650 --> 03:24:31.220
It's like, what is the
thing you can realize

03:24:31.220 --> 03:24:32.930
in a moment of
having a conversation

03:24:32.930 --> 03:24:36.740
with your child that
isn't distracting you

03:24:36.740 --> 03:24:38.070
from that relationship?

03:24:38.070 --> 03:24:40.790
It's not a memory of
when the world dissolved

03:24:40.790 --> 03:24:44.360
or when you were
indistinguishable from the sky,

03:24:44.360 --> 03:24:49.160
but it's just a way of
having free attention

03:24:49.160 --> 03:24:54.710
and unconditional
love in this totally

03:24:54.710 --> 03:25:00.020
ordinary and potentially
chaotic human experience, which

03:25:00.020 --> 03:25:02.480
can be psychologically
fraught and you

03:25:02.480 --> 03:25:05.240
can meet iterations
of yourself that you

03:25:05.240 --> 03:25:07.190
don't like that
are not equipping

03:25:07.190 --> 03:25:10.640
you to be the best possible
person in that relationship.

03:25:10.640 --> 03:25:14.420
And what we want to do is
cut through all of that

03:25:14.420 --> 03:25:19.040
and actually be in
love with our lives

03:25:19.040 --> 03:25:22.260
and with the people in our
lives more and more of the time.

03:25:22.260 --> 03:25:31.750
And I'm not saying that repeated
psychedelic journeys can't

03:25:31.750 --> 03:25:34.570
be integral to that
project, but you

03:25:34.570 --> 03:25:39.310
know that the project can't
be being high all the time.

03:25:39.310 --> 03:25:44.860
So whatever is extractable from
the occasional psychedelic trip

03:25:44.860 --> 03:25:48.160
has got to be manageable into
ordinary waking consciousness.

03:25:48.160 --> 03:25:53.360
And the real point of contact
does run through this--

03:25:53.360 --> 03:25:55.600
what I've been calling
the illusion of the self.

03:25:55.600 --> 03:26:00.130
And again, that part
is discoverable without

03:26:00.130 --> 03:26:02.410
any changes in contents.

03:26:02.410 --> 03:26:06.430
So you don't have to suddenly
feel the energy of your body

03:26:06.430 --> 03:26:10.480
rush out and be continuous
with the ocean of energy that

03:26:10.480 --> 03:26:12.250
is not your body.

03:26:12.250 --> 03:26:14.170
That's an experience
that's there to be had,

03:26:14.170 --> 03:26:15.580
I mean, there's no doubt.

03:26:15.580 --> 03:26:24.260
But the truth is just
looking at this cup

03:26:24.260 --> 03:26:29.420
is just as formless and
as mysterious as that when

03:26:29.420 --> 03:26:32.120
seen in the right
way, and that's

03:26:32.120 --> 03:26:36.570
what meditation encourages
one to recognize.

03:26:36.570 --> 03:26:39.080
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
share the experience

03:26:39.080 --> 03:26:43.970
that MDMA significantly
altered my perception of what's

03:26:43.970 --> 03:26:47.300
possible in terms of an
emotional stance towards self

03:26:47.300 --> 03:26:50.420
and others, including
animals, something that

03:26:50.420 --> 03:26:53.480
runs very deep for me and that
I had been actively suppressing

03:26:53.480 --> 03:26:55.520
in anticipation of having
me put my dog down.

03:26:55.520 --> 03:27:00.050
But also, I don't know how
to frame it except to say,

03:27:00.050 --> 03:27:01.760
my lab did animal
research for years,

03:27:01.760 --> 03:27:04.743
and I was always very conflicted
about it because I love animals

03:27:04.743 --> 03:27:06.410
and yet I wanted to
understand the brain

03:27:06.410 --> 03:27:10.550
and we need to work on
animal brains, and we--

03:27:10.550 --> 03:27:11.720
SAM HARRIS: Rodents or what?

03:27:11.720 --> 03:27:13.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, I'll
be very direct about this.

03:27:13.970 --> 03:27:15.800
I've worked on many species.

03:27:15.800 --> 03:27:20.990
I've worked on mice and rats.

03:27:20.990 --> 03:27:22.280
Admittedly, I've worked on--

03:27:22.280 --> 03:27:23.990
I've done some cat experiments.

03:27:23.990 --> 03:27:28.610
I've worked on large, non-human
primates including macaques.

03:27:28.610 --> 03:27:31.400
I no longer work on
any of those species.

03:27:31.400 --> 03:27:35.660
I've worked on cuttlefish,
cephalopods, a discussion

03:27:35.660 --> 03:27:39.470
for another time, brilliant
little creatures, maybe

03:27:39.470 --> 03:27:41.900
as smart as us, or who knows?

03:27:41.900 --> 03:27:42.980
Maybe smarter.

03:27:42.980 --> 03:27:47.930
And now I work on humans
because I couldn't reconcile

03:27:47.930 --> 03:27:51.320
the challenge inside me,
which was my love of animals

03:27:51.320 --> 03:27:52.200
and working on them.

03:27:52.200 --> 03:27:53.900
I just couldn't
do it any longer.

03:27:53.900 --> 03:27:57.320
And MDMA didn't set
that transition,

03:27:57.320 --> 03:27:59.930
that transition actually
had been set a lot earlier.

03:27:59.930 --> 03:28:01.520
It's something I
really grappled with.

03:28:01.520 --> 03:28:03.560
It didn't keep me up at
night, but it was always

03:28:03.560 --> 03:28:05.210
in the back of my mind.

03:28:05.210 --> 03:28:06.860
In any event, I hope
what we discovered

03:28:06.860 --> 03:28:10.850
was worthwhile, but this
that's a bigger debate.

03:28:10.850 --> 03:28:12.470
And I've strong
feelings about this,

03:28:12.470 --> 03:28:16.520
and maybe it's a topic
for another podcast.

03:28:16.520 --> 03:28:19.370
But I'm very happy that
now I work on humans,

03:28:19.370 --> 03:28:20.750
and they can tell
me if they want

03:28:20.750 --> 03:28:22.250
to be part of the
experiment or not,

03:28:22.250 --> 03:28:26.540
and I trust them and
I trust their answers.

03:28:26.540 --> 03:28:30.770
I think that MDMA, in its role
as an pathogen, I think really

03:28:30.770 --> 03:28:33.770
did set an understanding
of what's real and true.

03:28:33.770 --> 03:28:37.340
So I think truths
like that become--

03:28:37.340 --> 03:28:40.340
I felt that they didn't
hit me square in the face.

03:28:40.340 --> 03:28:44.057
The feeling behind the
conflict made itself evident,

03:28:44.057 --> 03:28:45.890
and what to do about
it made itself evident.

03:28:45.890 --> 03:28:49.040
So I suppose MDMA did
assist the transition

03:28:49.040 --> 03:28:52.658
to purely human research, as
opposed to animal research.

03:28:52.658 --> 03:28:54.200
The other thing that
I noticed it did

03:28:54.200 --> 03:28:58.280
is it made it not
scary to confront

03:28:58.280 --> 03:29:01.243
things that were scary to
confront in my conscious life.

03:29:01.243 --> 03:29:03.410
And I could think about
things in my conscious life,

03:29:03.410 --> 03:29:05.810
but it brought
them close in a way

03:29:05.810 --> 03:29:07.820
that I could get closer
and closer to the flame

03:29:07.820 --> 03:29:09.320
and then gain some
understanding.

03:29:09.320 --> 03:29:16.460
I'm still amazed at how answers
arrive, both during the session

03:29:16.460 --> 03:29:18.668
and in the weeks and
months that follow if one

03:29:18.668 --> 03:29:19.710
puts the attention to it.

03:29:19.710 --> 03:29:21.418
I think that's why
it's important to have

03:29:21.418 --> 03:29:23.810
a guide of some sort
or to have some pseudo

03:29:23.810 --> 03:29:28.160
structure because otherwise
one can get attached

03:29:28.160 --> 03:29:29.480
to the sounds in the room.

03:29:29.480 --> 03:29:31.770
And there's probably
meaning there,

03:29:31.770 --> 03:29:33.650
but I wanted to do
some deeper work.

03:29:33.650 --> 03:29:36.020
I have not had experience
with psilocybin, at least

03:29:36.020 --> 03:29:36.862
not since my youth.

03:29:36.862 --> 03:29:38.570
And I don't recommend
young people do it.

03:29:38.570 --> 03:29:40.948
I regret doing LSD and
psilocybin as a young person.

03:29:40.948 --> 03:29:43.490
I don't say that for politically
correct reasons or liability

03:29:43.490 --> 03:29:46.940
reasons, I just think my
mind was not developed.

03:29:46.940 --> 03:29:51.270
But I'm intrigued by something--
so here's the question--

03:29:51.270 --> 03:29:53.780
how is it that psilocybin
in particular and high dose

03:29:53.780 --> 03:29:56.660
psilocybin and the ego
dissolution that people

03:29:56.660 --> 03:30:01.700
talk about on psilocybin--
how do you think that lines up

03:30:01.700 --> 03:30:04.310
with some of the
experiences that you've

03:30:04.310 --> 03:30:07.640
been describing for a
adequate meditation practice?

03:30:07.640 --> 03:30:10.460
Because that's something that
I did not experience on MDMA.

03:30:10.460 --> 03:30:13.670
In fact, if anything, I
experienced for the first time

03:30:13.670 --> 03:30:18.620
what really feeling like
a isolated container was,

03:30:18.620 --> 03:30:21.385
and the difference-- and how
empathy and being bounded--

03:30:21.385 --> 03:30:23.510
having, in other words,
good boundaries and empathy

03:30:23.510 --> 03:30:24.497
could be symbiotic.

03:30:24.497 --> 03:30:26.330
I experienced that for
the first time there.

03:30:26.330 --> 03:30:28.520
And I do think that
there is learning inside

03:30:28.520 --> 03:30:30.800
of these states that
translates into everyday life

03:30:30.800 --> 03:30:32.588
when one is not on these states.

03:30:32.588 --> 03:30:34.130
And the last thing
I'll say is, no, I

03:30:34.130 --> 03:30:38.840
don't feel the impulse to go
and do 20 more MDMA sessions.

03:30:38.840 --> 03:30:41.750
I think the three as
part of this study

03:30:41.750 --> 03:30:45.080
were very effective for me.

03:30:45.080 --> 03:30:47.780
As I say, if you hear the
calling again you might do it,

03:30:47.780 --> 03:30:50.900
but I'm very curious about
psilocybin in particular

03:30:50.900 --> 03:30:52.940
and this notion
of ego dissolution

03:30:52.940 --> 03:30:54.920
because we've been
talking about the self.

03:30:54.920 --> 03:30:57.650
SAM HARRIS: Well, so
there are different ways

03:30:57.650 --> 03:31:04.240
In which the sense of self
can be eroded or expanded.

03:31:04.240 --> 03:31:08.470
There's lots of experiences that
can still have a center to them

03:31:08.470 --> 03:31:14.770
but be very novel
and transformational.

03:31:14.770 --> 03:31:20.050
And one can reify
those as a goal state.

03:31:20.050 --> 03:31:23.160
There's a concept in Buddhism
that I think is useful.

03:31:23.160 --> 03:31:26.520
It doesn't translate
well to English,

03:31:26.520 --> 03:31:31.290
or it can set up false
associations in English

03:31:31.290 --> 03:31:32.280
that are unfortunate.

03:31:32.280 --> 03:31:36.990
But so there's a concept of
emptiness in Buddhism, which

03:31:36.990 --> 03:31:39.630
sounds, again, gray and
dispiriting in English,

03:31:39.630 --> 03:31:52.150
but it's cognate terms are
things like unconditioned,

03:31:52.150 --> 03:31:55.860
unconstrained, open, centralist.

03:32:01.810 --> 03:32:03.400
When I'm talking
about non duality,

03:32:03.400 --> 03:32:06.190
when I'm talking about the
loss of a sense of subject

03:32:06.190 --> 03:32:08.410
and then what's
left, in Buddhism,

03:32:08.410 --> 03:32:10.840
they would often describe
what's left as emptiness.

03:32:10.840 --> 03:32:15.880
But an emptiness
is not a something.

03:32:15.880 --> 03:32:19.690
Importantly, it's not
the same thing as unity,

03:32:19.690 --> 03:32:22.550
so it's not a oneness
because it's--

03:32:26.130 --> 03:32:28.860
When the center drops
out of experience,

03:32:28.860 --> 03:32:34.650
it's not like you are
suddenly merged with the cup.

03:32:34.650 --> 03:32:38.910
Now, granted, this is
where psilocybin and other

03:32:38.910 --> 03:32:42.540
psychedelics can give
a false impression of I

03:32:42.540 --> 03:32:43.830
think what the goal is.

03:32:43.830 --> 03:32:47.100
You can have seeming
merging experiences--

03:32:47.100 --> 03:32:50.040
you can have unity experiences
on psychedelics, which

03:32:50.040 --> 03:32:54.312
can be quite powerful,
especially with other people

03:32:54.312 --> 03:32:55.770
and with nature,
where you can just

03:32:55.770 --> 03:33:05.270
feel the energy of
your body becomes

03:33:05.270 --> 03:33:07.310
incredibly vivid and powerful.

03:33:07.310 --> 03:33:12.410
It's like everything is just
buzzing with life energy.

03:33:12.410 --> 03:33:15.320
And then when you touch
another person's hand

03:33:15.320 --> 03:33:18.410
or you touch a
tree, there can be

03:33:18.410 --> 03:33:21.380
this continuity of
energy, which can be

03:33:21.380 --> 03:33:23.530
this overwhelming experience.

03:33:23.530 --> 03:33:27.950
And again, this is
a 20 megaton change

03:33:27.950 --> 03:33:29.780
in the contents
of consciousness.

03:33:29.780 --> 03:33:34.760
This is a non ordinary
state of consciousness.

03:33:34.760 --> 03:33:39.050
To give some indication
of how this happens--

03:33:39.050 --> 03:33:42.440
back in the day
when I was in my 20s

03:33:42.440 --> 03:33:44.090
and I was experimenting with--

03:33:44.090 --> 03:33:47.212
this was LSD, but
some friends and I

03:33:47.212 --> 03:33:48.920
had decided-- we had
this brilliant idea.

03:33:48.920 --> 03:33:54.380
We would camp above Muir woods
and then take some LSD at dawn

03:33:54.380 --> 03:33:58.190
and then walk down like a
mile I think from the campsite

03:33:58.190 --> 03:34:01.280
into the actual
proper grove of trees

03:34:01.280 --> 03:34:04.400
and commune with the giant
redwoods, the tallest

03:34:04.400 --> 03:34:06.285
trees on Earth.

03:34:06.285 --> 03:34:08.660
And so we dropped the acid at
dawn, and we start walking,

03:34:08.660 --> 03:34:11.210
but the acid came on
almost immediately.

03:34:11.210 --> 03:34:12.080
And we didn't get--

03:34:12.080 --> 03:34:13.730
I mean, we got nowhere
near the woods,

03:34:13.730 --> 03:34:15.980
and we got stopped by
a tree that was just

03:34:15.980 --> 03:34:18.823
like an ordinary
20 foot oak tree,

03:34:18.823 --> 03:34:20.240
the most boring
tree in the world.

03:34:20.240 --> 03:34:23.090
And that tree absorbed
the next six hours

03:34:23.090 --> 03:34:26.120
of our conscious attention
because it was just--

03:34:26.120 --> 03:34:27.170
it was the tree of life.

03:34:27.170 --> 03:34:30.260
I mean, there could
be no better tree.

03:34:30.260 --> 03:34:32.150
So we're talking about
nonordinary states

03:34:32.150 --> 03:34:36.860
of consciousness wherein
a merging with life

03:34:36.860 --> 03:34:38.995
and with the world is possible.

03:34:41.720 --> 03:34:45.090
So I'm not saying that kind
of experience isn't possible,

03:34:45.090 --> 03:34:51.500
but there's a sort of
expanded self reification.

03:34:51.500 --> 03:34:56.450
It is a kind of ego dissolution,
but there's a kind of egoity

03:34:56.450 --> 03:34:59.480
that goes along for
the ride as well,

03:34:59.480 --> 03:35:00.860
or can go along for the ride.

03:35:00.860 --> 03:35:06.290
And the real insight into
emptiness, the real centerless

03:35:06.290 --> 03:35:12.860
center of the bull's eye is a
recognition that in some ways

03:35:12.860 --> 03:35:14.720
equalizes all experiences.

03:35:14.720 --> 03:35:17.570
And again, it's just
as available now

03:35:17.570 --> 03:35:21.290
in this ordinary
podcasting experience

03:35:21.290 --> 03:35:26.570
as it is when you're merging
hands on with an oak tree,

03:35:26.570 --> 03:35:35.040
and on 400 micrograms of acid,
and this is the whole universe.

03:35:35.040 --> 03:35:40.580
And so it's the equality
of those two experiences

03:35:40.580 --> 03:35:43.700
that this concept of
emptiness captures, which

03:35:43.700 --> 03:35:47.090
a concept of oneness doesn't
quite capture because oneness

03:35:47.090 --> 03:35:50.810
is really this peak experience
of being dragged out

03:35:50.810 --> 03:35:55.790
of your somethingness into
a much bigger somethingness.

03:35:55.790 --> 03:35:59.090
Emptiness is just
no center and then

03:35:59.090 --> 03:36:01.130
everything is in its own place.

03:36:01.130 --> 03:36:03.830
There's still sights, and
sounds, and sensations,

03:36:03.830 --> 03:36:07.520
and thoughts, and feelings, but
there's no there's no center

03:36:07.520 --> 03:36:11.510
and there's no
clinging to anything.

03:36:11.510 --> 03:36:13.370
There's no clinging
to identity, there's

03:36:13.370 --> 03:36:15.830
no clinging to the good
stuff, there's no there's

03:36:15.830 --> 03:36:18.340
no resistance to the bad stuff.

03:36:18.340 --> 03:36:22.100
So pleasant and unpleasant
get strangely equalized,

03:36:22.100 --> 03:36:26.240
and it's very expansive.

03:36:26.240 --> 03:36:29.870
And most importantly, it
doesn't block anything.

03:36:29.870 --> 03:36:32.210
So yeah, if for whatever
reason if your nervous system

03:36:32.210 --> 03:36:36.170
is set up to have
the, oh my god, I'm

03:36:36.170 --> 03:36:39.260
now merging with the
tree experience, that's

03:36:39.260 --> 03:36:42.380
possible from the
state of no center.

03:36:42.380 --> 03:36:45.110
And on my recent--

03:36:45.110 --> 03:36:46.955
now not so recent,
three years ago, it

03:36:46.955 --> 03:36:51.710
was right before COVID,
but my last big psychedelic

03:36:51.710 --> 03:36:56.880
experience, I was very
much experiencing that.

03:36:56.880 --> 03:37:00.690
Whereas, insofar as I--

03:37:00.690 --> 03:37:04.590
at the peak, there was no me
to remember any of this stuff.

03:37:04.590 --> 03:37:09.600
But insofar as I could
experiment with--

03:37:09.600 --> 03:37:11.880
is this really different
from anything else?

03:37:11.880 --> 03:37:16.770
There is a equalizing to
the emptiness recognition,

03:37:16.770 --> 03:37:21.240
even in the presence of
a completely transformed

03:37:21.240 --> 03:37:24.300
neurophysiology.

03:37:24.300 --> 03:37:26.190
Again, there's a
point of contact.

03:37:26.190 --> 03:37:30.360
I mean, the real point of
contact between psychedelics

03:37:30.360 --> 03:37:32.460
and meditation for me is--

03:37:32.460 --> 03:37:34.632
but for my experiences
on psychedelics,

03:37:34.632 --> 03:37:36.090
I think there's
just no way I would

03:37:36.090 --> 03:37:40.200
have had the free attention to
be interested in the project

03:37:40.200 --> 03:37:42.188
at all.

03:37:42.188 --> 03:37:43.980
And there are other
aspects to the project.

03:37:43.980 --> 03:37:47.280
It's not just having this
insight into selflessness,

03:37:47.280 --> 03:37:49.920
it's all of the ethical
ramifications of that.

03:37:49.920 --> 03:37:52.170
It's just like, what kind
of person do you want to be?

03:37:52.170 --> 03:37:54.480
What are your values?

03:37:54.480 --> 03:37:57.180
What is a good life,
altogether, when

03:37:57.180 --> 03:38:00.150
you are talking
about relationships,

03:38:00.150 --> 03:38:03.300
and political engagement,
and the changes you can

03:38:03.300 --> 03:38:05.400
make in the world or not make?

03:38:05.400 --> 03:38:07.590
It's just, what kind of
person do you want to be?

03:38:07.590 --> 03:38:10.410
There's a much
larger consideration.

03:38:10.410 --> 03:38:15.180
And I mean, as you discovered,
an experience on MDMA

03:38:15.180 --> 03:38:23.330
can really both expand your
model of what is possible,

03:38:23.330 --> 03:38:25.130
and what is desirable,
what is normative.

03:38:25.130 --> 03:38:28.670
I mean, just what kind of self
do you want to be in the world?

03:38:28.670 --> 03:38:32.930
And it can also help
you cut through things

03:38:32.930 --> 03:38:36.260
that are inhibiting
your actualizing any

03:38:36.260 --> 03:38:39.913
of those possibilities in
ordinary waking consciousness.

03:38:39.913 --> 03:38:42.330
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I've certainly
found that to be the case.

03:38:42.330 --> 03:38:45.050
I mean, you raise a really
important point, which

03:38:45.050 --> 03:38:49.497
is that once these
learnings take place,

03:38:49.497 --> 03:38:51.080
these understandings
take place inside

03:38:51.080 --> 03:38:52.940
of psychedelic journeys--

03:38:52.940 --> 03:38:55.310
I do believe they translate
to neuroplasticity.

03:38:55.310 --> 03:38:57.710
I do want to highlight
the point for people.

03:38:57.710 --> 03:38:59.960
Oftentimes people
say, oh, you know,

03:38:59.960 --> 03:39:04.250
this mushroom or
this psychedelic

03:39:04.250 --> 03:39:07.010
it opens plasticity, but
of course, plasticity

03:39:07.010 --> 03:39:08.330
has to be directed someplace.

03:39:08.330 --> 03:39:11.780
Plasticity is just a process
like walking or anything

03:39:11.780 --> 03:39:13.680
else, underlying neural process.

03:39:13.680 --> 03:39:16.040
And I think it's
impossible for me

03:39:16.040 --> 03:39:18.440
to understand what
compartments of my life

03:39:18.440 --> 03:39:21.870
have been impacted by
these three MDMA sessions.

03:39:21.870 --> 03:39:24.967
But in some ways, I
wonder whether or not,

03:39:24.967 --> 03:39:27.050
not just the transition
away from animal research,

03:39:27.050 --> 03:39:31.280
but also a deeper
realization of the love

03:39:31.280 --> 03:39:34.040
for learning and
sharing information.

03:39:34.040 --> 03:39:36.110
I won't go so far as
to say this podcast

03:39:36.110 --> 03:39:39.140
is happening because of
that particular session,

03:39:39.140 --> 03:39:42.620
but these things they splay
out into multiple domains

03:39:42.620 --> 03:39:43.250
of the self.

03:39:43.250 --> 03:39:46.880
And I do think that
the key features that

03:39:46.880 --> 03:39:49.040
feel most important
to me to mention

03:39:49.040 --> 03:39:54.710
are that it really identified
true loves, things that I truly

03:39:54.710 --> 03:39:59.970
love, and made me less
cautious about feeling

03:39:59.970 --> 03:40:01.920
how intense those
loves really are.

03:40:01.920 --> 03:40:06.150
And then also lowered
the inhibition point

03:40:06.150 --> 03:40:09.235
of exploring, well,
what would that mean?

03:40:09.235 --> 03:40:10.860
And one of the reasons
I bring this up,

03:40:10.860 --> 03:40:13.470
and why I think it's so
important that you mentioned

03:40:13.470 --> 03:40:15.888
some issues around
politics and ethics

03:40:15.888 --> 03:40:17.430
and that many things
have splayed out

03:40:17.430 --> 03:40:20.460
from your exploration of
psychedelics, meditation,

03:40:20.460 --> 03:40:23.250
neuroscience, philosophy,
all the things that are you,

03:40:23.250 --> 03:40:27.360
and of course, that's only
a subset, is that so much

03:40:27.360 --> 03:40:31.200
of what I hear and see, so
much of what I hear and see

03:40:31.200 --> 03:40:37.490
in the self-help space,
contradicts itself and leads

03:40:37.490 --> 03:40:42.380
back to the origin
without a lot of progress.

03:40:42.380 --> 03:40:44.450
For instance, we
hear absence makes

03:40:44.450 --> 03:40:46.940
the heart grow fonder but then
out of sight, out of mind.

03:40:46.940 --> 03:40:48.920
You hear about
radical acceptance,

03:40:48.920 --> 03:40:52.303
but then what if it's radical
acceptance of non acceptance?

03:40:52.303 --> 03:40:53.720
I mean, there are
some experiences

03:40:53.720 --> 03:40:56.420
in people for which I radically
accept the fact that I

03:40:56.420 --> 03:40:58.880
want nothing to do with them.

03:40:58.880 --> 03:41:00.400
Am I supposed to transcend that?

03:41:00.400 --> 03:41:02.900
So these are the questions I
think that keep a lot of people

03:41:02.900 --> 03:41:07.400
from exploring things like
meditation because they

03:41:07.400 --> 03:41:10.610
feel like, well, is the idea
to just be OK with everything?

03:41:10.610 --> 03:41:12.680
Is radical acceptance
just like--

03:41:12.680 --> 03:41:16.610
we'll just bulldoze me
with things even if they--

03:41:16.610 --> 03:41:19.460
and my goal is to
somehow surpass

03:41:19.460 --> 03:41:20.785
the idea that they're harmful.

03:41:20.785 --> 03:41:22.160
And I don't think
that's actually

03:41:22.160 --> 03:41:24.260
the way any of this stuff
is supposed to work,

03:41:24.260 --> 03:41:28.220
although I don't claim to be
the authority on it either.

03:41:28.220 --> 03:41:30.950
I think notions of
radical acceptance,

03:41:30.950 --> 03:41:34.670
and radical honesty, and any
number of different sayings

03:41:34.670 --> 03:41:38.750
that one can find out there are
really the most salient beacons

03:41:38.750 --> 03:41:41.630
and guides that most people have
in order to try and navigate

03:41:41.630 --> 03:41:43.880
tough areas in their
life, including

03:41:43.880 --> 03:41:45.680
the relationship
to self, but others

03:41:45.680 --> 03:41:47.360
and political orientations.

03:41:47.360 --> 03:41:49.640
And so I feel like
almost all those things

03:41:49.640 --> 03:41:52.520
can be used to anchor down in
a stance that may or may not

03:41:52.520 --> 03:41:56.700
be informed or to
open up to ideas.

03:41:56.700 --> 03:42:00.170
And so I think that
none of this can really

03:42:00.170 --> 03:42:02.270
be solved in a single
practice it sounds like,

03:42:02.270 --> 03:42:06.140
but it does seem to me, based
on what you've told us today,

03:42:06.140 --> 03:42:09.620
is that only through a deep
understanding of the self

03:42:09.620 --> 03:42:13.520
as it really is, as
opposed to this illusion

03:42:13.520 --> 03:42:16.820
that you framed up,
could we actually

03:42:16.820 --> 03:42:18.410
arrive at some
answers about what's

03:42:18.410 --> 03:42:20.845
actually right for each
and every one of us.

03:42:20.845 --> 03:42:22.220
SAM HARRIS: Yeah,
I mean, there's

03:42:22.220 --> 03:42:25.880
one generic answer that
I think can be extracted

03:42:25.880 --> 03:42:30.710
both from psychedelics
and from meditation

03:42:30.710 --> 03:42:32.450
and from just
thinking more clearly

03:42:32.450 --> 03:42:34.670
about the nature of our lives.

03:42:34.670 --> 03:42:39.230
And it's to become
more process oriented,

03:42:39.230 --> 03:42:45.530
and to be more and more
sensitive to the mirage

03:42:45.530 --> 03:42:49.910
like character of
achieving our goals.

03:42:49.910 --> 03:42:51.830
Now, I'm not against
achieving goals.

03:42:51.830 --> 03:42:53.660
I have a lot of goals.

03:42:53.660 --> 03:42:54.257
I'm very busy.

03:42:54.257 --> 03:42:56.090
There are lots of things
I want to get done,

03:42:56.090 --> 03:43:01.130
and I'm satisfied as
anyone to finish a project.

03:43:01.130 --> 03:43:04.160
But if you look at
the time course of all

03:43:04.160 --> 03:43:06.200
of that fulfillment and--

03:43:08.840 --> 03:43:11.180
there are a few lessons
everyone I think has to draw.

03:43:11.180 --> 03:43:15.470
One is, most of your life
is spent in the process.

03:43:15.470 --> 03:43:20.750
The moment at which the goal is
fully conquered, that is just--

03:43:20.750 --> 03:43:24.440
I mean, that has
a tiny duration,

03:43:24.440 --> 03:43:27.005
and it has a very
short half life.

03:43:30.530 --> 03:43:33.890
The moment you arrive
at it, it begins

03:43:33.890 --> 03:43:35.970
to recede because,
in the meantime,

03:43:35.970 --> 03:43:37.430
you have all these
other goals that

03:43:37.430 --> 03:43:39.200
have appeared on the horizon.

03:43:39.200 --> 03:43:42.080
You've got people asking
what you're going to do next,

03:43:42.080 --> 03:43:46.940
and in some sense, if
you're focused on goals,

03:43:46.940 --> 03:43:49.190
you can never arrive.

03:43:49.190 --> 03:43:57.150
And I think what we're
all looking for in life,

03:43:57.150 --> 03:43:59.450
whether we're ever
thinking about taking

03:43:59.450 --> 03:44:03.830
psychedelics or practicing
something like meditation,

03:44:03.830 --> 03:44:07.220
we're looking for
good enough reasons

03:44:07.220 --> 03:44:13.610
to let our attention
fully rest in the present.

03:44:13.610 --> 03:44:16.970
That is the logic of success.

03:44:16.970 --> 03:44:19.490
The sense like, I've got all
these things I want to do--

03:44:19.490 --> 03:44:21.920
if I could just get rich
enough, or fit enough,

03:44:21.920 --> 03:44:25.450
or dial in my sleep well enough,
or improve my life in all

03:44:25.450 --> 03:44:27.200
of these ways, get the
right relationship,

03:44:27.200 --> 03:44:28.850
wouldn't it be
great to be married,

03:44:28.850 --> 03:44:33.390
I want to start a family,
I want all of these things.

03:44:33.390 --> 03:44:35.330
Why do I want these things?

03:44:35.330 --> 03:44:38.960
I want these things because
I'm telling myself--

03:44:38.960 --> 03:44:40.640
all of those things
are wonderful,

03:44:40.640 --> 03:44:43.430
I'm not I'm not discounting
those relative forms

03:44:43.430 --> 03:44:46.010
of happiness or
sources of happiness

03:44:46.010 --> 03:44:47.570
because it's all
completely valid,

03:44:47.570 --> 03:44:49.362
it's completely valid
to want those things.

03:44:49.362 --> 03:44:54.200
But one thing is
absolutely clear,

03:44:54.200 --> 03:44:57.200
it's possible to be miserable
in the presence of all

03:44:57.200 --> 03:44:58.400
of those things.

03:44:58.400 --> 03:45:01.790
And you can add great wealth,
and fame, and everything

03:45:01.790 --> 03:45:04.130
on top of that, and It's
possible to be absolutely

03:45:04.130 --> 03:45:09.267
miserable having everything
anyone could seemingly want.

03:45:09.267 --> 03:45:10.850
You just have to
open a newspaper just

03:45:10.850 --> 03:45:13.610
to see people living
out that predicament--

03:45:13.610 --> 03:45:17.600
spectacularly wealthy, famous,
healthy, successful people

03:45:17.600 --> 03:45:20.060
who could do anything they
want in life, apparently,

03:45:20.060 --> 03:45:22.160
and yet they're
doing this thing that

03:45:22.160 --> 03:45:24.770
is completely dysfunctional
and making them needlessly

03:45:24.770 --> 03:45:26.240
miserable.

03:45:26.240 --> 03:45:27.770
I won't name names,

03:45:27.770 --> 03:45:28.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: There's
enough of them out there.

03:45:28.610 --> 03:45:30.735
SAM HARRIS: Some people
come to mind at the moment.

03:45:33.640 --> 03:45:38.770
So there is a clear dissociation
between having everything

03:45:38.770 --> 03:45:40.750
and happiness that's possible.

03:45:40.750 --> 03:45:44.500
And it's also possible to
have very little, and almost

03:45:44.500 --> 03:45:46.750
nothing, and to be quite happy.

03:45:46.750 --> 03:45:49.060
I mean, you might not
have met these people,

03:45:49.060 --> 03:45:54.780
but I have met people who have
spent 10 years alone in a cave.

03:45:54.780 --> 03:45:57.390
And they come out of
that cave not floridly

03:45:57.390 --> 03:45:59.340
neurotic or psychotic,
they come out

03:45:59.340 --> 03:46:02.280
of that cave beaming
with compassion and joy.

03:46:02.280 --> 03:46:05.520
And I mean, they've been taking
MDMA for 10 years, essentially,

03:46:05.520 --> 03:46:07.380
and they come out
of the cave and now

03:46:07.380 --> 03:46:08.672
they're going to talk about it.

03:46:10.860 --> 03:46:12.940
And I'm not necessarily
recommending that project

03:46:12.940 --> 03:46:13.440
to anyone.

03:46:13.440 --> 03:46:15.960
I'm just saying, that is a
psychological possibility.

03:46:15.960 --> 03:46:17.730
So you have a double
dissociation here,

03:46:17.730 --> 03:46:19.772
whether you can have
everything and be miserable,

03:46:19.772 --> 03:46:24.100
or you can have nothing and
be beaming with happiness.

03:46:24.100 --> 03:46:27.690
So what is it that we actually
want in all of our seeking

03:46:27.690 --> 03:46:29.910
to arrange the
props in our lives

03:46:29.910 --> 03:46:33.960
and to have a
convincing enough story

03:46:33.960 --> 03:46:37.740
to tell about ourselves that
we're doing the right thing?

03:46:37.740 --> 03:46:40.500
What is all of that
effort predicated on?

03:46:40.500 --> 03:46:46.350
It's predicated on this
desire and this expectation

03:46:46.350 --> 03:46:50.880
that if we could get all of
this stuff in the right place

03:46:50.880 --> 03:46:53.790
and not have anything
terrifying to worry about,

03:46:53.790 --> 03:46:57.060
everyone we love is healthy for
the moment, and we're healthy,

03:46:57.060 --> 03:46:59.770
and we've got something to
look forward to on the weekend,

03:46:59.770 --> 03:47:03.625
and there's not a
plumbing leak in the house

03:47:03.625 --> 03:47:05.250
that we have to
immediately respond to,

03:47:05.250 --> 03:47:08.940
and we like our house, and
our career is going fine,

03:47:08.940 --> 03:47:11.290
and there's something
good to watch on Netflix,

03:47:11.290 --> 03:47:16.790
and we have all of it, now
can we just actually give

03:47:16.790 --> 03:47:17.830
up the war?

03:47:17.830 --> 03:47:22.760
Can we fully locate
our sense of wellbeing

03:47:22.760 --> 03:47:24.410
in the present moment?

03:47:24.410 --> 03:47:29.270
Can we relax the impulse
to brood about the past

03:47:29.270 --> 03:47:32.840
or think anxiously about
the future for long enough

03:47:32.840 --> 03:47:37.250
to discover that all
of this here is enough?

03:47:37.250 --> 03:47:40.790
Because our life our life is--

03:47:40.790 --> 03:47:43.760
we have this finite
resource of--

03:47:43.760 --> 03:47:46.860
I mean, we absolutely have
the finite resource of time,

03:47:46.860 --> 03:47:51.080
but within this the
finite continuum of time,

03:47:51.080 --> 03:47:53.360
we have the even more
precious resource

03:47:53.360 --> 03:48:04.760
of free attention that can find
our fulfillment in the present.

03:48:04.760 --> 03:48:07.550
Because even if we're even
if we're guarding our time

03:48:07.550 --> 03:48:10.020
to do the things that
are most important to us,

03:48:10.020 --> 03:48:15.640
we can spend all of that
time regretting the past,

03:48:15.640 --> 03:48:19.510
or anxiously
expecting the future,

03:48:19.510 --> 03:48:22.540
and just bouncing
between past and future

03:48:22.540 --> 03:48:24.730
in our thinking about
ourselves and our lives,

03:48:24.730 --> 03:48:28.000
and basically just
dancing over the present

03:48:28.000 --> 03:48:29.830
and never making
contact with it.

03:48:29.830 --> 03:48:35.780
So I think what we want
is a circumstance where

03:48:35.780 --> 03:48:38.900
a tension can be located in the
present in a way that's truly

03:48:38.900 --> 03:48:39.410
fulfilling.

03:48:39.410 --> 03:48:43.920
And unless you have had
some kind of radical insight

03:48:43.920 --> 03:48:47.710
that allows you to
do that on demand,

03:48:47.710 --> 03:48:53.260
you are in some sense hostage to
the circumstances of your life

03:48:53.260 --> 03:48:54.860
to do that for you.

03:48:54.860 --> 03:48:57.940
You're constantly
trying to engineer

03:48:57.940 --> 03:49:01.120
a state of the world
that will propagate back

03:49:01.120 --> 03:49:03.580
on a state of self
that will make

03:49:03.580 --> 03:49:05.410
the present moment good enough.

03:49:05.410 --> 03:49:09.250
And what meditation does, and
psychedelics to some degree

03:49:09.250 --> 03:49:12.910
does this, but meditation
very directly does this,

03:49:12.910 --> 03:49:18.370
it reverses the causality
and lets you actually

03:49:18.370 --> 03:49:21.190
change state such that
you can be fulfilled

03:49:21.190 --> 03:49:24.010
before anything happens.

03:49:24.010 --> 03:49:26.110
Your happiness is
no longer predicated

03:49:26.110 --> 03:49:28.100
on the next good
thing happening.

03:49:28.100 --> 03:49:31.000
You can be in the presence
of the next good or bad thing

03:49:31.000 --> 03:49:35.070
already being fulfilled
and already being at peace.

03:49:35.070 --> 03:49:38.753
I mean, there's a--

03:49:38.753 --> 03:49:40.170
I think there are
misleading nouns

03:49:40.170 --> 03:49:42.120
we can throw at
what is left there,

03:49:42.120 --> 03:49:50.340
but it is tranquility, peace,
freedom, lack of contraction,

03:49:50.340 --> 03:49:51.240
lack of conflict.

03:49:51.240 --> 03:49:54.880
I mean, all of that can be
more and more of a default.

03:49:54.880 --> 03:49:59.160
And all of that is also
compatible with deciding, yeah,

03:49:59.160 --> 03:50:00.660
why not get in shape?

03:50:00.660 --> 03:50:02.580
Why not engage this project?

03:50:02.580 --> 03:50:04.890
Why not change your career?

03:50:04.890 --> 03:50:08.760
I mean, it's not it's not that
you need to be somebody who--

03:50:08.760 --> 03:50:11.070
I mean, to your
point, you can notice

03:50:11.070 --> 03:50:13.320
all of these non optimal
things because no matter

03:50:13.320 --> 03:50:17.160
how much you meditate,
you're very likely going

03:50:17.160 --> 03:50:21.540
to spend a lot of your time
still lost in thought, still

03:50:21.540 --> 03:50:24.750
identified with it,
and still caring

03:50:24.750 --> 03:50:29.533
about the difference between
dysfunction and normativity

03:50:29.533 --> 03:50:30.075
in your life.

03:50:32.640 --> 03:50:38.860
The question is, what can you
what can you locate when--

03:50:38.860 --> 03:50:41.920
the question, it's
like, how much can you

03:50:41.920 --> 03:50:48.400
puncture that seeking happiness
project with the recognition

03:50:48.400 --> 03:50:51.940
that you're already free?

03:50:51.940 --> 03:50:53.980
That's what meditation
makes possible.

03:50:53.980 --> 03:50:57.370
You can keep just
1,000 times a day

03:50:57.370 --> 03:51:01.810
letting some daylight
into this search space.

03:51:01.810 --> 03:51:04.450
But it is still compatible.

03:51:04.450 --> 03:51:09.670
I mean, working out
is a great frame

03:51:09.670 --> 03:51:12.010
in which to look at
this because, I mean,

03:51:12.010 --> 03:51:14.950
in working out, when
you really work out--

03:51:14.950 --> 03:51:16.397
I'm thinking mostly of--

03:51:16.397 --> 03:51:17.980
I mean, it's really
anything, but it's

03:51:17.980 --> 03:51:21.730
resistance training, or cardio,
or something like jujitsu.

03:51:21.730 --> 03:51:24.130
You're intentionally
putting yourself

03:51:24.130 --> 03:51:28.930
in classically unpleasant
circumstances, physiologically.

03:51:28.930 --> 03:51:33.895
I mean, imagine what it's like
to do anything to failure.

03:51:37.250 --> 03:51:39.050
Well, if you just
check in on what

03:51:39.050 --> 03:51:41.450
that is like at the level
of sensation, I mean,

03:51:41.450 --> 03:51:44.930
that is basically-- it feels
like a medical emergency.

03:51:44.930 --> 03:51:46.450
If you were having
that experience

03:51:46.450 --> 03:51:48.200
for some other reason,
like if you woke up

03:51:48.200 --> 03:51:49.820
in the middle of
the night and felt

03:51:49.820 --> 03:51:52.280
what it feels like
to be deadlifting

03:51:52.280 --> 03:52:00.320
on your 10th rep on a set
where you would fail at 11,

03:52:00.320 --> 03:52:02.247
that's an emergency.

03:52:02.247 --> 03:52:04.580
But because you understand
what you're doing in the gym,

03:52:04.580 --> 03:52:06.800
and you've sought
out, and it's actually

03:52:06.800 --> 03:52:12.620
something you like doing, and
you can get a real dopamine hit

03:52:12.620 --> 03:52:19.760
from doing it, what
you're doing when you're

03:52:19.760 --> 03:52:22.880
doing that is you're owning a--

03:52:22.880 --> 03:52:31.620
you're actually transforming a
classically negative experience

03:52:31.620 --> 03:52:34.950
into something that's almost
intrinsically positive,

03:52:34.950 --> 03:52:37.110
certainly the net
on it is positive.

03:52:40.560 --> 03:52:43.080
Being able to do
that is more and more

03:52:43.080 --> 03:52:47.760
the experience of being
actually at peace,

03:52:47.760 --> 03:52:50.910
even while exerting
really intense effort

03:52:50.910 --> 03:52:52.180
in one direction.

03:52:52.180 --> 03:52:56.940
So you can be straining, and
I'm sure physiologically showing

03:52:56.940 --> 03:52:59.700
a lot of stress, I mean,
I'm sure the cortisol is up,

03:52:59.700 --> 03:53:05.280
and blood pressure's up,
heart rate is certainly up.

03:53:05.280 --> 03:53:09.447
So it's like, as far as the
body is concerned, it's stress

03:53:09.447 --> 03:53:10.530
as far as the eye can see.

03:53:10.530 --> 03:53:13.950
But you really can
be deeply equanimous

03:53:13.950 --> 03:53:16.795
and at peace, again, because
of the frame around it,

03:53:16.795 --> 03:53:18.420
because of the concepts
attached to it,

03:53:18.420 --> 03:53:19.878
because you know
what you're doing,

03:53:19.878 --> 03:53:23.310
you know why it's
happening, and you want it.

03:53:23.310 --> 03:53:25.620
So that's an attitude
you can bring

03:53:25.620 --> 03:53:29.780
into other stressful things
that take effort to accomplish.

03:53:29.780 --> 03:53:31.410
And so it's not
that you just need

03:53:31.410 --> 03:53:34.320
to be a pushover when you
learn how to meditate,

03:53:34.320 --> 03:53:37.647
or when you take MDMA, or
work on yourself in any

03:53:37.647 --> 03:53:38.230
of these ways.

03:53:38.230 --> 03:53:42.740
But what I think
you want to find

03:53:42.740 --> 03:53:45.650
is you want to find
your point of rest

03:53:45.650 --> 03:53:49.307
in the midst of any struggle.

03:53:49.307 --> 03:53:51.140
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I would
say that, certainly

03:53:51.140 --> 03:53:56.030
MDMA but, and again, I have
less experience with meditation,

03:53:56.030 --> 03:53:58.340
but they really I think
put us, ultimately,

03:53:58.340 --> 03:54:01.580
in positions of what can only
refer to as real strength.

03:54:01.580 --> 03:54:03.800
These can make
what before seemed

03:54:03.800 --> 03:54:06.230
like impossible
decisions, or even

03:54:06.230 --> 03:54:08.810
concepts or emotional
states to even think

03:54:08.810 --> 03:54:11.510
about for any period of
time without deliberately

03:54:11.510 --> 03:54:13.950
distracting or avoiding
in some other way

03:54:13.950 --> 03:54:18.530
and be able to lean into
those with open eyes.

03:54:18.530 --> 03:54:20.463
To me that's my
definition of strength.

03:54:20.463 --> 03:54:22.130
I don't know what
other people consider.

03:54:22.130 --> 03:54:27.140
But there's definitely something
real there in each case.

03:54:30.140 --> 03:54:34.250
This may seem like a divergence
but I and many other people

03:54:34.250 --> 03:54:37.410
are very curious about a
recent decision that you made,

03:54:37.410 --> 03:54:41.015
which was to close your
account on Twitter.

03:54:44.260 --> 03:54:47.810
You still have an Instagram
account I noticed.

03:54:47.810 --> 03:54:49.570
SAM HARRIS: I mean,
my team manages that.

03:54:49.570 --> 03:54:50.260
I've never--

03:54:50.260 --> 03:54:51.940
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's a lot
friendlier over at Instagram.

03:54:51.940 --> 03:54:52.750
I've been there a lot longer--

03:54:52.750 --> 03:54:54.050
SAM HARRIS: I've
never even seen it.

03:54:54.050 --> 03:54:55.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, it's
pretty good actually,

03:54:55.430 --> 03:54:57.055
considering-- imagine
what would happen

03:54:57.055 --> 03:54:58.240
if you did pay attention.

03:54:58.240 --> 03:55:00.590
They're doing a
good job with it.

03:55:00.590 --> 03:55:04.210
But your decision to close
your account on Twitter I think

03:55:04.210 --> 03:55:06.790
grabbed a lot of eyes
and ears, and there's

03:55:06.790 --> 03:55:08.170
a lot of questions about why.

03:55:08.170 --> 03:55:11.410
It was a very large account.

03:55:11.410 --> 03:55:14.320
It correlated with a number of
things that, for the outsider,

03:55:14.320 --> 03:55:20.290
people might be wondering
about new leadership, people

03:55:20.290 --> 03:55:23.560
who had been booted off, brought
back on, or at least invited

03:55:23.560 --> 03:55:26.050
back on, and so on.

03:55:26.050 --> 03:55:29.560
You are certainly not obligated
to explain your behavior

03:55:29.560 --> 03:55:33.580
to me or anybody
else for that matter.

03:55:33.580 --> 03:55:37.960
But I'm curious if you
might share with us

03:55:37.960 --> 03:55:41.500
what the motivation was for
taking the account down,

03:55:41.500 --> 03:55:44.320
and how you feel
in the absence of--

03:55:44.320 --> 03:55:47.530
I mean, your thumbs
presumably are freed up

03:55:47.530 --> 03:55:48.598
to do other things.

03:55:48.598 --> 03:55:50.890
SAM HARRIS: Yeah, I was
getting like an arthritic right

03:55:50.890 --> 03:55:52.720
thumb I think, and I think it--

03:55:52.720 --> 03:55:53.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: If
you don't mind sharing,

03:55:53.680 --> 03:55:55.270
I think there's a
lot of curiosity

03:55:55.270 --> 03:55:57.640
about you and your routines.

03:55:57.640 --> 03:56:01.930
You've been very generous
in sharing your knowledge,

03:56:01.930 --> 03:56:05.830
but also what makes
you tick, what

03:56:05.830 --> 03:56:08.420
motivates pretty big
decisions like that?

03:56:08.420 --> 03:56:10.360
It was a major platform for you.

03:56:10.360 --> 03:56:13.390
SAM HARRIS: Right, yeah, so
it was the only social media

03:56:13.390 --> 03:56:14.950
platform I've ever engaged.

03:56:14.950 --> 03:56:17.710
I mean, like you said,
I have an Instagram,

03:56:17.710 --> 03:56:20.860
I have a Facebook
account, but I've never

03:56:20.860 --> 03:56:22.180
used those as platforms.

03:56:22.180 --> 03:56:25.810
I was never on them, I've
never followed people.

03:56:25.810 --> 03:56:27.400
All the posting has
just come from--

03:56:27.400 --> 03:56:31.180
it's just marketing
from my team.

03:56:31.180 --> 03:56:33.980
But Twitter was me, I
mean, for better or worse,

03:56:33.980 --> 03:56:37.540
and I began to feel
more and more for worse.

03:56:37.540 --> 03:56:42.100
And it was interesting
because it was very--

03:56:42.100 --> 03:56:45.130
I've talked about it
a lot of my podcast,

03:56:45.130 --> 03:56:48.700
about my love/hate relationship
with Twitter over the years.

03:56:48.700 --> 03:56:51.580
Many good things came
to me from Twitter,

03:56:51.580 --> 03:56:54.070
and I was following a
lot of smart people.

03:56:54.070 --> 03:56:59.170
And it had become my newsfeed
and my first point of contact

03:56:59.170 --> 03:57:01.210
with information each day.

03:57:01.210 --> 03:57:03.850
And I was really attached to it
just for that reason, just as

03:57:03.850 --> 03:57:05.725
a consumer of content.

03:57:08.380 --> 03:57:11.350
And then it was also a
place where I genuinely

03:57:11.350 --> 03:57:14.680
wanted to communicate with
people and react to things.

03:57:14.680 --> 03:57:17.530
And I would see some article
that I thought was great,

03:57:17.530 --> 03:57:21.760
and I would signal boost
it to the people following

03:57:21.760 --> 03:57:22.400
me on Twitter.

03:57:22.400 --> 03:57:25.510
And that was rewarding,
and I could literally

03:57:25.510 --> 03:57:26.740
help people on Twitter.

03:57:26.740 --> 03:57:31.390
I mean, there are people who
have raised lots of money

03:57:31.390 --> 03:57:34.390
for on Twitter just by signal
boosting their GoFundMe's.

03:57:34.390 --> 03:57:38.020
And so I was engaged in a
way that seemed productive.

03:57:41.100 --> 03:57:47.000
But I was always worried that it
was producing needless conflict

03:57:47.000 --> 03:57:51.380
for me and was giving
me a signal in my life

03:57:51.380 --> 03:57:55.790
that I was being lured into
responding to and taking

03:57:55.790 --> 03:57:58.190
seriously that was
out of proportion

03:57:58.190 --> 03:58:02.630
to its representation of any
opinion or set of opinions

03:58:02.630 --> 03:58:05.090
that I should be
taking seriously.

03:58:05.090 --> 03:58:07.220
So I was noticing that--

03:58:07.220 --> 03:58:08.690
and again, this
evolved over years,

03:58:08.690 --> 03:58:17.860
I mean, this long predated
recent changes to Twitter,

03:58:17.860 --> 03:58:21.970
but I was noticing that many
of the worst things that

03:58:21.970 --> 03:58:24.190
had happened for
me professionally

03:58:24.190 --> 03:58:26.020
were first born on Twitter.

03:58:26.020 --> 03:58:29.140
I mean, just like some conflict
I got into with somebody,

03:58:29.140 --> 03:58:31.870
or something that I felt like
I needed to podcast about

03:58:31.870 --> 03:58:35.500
in response to on Twitter.

03:58:35.500 --> 03:58:38.680
Just so much of it, either
it's genesis was Twitter,

03:58:38.680 --> 03:58:46.180
or it's the further spin of it
that became truly unpleasant

03:58:46.180 --> 03:58:47.800
and dysfunctional
happened on Twitter.

03:58:47.800 --> 03:58:49.840
It was just-- Twitter was
part of the story when

03:58:49.840 --> 03:58:51.070
it got really bad.

03:58:51.070 --> 03:58:55.058
And I've had vacations
that have gone sideways

03:58:55.058 --> 03:58:57.100
just because I got on
Twitter and said something,

03:58:57.100 --> 03:58:58.930
and then I had
produced a controversy

03:58:58.930 --> 03:59:00.347
that I had to
respond to, and then

03:59:00.347 --> 03:59:02.530
I had to do a
podcast about that.

03:59:02.530 --> 03:59:05.050
And it was just, this is a mess.

03:59:05.050 --> 03:59:09.340
And so at that
point, I have friends

03:59:09.340 --> 03:59:11.920
who also had big
Twitter platforms who

03:59:11.920 --> 03:59:16.240
would say why are you responding
to anything on Twitter?

03:59:16.240 --> 03:59:19.960
Just tweet and ghost.

03:59:19.960 --> 03:59:20.710
Joe Rogan--

03:59:20.710 --> 03:59:20.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, post and ghost.

03:59:20.920 --> 03:59:23.950
SAM HARRIS: sat me down and
tried to give me a talking

03:59:23.950 --> 03:59:26.950
to, as did Bill Maher.

03:59:26.950 --> 03:59:28.850
And both of them engage
Twitter in that way.

03:59:28.850 --> 03:59:30.558
I mean, I think they
basically never look

03:59:30.558 --> 03:59:31.660
at their ad mentions.

03:59:31.660 --> 03:59:33.900
They never see whats
coming back at them.

03:59:33.900 --> 03:59:37.120
They use it effectively, the
way I use, or don't even, use

03:59:37.120 --> 03:59:38.180
Instagram or Facebook.

03:59:38.180 --> 03:59:41.710
I mean, I don't even see what's
going out there in my name.

03:59:41.710 --> 03:59:44.320
And so I could essentially
do that for myself

03:59:44.320 --> 03:59:45.610
on Twitter, presumably.

03:59:45.610 --> 03:59:48.910
And I did that for
some periods of time,

03:59:48.910 --> 03:59:51.940
but then I would
continually decide, OK,

03:59:51.940 --> 03:59:53.740
now it's all balanced
again, maybe I

03:59:53.740 --> 03:59:56.650
can just communicate here
because it was very tempting

03:59:56.650 --> 03:59:58.030
for me to communicate
with people

03:59:58.030 --> 04:00:01.320
because I would see
somebody clearly

04:00:01.320 --> 04:00:04.540
misunderstanding something
I had said on my podcast,

04:00:04.540 --> 04:00:09.640
and I think, why not clarify
this misunderstanding?

04:00:09.640 --> 04:00:14.520
And my efforts to do that
almost invariably produced a--

04:00:17.650 --> 04:00:22.390
sometimes it was a meandering
process of discovery.

04:00:22.390 --> 04:00:25.840
But often, it was just
a stark confrontation

04:00:25.840 --> 04:00:30.790
with what appeared to me to
be just lunacy and malevolence

04:00:30.790 --> 04:00:34.090
on a scale that I'd never
encounter elsewhere in my life.

04:00:34.090 --> 04:00:37.240
I'd never meet these
people in life,

04:00:37.240 --> 04:00:41.350
and yet I was meeting these
people by the tens of thousands

04:00:41.350 --> 04:00:41.980
on Twitter.

04:00:41.980 --> 04:00:46.800
And so the thing that began
to worry me about it--

04:00:46.800 --> 04:00:48.300
and again, I
understand that people

04:00:48.300 --> 04:00:50.967
have the opposite experience.

04:00:50.967 --> 04:00:52.800
I mean, depending on
what you're putting out

04:00:52.800 --> 04:00:55.410
and the kinds of
topics you're touching,

04:00:55.410 --> 04:00:57.060
you could have just
nothing but love

04:00:57.060 --> 04:00:59.400
coming back at you on Twitter.

04:00:59.400 --> 04:01:02.520
But because I am very--

04:01:02.520 --> 04:01:04.260
essentially, in the
center, politically,

04:01:04.260 --> 04:01:07.110
and because this is
now on my podcast,

04:01:07.110 --> 04:01:09.570
this is not in
the Waking Up app.

04:01:09.570 --> 04:01:12.570
I'm often criticizing
the far left

04:01:12.570 --> 04:01:14.880
and criticizing the far right.

04:01:14.880 --> 04:01:18.270
I'm basically pissing off
everyone some of the time.

04:01:18.270 --> 04:01:23.040
It's different, if you're
only criticizing the left,

04:01:23.040 --> 04:01:24.840
no doubt you get
hate from the left,

04:01:24.840 --> 04:01:28.500
but you have all the people on
the right who just reflexively

04:01:28.500 --> 04:01:33.690
and tribally are expressing
their solidarity for you,

04:01:33.690 --> 04:01:37.470
and who are dunking on
your enemies for you

04:01:37.470 --> 04:01:40.920
and when your enemies
come out of the woodwork.

04:01:40.920 --> 04:01:43.518
And if you're only
criticizing the right,

04:01:43.518 --> 04:01:45.060
you get a lot of
pain from the right,

04:01:45.060 --> 04:01:47.310
but you've got the people
on the left who are tribally

04:01:47.310 --> 04:01:49.440
identified with the
left, who are just going

04:01:49.440 --> 04:01:51.720
to reflexively defend you.

04:01:51.720 --> 04:01:55.560
If you're in the center
criticizing the left as hard

04:01:55.560 --> 04:01:59.400
as anyone on the right
ever criticizes the left,

04:01:59.400 --> 04:02:02.370
and you're also criticizing
the right as hard

04:02:02.370 --> 04:02:05.070
as anyone on the left
criticizes the right,

04:02:05.070 --> 04:02:07.560
you're getting hate from
both sides all the time,

04:02:07.560 --> 04:02:11.310
and no one is
reflexively and tribally

04:02:11.310 --> 04:02:14.037
defending you because you
pissed them off last time.

04:02:14.037 --> 04:02:15.870
You might be getting
hate from the left now,

04:02:15.870 --> 04:02:17.730
and the people on the
right agree with you

04:02:17.730 --> 04:02:19.950
but they can't forget the
thing you said about Trump

04:02:19.950 --> 04:02:23.310
on that podcast
two podcasts ago,

04:02:23.310 --> 04:02:25.000
so they're not
going to defend you.

04:02:25.000 --> 04:02:28.800
And so I basically
created hell for myself

04:02:28.800 --> 04:02:34.340
on Twitter because it
was just a theater of--

04:02:34.340 --> 04:02:37.350
it was just pure cacophony
most of the time.

04:02:37.350 --> 04:02:40.310
And what I was seeing was--

04:02:40.310 --> 04:02:43.820
I mean, there's no way
there's this many psychopaths

04:02:43.820 --> 04:02:46.070
in the world, but I was
seeing psychopaths everywhere.

04:02:46.070 --> 04:02:51.650
I was seeing the most
malicious dishonesty, and just

04:02:51.650 --> 04:02:56.025
goalpost moving, and hypocrisy.

04:02:56.025 --> 04:02:57.650
I mean, some of it's
trolling, and some

04:02:57.650 --> 04:02:59.990
of it's real confusion, and
some of it is psychopathy,

04:02:59.990 --> 04:03:06.130
but it's like, it was
so dark that I worried

04:03:06.130 --> 04:03:14.170
that it was actually giving me
a very negative and sticky view

04:03:14.170 --> 04:03:17.710
of humanity that was--

04:03:17.710 --> 04:03:22.240
I mean, one, I think it
is inaccurate, but two,

04:03:22.240 --> 04:03:25.540
it was something that I
was returning to so much

04:03:25.540 --> 04:03:29.562
because, again, I was checking
Twitter at least a dozen times

04:03:29.562 --> 04:03:32.020
a day, and I'm sure there were
some days where I checked it

04:03:32.020 --> 04:03:32.870
100 times a day.

04:03:32.870 --> 04:03:35.370
I mean, it was, again, it was
my main source of information.

04:03:35.370 --> 04:03:37.780
I was constantly reading
articles and then

04:03:37.780 --> 04:03:41.158
putting my own stuff out.

04:03:41.158 --> 04:03:42.700
That it became this
kind of fun house

04:03:42.700 --> 04:03:45.700
mirror in which I was looking
at the most grotesque side

04:03:45.700 --> 04:03:52.990
of humanity and
feeling implicated

04:03:52.990 --> 04:03:55.990
in ways that were
important because it

04:03:55.990 --> 04:04:00.620
was reputationally important,
or seem to be important.

04:04:00.620 --> 04:04:02.350
I know a lot of these people.

04:04:02.350 --> 04:04:04.150
These weren't just
faceless trolls,

04:04:04.150 --> 04:04:07.720
these are people with whom
I have had relationships

04:04:07.720 --> 04:04:10.210
and in some cases
friendships, who

04:04:10.210 --> 04:04:14.290
because of what, largely,
Trump and COVID did

04:04:14.290 --> 04:04:19.630
to our political landscape in
the last half a dozen years,

04:04:19.630 --> 04:04:22.870
we're beginning to
act in ways that

04:04:22.870 --> 04:04:27.280
seemed starkly dishonest
and crazy making to me.

04:04:27.280 --> 04:04:31.000
So I was just noticing that I
was forming a view of people

04:04:31.000 --> 04:04:33.550
who I actually have had
dinner with that was

04:04:33.550 --> 04:04:37.340
way more negative, based
on their Twitter behavior,

04:04:37.340 --> 04:04:40.330
then I think would ever be
justified by any way they would

04:04:40.330 --> 04:04:42.730
behave in life with me.

04:04:42.730 --> 04:04:46.360
I mean, I was never going to
have a face-to-face encounter

04:04:46.360 --> 04:04:48.490
with any of these
people that was

04:04:48.490 --> 04:04:52.060
this malicious, and
dishonest, and gaslighting,

04:04:52.060 --> 04:04:57.730
and weird as what was
happening hourly on Twitter.

04:04:57.730 --> 04:05:00.340
And so I just began
to become more

04:05:00.340 --> 04:05:05.800
sensitive to what this was--

04:05:05.800 --> 04:05:08.380
just the residue of
all of this in my life,

04:05:08.380 --> 04:05:13.263
and just how often the
worst thing about me

04:05:13.263 --> 04:05:15.180
in my relationship with
the people in my life,

04:05:15.180 --> 04:05:17.430
like just talking to
my wife or my kids,

04:05:17.430 --> 04:05:21.720
was just the fact that I had
been on Twitter at some point

04:05:21.720 --> 04:05:24.520
in the previous hour.

04:05:24.520 --> 04:05:28.125
And there was some
residue of that

04:05:28.125 --> 04:05:29.798
in my interaction with them.

04:05:29.798 --> 04:05:31.590
It's like, what are
you stressed out about?

04:05:31.590 --> 04:05:32.715
What are you annoyed about?

04:05:32.715 --> 04:05:34.440
What are you pissed off about?

04:05:34.440 --> 04:05:37.320
What can't you get
out of your head?

04:05:37.320 --> 04:05:39.060
What is the thing
that you now feel

04:05:39.060 --> 04:05:41.550
like you need to spend
the next week of your life

04:05:41.550 --> 04:05:45.870
focused on because it
went so sideways for you?

04:05:45.870 --> 04:05:48.630
All of that was Twitter.

04:05:48.630 --> 04:05:50.590
I mean, literally 100%
of that was Twitter.

04:05:50.590 --> 04:05:54.690
And so I just, at one point,
it was actually on Thanksgiving

04:05:54.690 --> 04:05:56.670
day, I just looked
at this and I just--

04:05:56.670 --> 04:05:59.140
I mean, there was very
little thought went into it.

04:05:59.140 --> 04:06:01.590
I mean, literally,
there was more

04:06:01.590 --> 04:06:06.133
thought involved in whether I
wanted coffee when you asked me

04:06:06.133 --> 04:06:07.050
when I showed up here.

04:06:07.050 --> 04:06:09.217
I mean it was just like,
at a certain point I just I

04:06:09.217 --> 04:06:13.223
just saw it, and I just
ripped the Band-Aid off.

04:06:13.223 --> 04:06:14.640
And to answer your
other question,

04:06:14.640 --> 04:06:17.940
it's been almost
wholly positive,

04:06:17.940 --> 04:06:21.970
as you might expect given the
litany of pain and discomfort

04:06:21.970 --> 04:06:22.860
I just ran through.

04:06:22.860 --> 04:06:29.470
But I mean, it's
surprising to recognize

04:06:29.470 --> 04:06:33.070
how much of a presence
it was in my life

04:06:33.070 --> 04:06:35.680
given the sense of
what is now missing.

04:06:35.680 --> 04:06:39.730
I mean, there's no
question there's

04:06:39.730 --> 04:06:41.020
an addictive component to it.

04:06:41.020 --> 04:06:41.770
And when you see--

04:06:41.770 --> 04:06:43.312
I mean, like when
I look at what Elon

04:06:43.312 --> 04:06:47.320
is doing on Twitter, forget
about his ownership of it--

04:06:47.320 --> 04:06:50.560
I've got a lot to
say about the choices

04:06:50.560 --> 04:06:55.000
he's making for the platform,
but just his personal use of it

04:06:55.000 --> 04:06:58.095
is just so obviously
an expression of--

04:06:58.095 --> 04:06:59.470
I mean, I don't
know if addiction

04:06:59.470 --> 04:07:04.240
is the clinically
appropriate term,

04:07:04.240 --> 04:07:11.520
but his dysfunctional attachment
to using the platform.

04:07:11.520 --> 04:07:15.610
Again, forget about changing
it and owning it, but just

04:07:15.610 --> 04:07:19.300
the degree to which it
is pointlessly disrupting

04:07:19.300 --> 04:07:22.060
the life of one of the
most productive people

04:07:22.060 --> 04:07:23.560
in any generation.

04:07:28.090 --> 04:07:30.850
That was also instructive
to me because I know Elon,

04:07:30.850 --> 04:07:38.650
and from a friend's eye
view of the situation,

04:07:38.650 --> 04:07:41.200
it's so obviously
not good for him

04:07:41.200 --> 04:07:45.130
that he's spending this
much time on Twitter.

04:07:45.130 --> 04:07:46.750
I just brought that back to me.

04:07:46.750 --> 04:07:48.340
It's like, well, if
it's not-- if this

04:07:48.340 --> 04:07:49.840
is what it's doing
to Elon, and he's

04:07:49.840 --> 04:07:51.298
got all these other
things he could

04:07:51.298 --> 04:07:54.910
be doing with his attention,
how much of my use of Twitter

04:07:54.910 --> 04:07:58.960
is actually a good idea and
optimize to my well-being

04:07:58.960 --> 04:08:02.360
and the well-being of
the people around me?

04:08:02.360 --> 04:08:05.530
So anyway, there was an
addictive component to it I

04:08:05.530 --> 04:08:09.610
think, and so when
that got stripped off,

04:08:09.610 --> 04:08:11.920
I do notice that there's--

04:08:11.920 --> 04:08:14.470
I mean, there are times
I pick up my phone

04:08:14.470 --> 04:08:16.630
and I realize this is
like the old me picking it

04:08:16.630 --> 04:08:19.540
up my phone for a reason that
no longer exists because there's

04:08:19.540 --> 04:08:20.890
not that much--

04:08:20.890 --> 04:08:22.390
I have a Slack
channel with my team,

04:08:22.390 --> 04:08:24.970
and I've got email,
obviously, but it's

04:08:24.970 --> 04:08:29.050
like, that is not much of
what I was doing with my phone

04:08:29.050 --> 04:08:30.070
really in the end.

04:08:30.070 --> 04:08:32.500
And so it's just my
phone is much less

04:08:32.500 --> 04:08:33.650
of a presence in my life.

04:08:33.650 --> 04:08:39.610
And so it's almost
wholly good, but yeah,

04:08:39.610 --> 04:08:43.080
I think there is
some danger in--

04:08:43.080 --> 04:08:47.070
or some possible
danger in losing touch

04:08:47.070 --> 04:08:49.170
with certain aspects of
culture, which, again, I'm

04:08:49.170 --> 04:08:49.920
not even sure--

04:08:49.920 --> 04:08:52.545
I mean, there's this question of
how much is Twitter real life,

04:08:52.545 --> 04:08:55.140
and how much is it
just a mass delusion?

04:08:55.140 --> 04:08:56.280
I don't know.

04:08:56.280 --> 04:08:58.590
But insofar as it
actually matters

04:08:58.590 --> 04:09:02.730
what happens on
Twitter, or insofar

04:09:02.730 --> 04:09:05.430
as I was actually getting
a news diet which I'm not

04:09:05.430 --> 04:09:07.590
going to be able to
recapitulate for myself,

04:09:07.590 --> 04:09:11.370
or I'm just not in fact going
to recapitulate for myself even

04:09:11.370 --> 04:09:14.640
if I could, if any
of that matters,

04:09:14.640 --> 04:09:16.860
I haven't discovered that yet.

04:09:16.860 --> 04:09:19.980
I mean, it was taking up an
immense amount of bandwidth

04:09:19.980 --> 04:09:21.060
and it's impressive.

04:09:21.060 --> 04:09:27.270
I think I said, it was like
I amputated a phantom limb.

04:09:27.270 --> 04:09:30.690
It was not a real limb, but it
was this continuous presence

04:09:30.690 --> 04:09:32.154
in my life that--

04:09:35.810 --> 04:09:38.060
it's weird, it actually
relates to the concept

04:09:38.060 --> 04:09:40.310
of self in surprising
ways because I

04:09:40.310 --> 04:09:45.620
felt there was a part of
myself that existed on Twitter.

04:09:45.620 --> 04:09:49.310
And I just performed a
suicide of that self.

04:09:49.310 --> 04:09:52.520
This is ending right
now, there's no residue,

04:09:52.520 --> 04:09:55.430
there's nothing to go
back and check, it's gone.

04:09:55.430 --> 04:09:58.400
And I didn't go back
and look at my--

04:09:58.400 --> 04:10:00.320
what's interesting to
consider is that I'd

04:10:00.320 --> 04:10:03.580
been on Twitter for 12 years.

04:10:03.580 --> 04:10:04.690
I don't keep a journal.

04:10:04.690 --> 04:10:06.643
I mean, Twitter, in
my timeline would

04:10:06.643 --> 04:10:07.810
have been a kind of journal.

04:10:07.810 --> 04:10:09.520
I could have gone back
to a specific hour

04:10:09.520 --> 04:10:11.840
and a specific day and looked at
what I was paying attention to.

04:10:11.840 --> 04:10:13.923
I mean, that could have
been an interesting record

04:10:13.923 --> 04:10:15.400
of just who I've
been for a decade,

04:10:15.400 --> 04:10:18.310
and probably a pretty
humbling record of who I've

04:10:18.310 --> 04:10:21.730
been for a decade in terms
of the kinds of things

04:10:21.730 --> 04:10:23.710
that captivated my attention.

04:10:26.525 --> 04:10:29.960
I didn't even think to
go nostalgically just

04:10:29.960 --> 04:10:31.640
look at any of that,
or see if any of it

04:10:31.640 --> 04:10:33.710
was worth saving, or
archiving, or thinking--

04:10:33.710 --> 04:10:35.750
I just-- delete.

04:10:39.830 --> 04:10:43.790
And so my actual sense of
who I am and my engagement

04:10:43.790 --> 04:10:50.500
with my audience, the world of
people who could potentially

04:10:50.500 --> 04:10:51.280
know me.

04:10:51.280 --> 04:10:54.460
What does it mean
to have a platform?

04:10:54.460 --> 04:10:56.800
Where do I exist, digitally?

04:10:56.800 --> 04:11:01.300
My sense of all of
that got truncated

04:11:01.300 --> 04:11:07.310
in a way that is
much less noisy.

04:11:07.310 --> 04:11:11.050
I mean, it's amazing
how much can't

04:11:11.050 --> 04:11:13.510
get fucked up now in my life.

04:11:13.510 --> 04:11:18.780
It's like with Twitter,
almost anything could happen.

04:11:18.780 --> 04:11:21.480
The next tweet was
always an opportunity

04:11:21.480 --> 04:11:23.880
to massively complicate my life.

04:11:23.880 --> 04:11:26.800
There is no analogous
space for me now.

04:11:26.800 --> 04:11:29.160
And so it's what I'm going
to say on your podcast, what

04:11:29.160 --> 04:11:30.827
I'm going to say on
my own podcast, what

04:11:30.827 --> 04:11:32.370
I'm going to write next.

04:11:32.370 --> 04:11:41.630
That's much more deliberative
and the opportunities

04:11:41.630 --> 04:11:45.560
to take my foot out of my mouth
or to reconsider whether any

04:11:45.560 --> 04:11:46.850
of this is worth it.

04:11:46.850 --> 04:11:48.950
Is this the Hill I really
want to die on now.

04:11:51.500 --> 04:11:54.560
It can be much more considered.

04:11:54.560 --> 04:11:57.390
I mean, I think all
of that's to the good.

04:11:57.390 --> 04:12:01.380
But even more important
than that is there's not--

04:12:01.380 --> 04:12:03.990
I'm not getting this
continuous signal

04:12:03.990 --> 04:12:06.490
that is always
inviting a response,

04:12:06.490 --> 04:12:11.920
whether on Twitter, or on my
own podcast, or anywhere else.

04:12:11.920 --> 04:12:15.300
And it's just much less noisy.

04:12:15.300 --> 04:12:18.960
I mean, life is much
less noisy and cluttered.

04:12:18.960 --> 04:12:23.250
And that definitely
feels better.

04:12:23.250 --> 04:12:24.893
That's 100% better.

04:12:24.893 --> 04:12:26.560
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm
happy to hear that.

04:12:26.560 --> 04:12:30.840
I know a number of people miss
you there, but you sound happy.

04:12:30.840 --> 04:12:32.430
I sense the genuine happiness.

04:12:32.430 --> 04:12:35.470
And several things
come to mind--

04:12:35.470 --> 04:12:38.970
first of all, thank you for
sharing your rationale there

04:12:38.970 --> 04:12:39.637
and how it went.

04:12:39.637 --> 04:12:41.262
I think for a lot of
people they think,

04:12:41.262 --> 04:12:43.530
oh, you must have walked
around in circles for hours

04:12:43.530 --> 04:12:45.600
talking about whether
it was delete,

04:12:45.600 --> 04:12:50.340
as many good decisions
are executed.

04:12:50.340 --> 04:12:52.830
I'm a big fan of Cal
Newport's work, deep work.

04:12:52.830 --> 04:12:54.450
In many ways, Cal's--

04:12:54.450 --> 04:12:56.580
I've never met him,
but we know each other

04:12:56.580 --> 04:12:58.230
through the internet space.

04:12:58.230 --> 04:13:01.920
He's really ahead of his time
with this notion of deep work

04:13:01.920 --> 04:13:03.820
and limiting distractions.

04:13:03.820 --> 04:13:05.850
I think he's even got
a book about a world

04:13:05.850 --> 04:13:08.013
without email or
something, really extreme.

04:13:08.013 --> 04:13:09.930
SAM HARRIS: So I mean,
he deserves some credit

04:13:09.930 --> 04:13:13.170
because he had been somewhat
a proximate cause to this.

04:13:13.170 --> 04:13:14.820
He had been on my
podcast, and he

04:13:14.820 --> 04:13:18.240
had encouraged me
to delete Twitter

04:13:18.240 --> 04:13:23.940
because I had been I had been
reaching some kind of crisis

04:13:23.940 --> 04:13:26.040
point with it prior
to that podcast

04:13:26.040 --> 04:13:27.210
and so we talked about it.

04:13:27.210 --> 04:13:30.750
And I had recorded that
podcast but hadn't released it.

04:13:30.750 --> 04:13:32.640
I actually recorded
the podcast the day

04:13:32.640 --> 04:13:34.882
before I wound up
deleting Twitter,

04:13:34.882 --> 04:13:36.090
but I hadn't yet released it.

04:13:36.090 --> 04:13:38.850
So in my podcast with
him, in the intro to it,

04:13:38.850 --> 04:13:42.030
I then give a post-mortem
on my deleting it.

04:13:42.030 --> 04:13:44.460
But he was one of the
last people who was

04:13:44.460 --> 04:13:46.540
in my head around these issues.

04:13:46.540 --> 04:13:48.510
And actually, it
was not by accident

04:13:48.510 --> 04:13:51.180
I had invited him on the
podcast because I increasingly

04:13:51.180 --> 04:13:54.863
wanted to think about whether
this was totally dysfunctional.

04:13:54.863 --> 04:13:57.030
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, I'm
a big fan of Cal Newport.

04:13:57.030 --> 04:13:58.990
And I am on social media.

04:13:58.990 --> 04:13:59.700
I'm on Twitter.

04:13:59.700 --> 04:14:03.840
I had some high-friction
interactions there,

04:14:03.840 --> 04:14:06.330
and I have a process
for dealing with those.

04:14:06.330 --> 04:14:09.930
I tend to avoid high-friction
confrontations online.

04:14:09.930 --> 04:14:13.120
But Instagram is a much
friendlier place by the way.

04:14:13.120 --> 04:14:15.490
If you want to come over
to where the nice kids--

04:14:15.490 --> 04:14:17.557
the cool kids
actually hang out--

04:14:17.557 --> 04:14:19.890
SAM HARRIS: Strangely, I'm
not looking for a substitute.

04:14:21.993 --> 04:14:24.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Don't let
me entice you over there.

04:14:24.160 --> 04:14:26.910
But I think that
this notion of really

04:14:26.910 --> 04:14:31.500
being able to access what
Cal calls deep work, what

04:14:31.500 --> 04:14:34.500
Rick Rubin talks about
being able to touch

04:14:34.500 --> 04:14:38.250
the source of great creativity
and focus on a regular basis,

04:14:38.250 --> 04:14:41.460
does require that one
have certain types of,

04:14:41.460 --> 04:14:43.260
and in some cases,
zero interaction

04:14:43.260 --> 04:14:45.000
with certain platforms.

04:14:45.000 --> 04:14:47.700
That merely being on a
platform and blocking

04:14:47.700 --> 04:14:49.110
people that just won't provide.

04:14:49.110 --> 04:14:50.625
I think a lot of
energy opens up.

04:14:50.625 --> 04:14:52.500
And I'm fascinated by
this concept of energy.

04:14:52.500 --> 04:14:54.270
I mean, we only
have so much energy,

04:14:54.270 --> 04:14:57.270
neural energy, to devote.

04:14:57.270 --> 04:14:59.640
And in many ways
what you described,

04:14:59.640 --> 04:15:01.740
there's really I think
striking parallels

04:15:01.740 --> 04:15:04.900
to what we've been talking about
all along these last hours,

04:15:04.900 --> 04:15:07.020
which is that sometimes
the thing that

04:15:07.020 --> 04:15:11.400
feels so powerful, that has
such a gravitational pull,

04:15:11.400 --> 04:15:13.720
and that we think this is
experience, this is life,

04:15:13.720 --> 04:15:16.290
this is just the way it is,
actually is an illusion.

04:15:16.290 --> 04:15:18.300
And when you step
away from it, you

04:15:18.300 --> 04:15:21.420
realize that there's this whole
other dimension of interactions

04:15:21.420 --> 04:15:26.940
that was available all along but
that we, for whatever reason,

04:15:26.940 --> 04:15:29.910
were intervening in by way
of our reflexive, distracted

04:15:29.910 --> 04:15:30.580
behavior.

04:15:30.580 --> 04:15:32.790
So I think there's
a poetry there.

04:15:32.790 --> 04:15:35.460
SAM HARRIS: I was a
hard case, but yeah, I

04:15:35.460 --> 04:15:39.273
got religion on this point,
and it's a good change.

04:15:39.273 --> 04:15:40.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, Sam, I want

04:15:40.690 --> 04:15:46.190
to say a couple of things--
first of all, every time

04:15:46.190 --> 04:15:50.810
you talk I learn
so much, and that's

04:15:50.810 --> 04:15:52.940
in the dimensions of
neuroscience, even

04:15:52.940 --> 04:15:58.400
hard core neural circuitry
type stuff, which is my home.

04:15:58.400 --> 04:16:04.930
When you talk about philosophy,
or meditation, or psychedelics,

04:16:04.930 --> 04:16:10.570
and even politics, a topic that
I'm woefully undereducated in,

04:16:10.570 --> 04:16:15.297
but you have this amazing
ability to blend and synergize

04:16:15.297 --> 04:16:15.880
across things.

04:16:15.880 --> 04:16:17.470
And I think, today,
what occurs to me

04:16:17.470 --> 04:16:19.840
is that not only
is that no accident

04:16:19.840 --> 04:16:21.880
because of your
training, and the rigor,

04:16:21.880 --> 04:16:23.740
and the depth at
which you've explored

04:16:23.740 --> 04:16:26.710
these different topics, but
also your openness to it.

04:16:26.710 --> 04:16:29.530
But I think, at least
for me, above all

04:16:29.530 --> 04:16:32.560
it's because I think you
are able to encapsulate

04:16:32.560 --> 04:16:35.680
this idea of the self
and the different ways

04:16:35.680 --> 04:16:38.230
in which we each and
all can potentially

04:16:38.230 --> 04:16:43.150
interact with the environment
and our inner landscape.

04:16:43.150 --> 04:16:45.430
Your description of
meditation, I have to say,

04:16:45.430 --> 04:16:49.460
now has forever changed the
way I think about meditation.

04:16:49.460 --> 04:16:52.080
I would no longer just think
of it as a perceptual exercise.

04:16:52.080 --> 04:16:53.830
On the podcast, I've
been talking about it

04:16:53.830 --> 04:16:56.860
as something to do for these
various benefits, the benefit

04:16:56.860 --> 04:17:00.790
set of more focus, lower stress,
of which certainly exists.

04:17:00.790 --> 04:17:05.620
But what you describe
today, has such an allure

04:17:05.620 --> 04:17:11.470
and holds such a promise
that, as I mentioned,

04:17:11.470 --> 04:17:13.240
I'm certainly going
to change my behavior.

04:17:13.240 --> 04:17:14.800
And I know I'm
speaking on behalf

04:17:14.800 --> 04:17:16.900
of many, many
people, when I just

04:17:16.900 --> 04:17:20.410
want to extend my thanks
for your coming here today

04:17:20.410 --> 04:17:23.530
to teach us even more
because, of course, you

04:17:23.530 --> 04:17:27.730
have your podcast and the
app, the Waking Up app.

04:17:27.730 --> 04:17:33.620
And the fact that, regardless
of the political landscapes,

04:17:33.620 --> 04:17:36.830
regardless of what neuroscience
feels about psychedelics,

04:17:36.830 --> 04:17:39.560
or where things are
at any point in time,

04:17:39.560 --> 04:17:43.640
you strike me as somebody who
is very committed to sharing

04:17:43.640 --> 04:17:46.910
knowledge and thoughtful
deep discourse

04:17:46.910 --> 04:17:48.200
so that people can benefit.

04:17:48.200 --> 04:17:50.860
And there are very
few people like you.

04:17:50.860 --> 04:17:52.820
In fact, there's
probably only just one.

04:17:52.820 --> 04:17:54.320
And so I feel very
grateful to be

04:17:54.320 --> 04:17:57.155
sitting across the table from
him for these last hours.

04:17:57.155 --> 04:17:58.280
SAM HARRIS: Oh, nice, nice.

04:17:58.280 --> 04:18:00.375
Well, I really
enjoyed this, and I

04:18:00.375 --> 04:18:02.000
want to congratulate
you on what you've

04:18:02.000 --> 04:18:07.160
built here because your podcast
is everywhere, and I'm a fan.

04:18:07.160 --> 04:18:09.920
And even more than
that, I'm continually

04:18:09.920 --> 04:18:14.060
seeing the evidence of you
reaching people and benefiting

04:18:14.060 --> 04:18:21.380
people, and this is one of
the best examples of new media

04:18:21.380 --> 04:18:25.190
just carving out a space
that people didn't really

04:18:25.190 --> 04:18:29.540
know existed because this is
not television, it's not radio,

04:18:29.540 --> 04:18:30.600
it's not--

04:18:30.600 --> 04:18:32.330
and all of a sudden,
people have time

04:18:32.330 --> 04:18:34.550
to hear a conversation
of great length

04:18:34.550 --> 04:18:40.400
that goes into nitty gritty
scientific detail on hormones.

04:18:40.400 --> 04:18:42.660
I mean, who would have thought
that was even possible?

04:18:42.660 --> 04:18:45.530
And so, yeah, I would
just-- congratulations,

04:18:45.530 --> 04:18:46.770
it's fantastic to see.

04:18:46.770 --> 04:18:49.400
And I'm just very happy
for the opportunity

04:18:49.400 --> 04:18:50.935
to talk to you and your people.

04:18:50.935 --> 04:18:52.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, thank you,

04:18:52.310 --> 04:18:53.540
it's very gratifying to hear.

04:18:53.540 --> 04:18:57.170
And I feel very blessed,
in no small part because

04:18:57.170 --> 04:18:58.610
of our conversation today.

04:18:58.610 --> 04:18:59.360
Thank you so much.

04:18:59.360 --> 04:19:00.080
